+@001
+# Representations and tokenization|foundation|technical
+Why can the same sentence require different token counts in two models?	Compare vocabulary, normalization, byte handling and a fixed multilingual sample; separate tokens from words.
+How do subword vocabularies handle rare names and spelling mistakes?	Trace segmentation of controlled examples and examine the tradeoff between vocabulary size and sequence length.
+What changes when text, images and audio share one model interface?	Separate modality encoders, representations, alignment and output tasks; avoid implying identical internal processing.
+Why does numerical reasoning remain difficult for a text predictor?	Compare number representation, training examples, exact computation and tool-assisted answers on reproducible arithmetic tasks.
+How does positional information let a transformer distinguish word order?	Explain an identified positional scheme with short examples; compare training context with longer inputs.
+# Training and learning|intermediate|technical
+What does next-token training teach beyond memorizing sentences?	Distinguish the prediction objective, learned statistical structure, generalization tests and examples that expose memorization.
+Why are training, validation and test data kept separate?	Trace a small experiment and show how leakage changes the apparent performance estimate.
+What happens during gradient descent in a language-model training step?	Follow loss, gradients, parameter updates and learning rate in a toy network rather than claiming access to proprietary training.
+How do supervised instruction tuning and preference optimization differ?	Compare datasets, objectives, human judgments and evaluation; do not equate preference with factual accuracy.
+Why can additional training data make an evaluation less trustworthy?	Examine benchmark overlap, near duplicates and the difference between legitimate learning and contaminated measurement.
+# Inference and generation|intermediate|technical
+How does a model choose the next token from its output scores?	Work through logits, probabilities and selection using a small hypothetical vocabulary with explicitly illustrative numbers.
+Why do temperature and top-p produce different kinds of variation?	Compare repeated generations from the same distribution while controlling prompt, model version and sampling settings.
+What does a key-value cache store during text generation?	Trace repeated attention work, cache growth and memory use; distinguish stored activations from permanent model knowledge.
+Why does generating the first token often take longer than later tokens?	Separate prompt processing, decoding, batching and network delay using a measured serving trace.
+Can a model give different answers when sampling is disabled?	Investigate hardware kernels, server settings, version changes and numerical nondeterminism before assigning one cause.
+# Context and external knowledge|intermediate|technical
+Why can information in a long context still be overlooked?	Test information placement, distractors, retrieval tasks and answer scoring; distinguish advertised length from effective use.
+When should an application retrieve documents instead of fine-tuning a model?	Compare update frequency, task adaptation, provenance, privacy and measured answer quality on the same use case.
+How do document chunk boundaries change retrieval results?	Compare headings, tables, overlap and chunk length against a fixed question set with source-grounded scoring.
+What makes a retrieved passage relevant but insufficient for an answer?	Separate topical similarity, entailment, missing qualifiers and conflicting sources using annotated examples.
+How should a system answer when its retrieved documents disagree?	Preserve dates, authority and exact claims; compare abstention and attributed disagreement instead of silently merging answers.
+# Hardware and serving|intermediate|technical
+Where does GPU memory go when a model is loaded and used?	Separate weights, activations, caches and runtime overhead; measure one version and workload rather than quoting a universal requirement.
+How does quantization trade memory savings against output quality?	Compare bit widths, calibration assumptions, tasks and hardware support with matched evaluation conditions.
+Why can a smaller local model respond faster than a larger remote model?	Measure compute, memory bandwidth, prompt length, network latency and batching instead of attributing speed to parameter count alone.
+When does batching improve throughput while worsening an individual request's latency?	Use a queueing example and distinguish tokens per second, time to first token and end-to-end completion time.
+How do speculative decoding and ordinary decoding differ operationally?	Explain draft verification and acceptance behavior; inspect a published implementation without assuming every workload benefits.
+# Reliability and evaluation|advanced|science
+How can an answer sound certain while lacking supporting evidence?	Compare fluency, confidence wording and source support on a labeled set; do not infer internal certainty from tone.
+What does a useful hallucination evaluation need to count?	Define unsupported claims, contradicted claims, omissions and abstentions; use source-level adjudication and report disagreements.
+Why can two benchmarks rank the same models differently?	Compare task composition, prompts, scoring, contamination and uncertainty before treating one aggregate as universal capability.
+How can a model's refusal rate hide both safety gains and usability failures?	Distinguish correctly refused harmful tasks, benign refusals and unsafe completions in an explicitly authorized evaluation.
+What does calibration mean when a model reports a probability?	Compare claimed likelihood with observed correctness across bins; separate token probabilities from answer-level confidence.
+# Human use and boundaries|advanced|civic
+How should an AI interface distinguish a suggestion from an executed action?	Trace preview, authorization, execution and confirmation in a reversible example; record failures without claiming success.
+What information should accompany an AI-generated citation?	Check source existence, passage location, relevance, quotation accuracy and access date on actual examples.
+How do AI applications leak sensitive information through ordinary logging?	Map prompts, tool results, traces and retention policies in a documented architecture without exposing real private data.
+Why is prompt injection a system-boundary problem rather than only a wording problem?	Use benign authorized demonstrations to distinguish trusted instructions, untrusted documents, tool permissions and human review.
+How can a team compare AI productivity gains without measuring only output volume?	Evaluate task correctness, review effort, rework, costs and participant differences against a defined baseline.
+# Economics and public claims|advanced|synthesis
+What belongs in the cost of an AI answer beyond token pricing?	Include retrieval, infrastructure, failed requests, review and maintenance using a clearly labeled workload model.
+How should energy comparisons separate training from serving?	State hardware, utilization, geography, measurement boundaries and workload; avoid assigning one universal footprint per query.
+When does an impressive AI demonstration fail to establish reliable autonomy?	Inspect hidden assistance, resets, task selection, failure disclosure and repeated trials rather than extrapolating from one edited clip.
+What evidence would justify calling an AI system open source?	Compare actual licenses and released components; distinguish access to weights, data, training code and modification rights.
+How can a model comparison remain useful after product versions change?	Publish exact versions, dates, prompts, settings and raw outcomes; define what must be rerun after an update.
+@002
+# Evidence before mystery|foundation|history
+What makes a historical event unresolved rather than merely poorly documented?	Separate missing evidence, contradictory records and sensational framing; build an explicit list of answerable and unanswerable questions.
+How can historians date an event when surviving accounts were written much later?	Compare witness dates, transmission, internal references and independent anchors without treating later narration as eyewitness evidence.
+When does an archive's silence become meaningful evidence?	Examine record-keeping expectations, survival, access and alternative explanations before drawing conclusions from absence.
+How can an old document be authentic while its story remains unreliable?	Separate material provenance, authorship, hearsay, rhetorical purpose and factual corroboration.
+What distinguishes a primary source from an independent source?	Trace copied accounts and shared witnesses so repeated publication does not become false corroboration.
+# Missing expeditions|intermediate|history
+What can the Franklin expedition's surviving records establish about its final movements?	Combine dated documents, archaeological finds and Inuit testimony; distinguish chronology from disputed causal reconstructions.
+How did Inuit knowledge influence the search for Franklin's ships?	Follow attributed accounts, search decisions and archaeological confirmation without erasing Indigenous contributors.
+Which parts of the Roanoke disappearance are documented and which are later speculation?	Separate contemporary reports, colony timelines, material finds and unsupported explanations.
+How should a documentary investigate the disappearance of Percy Fawcett?	Use expedition correspondence, contemporaneous reports and local perspectives; avoid presenting conjectural routes as recovered fact.
+Why do lost-expedition stories often minimize guides and support workers?	Compare credits, diaries and logistical records from one expedition and identify whose accounts survive.
+# Texts and undeciphered writing|intermediate|history
+What does the Voynich manuscript's material history reveal without deciphering its text?	Compare dating, ownership records, illustrations and script analysis while keeping translation claims separate.
+How can a claimed Voynich decipherment be tested fairly?	Require a reproducible method, predictive readings and treatment of the whole corpus rather than isolated suggestive words.
+Why has the Indus script resisted accepted decipherment?	Discuss corpus size, sign context, bilingual evidence and competing language assumptions without endorsing a proposed reading.
+What makes the Phaistos Disc difficult to interpret?	Separate artifact context, inscription length, sign classification and limitations on testing a decipherment.
+How do palimpsests recover lost texts while introducing new uncertainties?	Explain imaging, layered writing, transcription decisions and the difference between recovering marks and interpreting language.
+# Archaeological enigmas|intermediate|history
+What did the Antikythera mechanism's surviving fragments allow researchers to reconstruct?	Compare imaging, inscriptions and mechanical models; distinguish observed gears from reconstructed missing parts.
+How do researchers test explanations for the Nazca lines?	Compare landscape context, dating, pathways and competing ritual interpretations without invoking unsupported technology claims.
+What does evidence from Gobekli Tepe change about early monumental construction?	Examine excavated contexts and chronology; avoid turning one site's interpretation into a universal rewrite of prehistory.
+How can a buried city's identity remain disputed after excavation?	Compare inscriptions, geography, settlement phases and historical names in a bounded archaeological case.
+Why do stone-sphere and megalith stories attract false precision?	Inspect measurement, geological context, tool evidence and selective photographs before evaluating construction claims.
+# Disaster and abandonment narratives|intermediate|history
+What can evidence establish about the abandonment of a Maya city?	Use one site and time range; compare environmental, political and demographic explanations without a single-cause civilization collapse story.
+How should the Bronze Age collapse be divided into regional questions?	Compare dated destruction layers, migration evidence and surviving states rather than assuming one simultaneous event.
+What distinguishes the historical Mary Celeste record from its fictional retellings?	Trace logs, official inquiries and later additions; keep plausible scenarios separate from established causes.
+How can evidence from the Dyatlov Pass incident be assessed without turning speculation into accusation?	Separate contemporaneous records, environmental models, later testimony and the limits of reconstruction.
+Why do explanations of Tunguska depend on models as well as eyewitness accounts?	Compare observation timing, physical traces, atmospheric calculations and uncertainty in the event's parameters.
+# Forgery and sensational claims|advanced|history
+How was the Piltdown interpretation undermined by later testing?	Trace the evidence, institutional assumptions and scientific correction without treating one fraud as proof that all fossils are suspect.
+What can the Hitler diaries forgery teach about provenance checking?	Compare material tests, chain of possession, commercial incentives and the timing of editorial decisions.
+How do fabricated ancient maps acquire apparently scholarly credibility?	Trace publication histories, dating claims, copied citations and the distinction between an old surface and old content.
+When does a treasure legend rely on one unverifiable document?	Examine the Beale papers as a provenance problem; separate claimed cipher solutions from independently confirmed evidence.
+How can a historical quotation become widely attributed to the wrong person?	Follow earliest attestations, translation changes and citation copying instead of relying on popular quotation pages.
+# Methods that resolve or narrow mysteries|advanced|science
+How does radiocarbon calibration change a seemingly precise historical date?	Explain laboratory measurement, calibration ranges, contamination and contextual interpretation with a published example.
+What can ancient DNA establish about a named historical population?	Distinguish ancestry models, sample representativeness, contamination controls and unsupported claims about individual identity.
+How do shipwreck researchers identify a wreck without finding its name?	Compare location, construction, cargo, dates and archival records; report confidence and conflicting matches.
+What does geophysical mapping reveal before an archaeological excavation?	Compare radar or magnetometry anomalies with ground truth; avoid describing every anomaly as a wall or tomb.
+Can isotope evidence reconstruct a person's travels from a single tooth?	Explain the sampled tissue, geographic baselines, overlapping signatures and limits on route precision.
+# Responsible historical storytelling|advanced|media
+How should a film show two explanations when their evidence is unequal?	Compare evidential support explicitly rather than granting equal screen time as a substitute for analysis.
+How can a reconstruction disclose which details were invented for clarity?	Separate observed objects, sourced actions, plausible staging and unsupported dialogue in visual labels and production notes.
+What should change when new evidence overturns a historical mystery video?	Plan corrections, dated updates, source links and preservation of the earlier claim for accountability.
+How can a mystery documentary end honestly without resolving its central question?	Summarize established evidence, eliminate unsupported branches and specify what discovery would meaningfully change the conclusion.
+When should a local legend remain a story rather than an investigation into identifiable people?	Consider living communities, privacy, consent and the harms of converting folklore into accusations.
+@003
+# Sources and chronological foundations|foundation|history
+How do inscriptions, coins and literature answer different questions about ancient India?	Compare dating, audience, geographic reach and survival bias; avoid treating one source class as universally superior.
+Why do archaeological cultures and named kingdoms not map neatly onto each other?	Distinguish material classifications, political labels, linguistic evidence and retrospective historical categories.
+How does an ancient Indian chronology combine relative and absolute dates?	Trace stratigraphy, inscriptions, astronomical claims and calibrated measurements within one bounded period.
+What does decipherment of Brahmi allow historians to investigate?	Explain the history of the decipherment and a sourced inscription example; distinguish reading characters from resolving interpretation.
+How can a royal inscription exaggerate power while preserving useful evidence?	Analyze titles, patronage, genre and independent geographic or material corroboration.
+# Harappan settlement and environment|intermediate|history
+How did Dholavira organize water storage within its settlement plan?	Compare reservoirs, channels, excavated phases and environmental constraints using site documentation rather than a timeless ideal-city narrative.
+What do Mohenjo-daro's drains reveal about urban maintenance?	Examine construction, household connections, access points and uncertainty about waste flows and administrative responsibility.
+How do Harappan settlement sizes differ across regions?	Compare survey methods, site preservation and chronology before inferring a single uniform urban hierarchy.
+What can environmental evidence establish about changing Harappan river landscapes?	Distinguish sediment records, channel histories, dates and debates over identification with later textual rivers.
+How should the decline of a Harappan city be separated from the end of an entire civilization?	Compare local occupation sequences, regional continuity and multiple causal hypotheses.
+# Harappan craft and exchange|intermediate|history
+What can Harappan standardized weights reveal about exchange?	Compare distributions, contexts and measurement tolerances; distinguish coordination from proof of one centralized government.
+How were carnelian beads produced and circulated?	Use workshop evidence, material sourcing and experimental studies; separate observed production stages from inferred trade organization.
+What do seals establish about administration without a deciphered script?	Compare find contexts, motifs and sealings while avoiding unsupported readings of titles or institutions.
+How strong is the evidence for Harappan contact with Mesopotamia?	Compare texts, artifacts, dates and provenance rather than treating every stylistic resemblance as trade.
+What do shell-working sites reveal about coastal and inland connections?	Track raw materials, workshop waste and finished objects across identified sites and phases.
+# Vedic and early Iron Age worlds|intermediate|history
+How can Vedic texts be used as historical evidence without treating them as census records?	Examine genre, oral transmission, social vocabulary and differences between textual ideals and lived conditions.
+What can Painted Grey Ware distributions establish about settlement?	Separate pottery classification, chronology and site context from automatic identification with epic peoples.
+How did iron technology spread unevenly across the subcontinent?	Compare dated contexts, local production evidence and agricultural implications without one universal technological transition.
+What is debated about the relationship between archaeology and the early epics?	Distinguish textual layers, later place identifications, material horizons and evidential limits on proving narrative events.
+How did changing agricultural landscapes support early states?	Use regional settlement and environmental evidence; avoid reducing political change to a single crop or tool.
+# Mahajanapadas and Magadha|intermediate|history
+Why did early urban centers develop differently across the Ganga plain?	Compare sites, transport routes, craft evidence and chronology rather than assuming one model of second urbanization.
+What do Buddhist and Jain sources say about the mahajanapadas?	Compare textual lists, dating and geographic interpretation with material and inscriptional evidence where available.
+How can Magadha's expansion be investigated beyond lists of kings?	Examine geography, resources, institutions and differing source traditions without turning later explanations into contemporary records.
+What can punch-marked coins reveal about exchange and authority?	Compare symbols, weights, find contexts and chronology; separate monetary circulation from certain attribution to one ruler.
+How do gana-sangha descriptions complicate a simple monarchy-versus-democracy comparison?	Read institutional vocabulary in context and identify who is included or excluded in the surviving accounts.
+# Mauryan state and society|intermediate|history
+What do Ashoka's inscriptions reveal about communication across an empire?	Compare languages, scripts, locations and audiences; distinguish a proclamation's reach from proof of uniform enforcement.
+How does the language of dhamma vary across Ashokan edicts?	Compare selected inscriptions and translations in their political and ethical contexts without equating every usage.
+What can archaeology establish about Pataliputra's urban form?	Compare excavated structures, changing river courses and descriptions; state what remains reconstructed or inaccessible.
+How should the Arthashastra be related to Mauryan administration?	Address composition, textual layers and prescriptive genre before using it as a direct account of one reign.
+What evidence supports regional differences in Mauryan governance?	Combine inscriptions, archaeological distribution and later accounts; avoid a perfectly uniform bureaucratic map.
+# Post-Mauryan political networks|intermediate|history
+What do Indo-Greek coins reveal about royal representation?	Compare legends, portraiture, circulation and attribution; avoid inferring population language from coin language alone.
+How did Kushan rulers present authority across cultural settings?	Use coin types, inscriptions and named sites; distinguish royal messaging from a uniform belief system.
+What can Satavahana inscriptions reveal about donors and social networks?	Analyze named donors, titles, locations and chronology alongside uncertainties in dynastic dating.
+How should the political reach of the Shungas be reconstructed?	Compare literary claims, inscriptions, material evidence and disputed attributions rather than drawing a definitive border from one account.
+What do Western Kshatrapa records reveal about regional power?	Examine inscriptions, coins, infrastructure patronage and relationships with neighboring rulers.
+# South India and the Deccan|intermediate|history
+How can Sangam poetry inform history without becoming literal reportage?	Distinguish poetic conventions, compilation history, patronage and archaeological corroboration.
+What does archaeology at Arikamedu reveal about overseas exchange?	Compare imported materials, local production, occupation phases and changes in interpretations of the site.
+How were megalithic burial landscapes organized in different peninsular regions?	Compare burial forms, dates, assemblages and social interpretations without treating all megaliths as one people.
+What do early Tamil-Brahmi inscriptions reveal about literacy and patronage?	Compare contexts, reading disputes, dates and the limited kinds of social activity the inscriptions record.
