+@001
+# Classical machine learning and data problems|foundation|technical
+When does a decision tree suit a problem better than a language model?	Compare structured inputs, explainability and task-specific validation; do not generalize from one dataset.
+How does linear regression differ from predicting a category?	Trace targets, loss functions and assumptions using labeled illustrative data.
+What makes a dataset imbalanced rather than merely small?	Compare class frequencies, error costs and evaluation choices in a bounded classification task.
+How can missing values change a machine-learning result?	Inspect missingness patterns, preprocessing and leakage using a reproducible synthetic example.
+Why can a model fail after deployment even when its original test score was strong?	Distinguish distribution shift, label changes and implementation defects through documented measurements.
+What does clustering reveal without supplying verified real-world categories?	Compare distance choices, scaling and interpretation; avoid treating algorithmic groups as natural social identities.
+How does a recommendation system learn from feedback that its own rankings helped create?	Trace exposure, interaction and evaluation bias using original recommender research.
+# Computer vision|intermediate|technical
+How do image classification, detection and segmentation answer different questions?	Compare outputs and annotation requirements on rights-cleared images.
+Why can an image classifier learn a background instead of the intended object?	Investigate spurious correlations with controlled image changes and held-out environments.
+What makes optical character recognition harder for tables than for plain paragraphs?	Separate recognition, reading order, cell structure and evaluation against source documents.
+How does an image embedding support similarity search without understanding every visual relationship?	Compare retrieval tasks, training objectives and documented failure cases.
+Why can depth estimation from one image remain ambiguous?	Inspect geometric assumptions, learned priors and ground-truth comparisons.
+How do video models represent change across time rather than only a sequence of pictures?	Compare temporal sampling, motion information and bounded benchmark tasks.
+# Speech and audio intelligence|intermediate|technical
+How do speech recognition and speaker identification use different evidence?	Compare targets, enrollment and evaluation; exclude identification of unconsenting people.
+Why does separating overlapping voices remain difficult?	Inspect source overlap, recording conditions and task-specific separation metrics.
+How can an automatic transcript preserve code-switching between Hindi and English?	Evaluate aligned reference transcripts, language switches and named entities; report model version.
+What does a speech model's word-error rate hide about practical transcription quality?	Compare punctuation, speaker turns, names and meaning-changing errors.
+How can emotion recognition from speech confuse recording conditions with emotion?	Review primary validation studies, labels and population limits; avoid personal psychological inference.
+# Robotics and embodied systems|intermediate|technical
+How does a robot turn sensor readings into an estimate of its location?	Explain a documented localization pipeline and uncertainty without providing hazardous deployment instructions.
+What distinguishes a robot's planned path from its actual movement?	Compare sensing, dynamics, control and obstacle uncertainty in a safe simulation.
+Why is grasping an unfamiliar object difficult even when recognition is accurate?	Separate shape, contact, friction and action feedback using published robotics experiments.
+How can simulation help robot training while introducing a reality gap?	Compare modeled physics, variation and measured transfer performance.
+What does shared control mean when a person and a robot both influence an action?	Inspect role allocation, overrides and user-study evidence in a defined application.
+How do warehouse robots differ from general-purpose household robots in task requirements?	Compare environments, objects, human presence and failure recovery; avoid universal autonomy rankings.
+Why does a robot demonstration require evidence about resets and human assistance?	Record interventions, retries, conditions and actual success definitions.
+# Agents, tools and action systems|advanced|technical
+How does a tool-calling model obtain information that is absent from its context?	Trace schemas, arguments, execution and returned evidence in an original harmless task.
+What separates an agent's plan from a verified completed action?	Compare intended steps, tool outcomes and persistent state checks.
+How can a tool failure propagate into an agent's later decisions?	Use a sandboxed missing-file example; inspect retries, stale assumptions and stop conditions.
+Why does a long-running agent need checkpoints outside its conversational memory?	Compare recovery, progress records and duplicate-action prevention.
+How can permissions be narrowed to the task an AI agent actually needs?	Map read, write and external-action privileges using placeholder resources.
+What makes a human approval useful at an agent's decision boundary?	Compare reviewable evidence, scope and reversibility; avoid asking users to approve unspecified future actions.
+How can an agent recover after another process changes the same file?	Explain version guards, conflict detection and preservation using disposable local records.
+# AI systems beyond neural text generation|intermediate|technical
+What does a knowledge graph represent differently from a text embedding?	Compare entities, relations, provenance and query behavior on an original small dataset.
+How can a rule engine outperform a learned model on a tightly specified task?	Compare coverage, maintenance, uncertainty and explicit exceptions.
+What do constraint solvers contribute to planning problems?	Model an illustrative timetable with declared constraints; distinguish feasibility from optimality.
+How does reinforcement learning define feedback differently from supervised labels?	Trace state, action, reward and evaluation in a safe toy environment.
+Why can a reward function encourage behavior its designer did not intend?	Compare objective specification and observed behavior in published or simulated examples.
+How does a digital twin differ from a visually accurate 3D model?	Inspect data connections, update rules, validation and the intended decision.
+# Infrastructure and distributed computing|advanced|technical
+Why can network communication limit distributed model training?	Compare compute, synchronization, bandwidth and documented workload measurements.
+How do edge and cloud inference divide latency, privacy and maintenance responsibilities?	Map actual data flows and update mechanisms for a bounded application.
+What does an accelerator's advertised operation count omit about usable AI speed?	Compare precision, memory movement, software and realistic measured tasks.
+How do checkpoint formats affect moving a model between runtimes?	Inspect documented architectures, tensor formats, tokenizer dependencies and compatibility limits.
+Why can serving many model versions become an operational problem?	Trace routing, reproducibility, rollback and monitoring in an illustrative deployment.
+# Security, privacy and governance|advanced|digital
+How can training-data membership be studied without claiming access to a model's full dataset?	Review original privacy research and limits; exclude targeting private individuals.
+What does federated learning keep local and what can still be disclosed?	Trace parameters, updates, aggregation and documented threat assumptions.
+How does differential privacy define a protection differently from removing names?	Use primary definitions and clearly illustrative examples; do not certify a real dataset.
+Why can red-team findings fail to establish the overall safety of a system?	Inspect attack coverage, sampling, reproducibility and untested conditions.
+What does a model card disclose that marketing often leaves implicit?	Compare intended uses, training disclosures, evaluations and limitations in an actual versioned card.
+How can an organization trace responsibility when an AI component is supplied by another company?	Map contracts, controls, monitoring and applicable official requirements; avoid legal conclusions about a specific deployment.
+# Data labor, language and access|advanced|community
