+## 01. Evidence before mystery
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-002-013 | What makes a historical event unresolved rather than merely poorly documented? | Separate missing evidence, contradictory records and sensational framing; build an explicit list of answerable and unanswerable questions. |
+| BANK-002-014 | How can historians date an event when surviving accounts were written much later? | Compare witness dates, transmission, internal references and independent anchors without treating later narration as eyewitness evidence. |
+| BANK-002-015 | When does an archive's silence become meaningful evidence? | Examine record-keeping expectations, survival, access and alternative explanations before drawing conclusions from absence. |
+| BANK-002-016 | How can an old document be authentic while its story remains unreliable? | Separate material provenance, authorship, hearsay, rhetorical purpose and factual corroboration. |
+| BANK-002-017 | What distinguishes a primary source from an independent source? | Trace copied accounts and shared witnesses so repeated publication does not become false corroboration. |
