+## 07. Methods that resolve or narrow mysteries
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-002-043 | How does radiocarbon calibration change a seemingly precise historical date? | Explain laboratory measurement, calibration ranges, contamination and contextual interpretation with a published example. |
+| BANK-002-044 | What can ancient DNA establish about a named historical population? | Distinguish ancestry models, sample representativeness, contamination controls and unsupported claims about individual identity. |
+| BANK-002-045 | How do shipwreck researchers identify a wreck without finding its name? | Compare location, construction, cargo, dates and archival records; report confidence and conflicting matches. |
+| BANK-002-046 | What does geophysical mapping reveal before an archaeological excavation? | Compare radar or magnetometry anomalies with ground truth; avoid describing every anomaly as a wall or tomb. |
+| BANK-002-047 | Can isotope evidence reconstruct a person's travels from a single tooth? | Explain the sampled tissue, geographic baselines, overlapping signatures and limits on route precision. |
