+## 08. Responsible historical storytelling
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-002-048 | How should a film show two explanations when their evidence is unequal? | Compare evidential support explicitly rather than granting equal screen time as a substitute for analysis. |
+| BANK-002-049 | How can a reconstruction disclose which details were invented for clarity? | Separate observed objects, sourced actions, plausible staging and unsupported dialogue in visual labels and production notes. |
+| BANK-002-050 | What should change when new evidence overturns a historical mystery video? | Plan corrections, dated updates, source links and preservation of the earlier claim for accountability. |
+| BANK-002-051 | How can a mystery documentary end honestly without resolving its central question? | Summarize established evidence, eliminate unsupported branches and specify what discovery would meaningfully change the conclusion. |
+| BANK-002-052 | When should a local legend remain a story rather than an investigation into identifiable people? | Consider living communities, privacy, consent and the harms of converting folklore into accusations. |
