+# 005 Documentary Storytelling: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**23 categories · 109 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Documentary Storytelling. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Finding a documentary question
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-005-013 | How does a broad theme become a question a documentary can answer? | Bound place, people, time and available evidence; distinguish a topic statement from an investigable premise. |
+| BANK-005-014 | When is a compelling character insufficient to sustain a documentary? | Test access, change, conflict, consent and the connection between an individual's story and the film's larger question. |
+| BANK-005-015 | How should a treatment separate observed facts from proposed scenes? | Use labels for existing evidence, hoped-for access and illustrative reconstruction instead of promising events that have not occurred. |
+| BANK-005-016 | What makes a documentary's central claim falsifiable or revisable? | Identify the proposition, evidence threshold and plausible findings that would require changing the film. |
+| BANK-005-017 | How can early research reveal that a film's original framing is unfair? | Compare source perspectives and missing voices; show how the question changes instead of merely adding token balance. |
+
+## 02. Research architecture
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-005-018 | How should a production track a claim from source to narration? | Connect exact passages, interview statements, dates and scene references; distinguish source existence from claim support. |
+| BANK-005-019 | What belongs in a chronology when witnesses disagree about sequence? | Record competing dates, confidence and consequences for the story; avoid silently selecting the most dramatic account. |
+| BANK-005-020 | How can a document log prevent the same copied claim from appearing independently confirmed? | Trace provenance and citation chains rather than counting publications as separate witnesses. |
+| BANK-005-021 | When does a source's expertise stop matching the question being asked? | Compare specialization, firsthand knowledge, conflicts and the precise claim rather than relying on titles alone. |
+| BANK-005-022 | How should unanswered research questions affect production scheduling? | Separate critical factual blockers, optional enrichment and issues that can be presented as unresolved. |
+
+## 03. Interviews and relationships
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-005-023 | How can an interview question invite detail without feeding the desired answer? | Compare leading and open prompts against a bounded scene objective; preserve the respondent's actual wording. |
+| BANK-005-024 | What should happen when an interviewee corrects a previously recorded statement? | Retain the correction, investigate the discrepancy and update claims without hiding the change. |
+| BANK-005-025 | How do ongoing consent and initial release forms serve different purposes? | Trace access, vulnerability, changing distribution and opportunities to revisit participation without treating legal paperwork as the whole relationship. |
+| BANK-005-026 | How can a filmmaker include disagreement without arranging a manufactured confrontation? | Use independently gathered accounts and evidence; avoid staging conflict merely to create a dramatic scene. |
+| BANK-005-027 | What is lost when only the most fluent speakers represent a community? | Examine translation, confidence, institutional access and alternative forms of participation. |
+
+## 04. Observational scenes
+
