+# 008 Personal Finance: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**23 categories · 120 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Personal Finance. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Household cash flow
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Use current official terms, filings or macroeconomic data; state jurisdiction, date, fees, taxes, risk, units and assumptions. Use transparent illustrative calculations rather than personal recommendations or return promises.
+
+**Source types to locate:** regulator or official statistical publications; actual contracts, fee schedules and audited filings; original economic studies and disclosed calculation inputs. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-008-013 | How should a household budget handle income that arrives irregularly? | Separate essential timing, variable receipts, reserves and optimistic assumptions using an illustrative month-by-month example. |
+| BANK-008-014 | Why can a household show a surplus on paper while running short of cash? | Trace due dates, annual bills, account balances and restricted funds rather than comparing monthly totals alone. |
+| BANK-008-015 | How do fixed, variable and discretionary expenses overlap in real decisions? | Use actual categories and contractual constraints; avoid assuming every household can cut the same items. |
+| BANK-008-016 | What information does an expense tracker need to support a decision? | Compare categories, timing, recurring commitments and effort required; distinguish recording spending from changing outcomes. |
+| BANK-008-017 | How should a family discuss shared expenses when incomes differ? | Compare transparent contribution rules, autonomy, unpaid work and changing circumstances without prescribing one arrangement. |
+
+## 02. Reserves and debt
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use current official terms, filings or macroeconomic data; state jurisdiction, date, fees, taxes, risk, units and assumptions. Use transparent illustrative calculations rather than personal recommendations or return promises.
+
+**Source types to locate:** regulator or official statistical publications; actual contracts, fee schedules and audited filings; original economic studies and disclosed calculation inputs. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-008-018 | How does an emergency reserve differ from money saved for a predictable bill? | Separate uncertainty, time horizon and liquidity; use household scenarios rather than a universal required balance. |
+| BANK-008-019 | What changes when debt interest compounds more often than payments arrive? | Work through dates, balances, fees and compounding with clearly illustrative numbers. |
