+# 009 Psychology / Human Behavior: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**24 categories · 126 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Psychology / Human Behavior. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Cognitive limits
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-009-013 | How do working-memory limits change with the task rather than one fixed item count? | Compare stimuli, chunking, attention and measurement; avoid presenting a famous number as a universal capacity. |
+| BANK-009-014 | Why is recognizing information easier than recalling it in some settings? | Compare cues, familiarity, retrieval demands and study design rather than treating recognition as proof of deep learning. |
+| BANK-009-015 | How can attention miss a visible event without the eyes failing to see it? | Use a defined inattentional-blindness experiment and distinguish perception, awareness and later report. |
+| BANK-009-016 | What makes task switching costly even when the tasks are familiar? | Compare switching, preparation, interference and realistic workload conditions rather than assuming all interruptions are equally harmful. |
+| BANK-009-017 | How do expectations shape perception without making perception arbitrary? | Examine a controlled task, sensory evidence and competing explanations; distinguish influence from total determination. |
+
+## 02. Memory and belief
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-009-018 | How can a confident memory become inaccurate over time? | Compare retrieval, suggestion, source confusion and objective records without implying all testimony is useless. |
+| BANK-009-019 | What distinguishes a false memory experiment from ordinary forgetting? | Inspect procedure, outcome definition, participant awareness and the limits of applying laboratory results to real cases. |
+| BANK-009-020 | Why can repeating a claim increase its apparent truth? | Examine familiarity, prior knowledge, correction and boundary conditions rather than promising one exposure always persuades. |
+| BANK-009-021 | How do people update beliefs when evidence contradicts their expectations? | Compare task designs, incentives, prior commitments and actual revisions rather than labeling disagreement as bias automatically. |
+| BANK-009-022 | What can a misinformation correction change besides factual belief? | Distinguish recall, confidence, attitudes and behavior; inspect follow-up and effects in the studied population. |
+
+## 03. Judgment and decisions
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-009-023 | When does framing change a decision even though the numerical outcomes match? | Compare exact wording, reference points, populations and alternative explanations in a defined experiment. |
+| BANK-009-024 | How do base rates interact with vivid individual evidence? | Work through a transparent probability example and compare comprehension across presentations. |
+| BANK-009-025 | What does loss aversion mean in a specified task? | Separate reference points, outcome ranges, individual variation and disagreements about generalizability. |
+| BANK-009-026 | Why can an anchor influence estimates even when its source is weak? | Compare plausible versus arbitrary anchors, knowledge and task conditions rather than treating the effect as irresistible manipulation. |
+| BANK-009-027 | How can a decision be rational under constraints while differing from an idealized model? | Specify goals, information, costs and time limits before judging a choice against one benchmark. |
+
+## 04. Habits and motivation
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-009-028 | What distinguishes a habit from a frequently repeated deliberate action? | Compare cues, automaticity measures, outcome sensitivity and the limitations of self-report. |
+| BANK-009-029 | Why can a change of environment disrupt an established routine? | Examine context cues, competing demands and longitudinal evidence rather than promising a universal reset technique. |
+| BANK-009-030 | How do rewards affect motivation differently across tasks? | Compare reward type, task interest, timing and measured outcomes instead of applying one slogan about incentives. |
+| BANK-009-031 | What can implementation-intention studies establish about following through on goals? | Inspect populations, task specificity, adherence and effect variation; distinguish planning aids from guaranteed success. |
+| BANK-009-032 | How should a study separate motivation from the resources needed to act? | Measure access, time, money, support and opportunity rather than interpreting every failure as lack of effort. |
+
+## 05. Social influence
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-009-033 | Why do conformity experiments distinguish public agreement from private belief? | Compare response settings, group unanimity, task difficulty and evidence of later opinion change. |
+| BANK-009-034 | How does the presence of a dissenting person change group influence? | Inspect the experimental manipulation and consider information, social support and context. |
