+# 010 True Crime Documentary: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**23 categories · 113 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for True Crime Documentary. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Case selection and public records
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-010-013 | What is known at the investigation stage that is not yet a court finding? | Distinguish allegations, charges, evidence summaries, rulings and final outcomes using the actual procedural stage. |
+| BANK-010-014 | How should a true-crime chronology handle conflicting timestamps? | Record time zones, device clocks, source precision and uncertainty instead of silently forcing a dramatic sequence. |
+| BANK-010-015 | What makes a court document reliable for one question but limited for another? | Separate sworn claims, admitted evidence, procedural decisions and facts actually determined by the court. |
+| BANK-010-016 | How can a film describe an acquittal without implying either proven innocence or hidden guilt? | Use the applicable standard, judgment and procedural context; avoid speculation about an identifiable person's conduct. |
+| BANK-010-017 | When should a case not become a documentary despite strong audience interest? | Consider evidence quality, participant vulnerability, privacy, unresolved allegations and a defensible public-interest rationale. |
+
+## 02. Witnesses and memory
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-010-018 | How does an identification procedure affect the interpretation of an eyewitness match? | Compare lineup design, instructions, feedback, recording and current applicable research without deciding a case from general studies alone. |
+| BANK-010-019 | What is the difference between a witness changing details and a witness knowingly lying? | Examine memory, questioning, new information and corroboration; do not infer intent from inconsistency alone. |
+| BANK-010-020 | How can repeated interviews alter what a witness later remembers? | Inspect timing, suggestions, recorded wording and contamination risks rather than treating every later detail as independent evidence. |
+| BANK-010-021 | What should a documentary disclose about an anonymous source? | Explain the basis for credibility, corroboration and relevant limitations without revealing identifying details or promising certainty. |
+| BANK-010-022 | How should translated witness statements be checked? | Preserve the source language, translator qualifications, ambiguity and the distinction between quotation and interpretation. |
+
+## 03. Confessions and interviews
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-010-023 | Why does a confession still require corroboration? | Compare details independently known, information supplied during questioning and evidence established outside the interview. |
+| BANK-010-024 | How should interrogation footage be contextualized rather than used as a guilt-reading test? | Include missing time, instructions, circumstances and legal context; avoid body-language certainty claims. |
+| BANK-010-025 | What can research on false confessions establish about a particular recording? | Distinguish general risk factors from case-specific proof and identify the relevant expert and evidentiary standards. |
+| BANK-010-026 | How can an edited interview reverse the apparent meaning of an answer? | Trace the full question, qualifiers, sequence and surrounding statements rather than using isolated reaction shots. |
+| BANK-010-027 | What ethical boundaries apply when interviewing someone affected by a violent crime? | Consider informed consent, withdrawal, timing, support and distribution consequences without treating a release as the entire duty. |
+
+## 04. Forensic interpretation
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-010-028 | What does a DNA association establish before activity and transfer are considered? | Separate source-level likelihood, mixtures, contamination, timing and the circumstances of deposition. |
+| BANK-010-029 | How can chain-of-custody problems affect the weight of forensic evidence? | Trace handling and documentation while distinguishing a documented gap from proof of deliberate tampering. |
+| BANK-010-030 | Why do forensic error rates need to match the actual task? | Compare laboratory conditions, examiner decisions, sample difficulty and the difference between method and case-specific uncertainty. |
+| BANK-010-031 | What can toxicology establish about exposure without proving impairment at an earlier moment? | Inspect sampling time, substances, metabolism, concentration and expert interpretation in the relevant context. |
+| BANK-010-032 | How should a documentary present disputed forensic testimony? | Identify qualifications, validated methods, contrary evidence and the court's treatment without deciding expertise by presentation style. |
+
+## 05. Digital evidence
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-010-033 | What can a phone location record establish about a person rather than a device? | Distinguish tower data, GPS, account access, possession and precision; avoid mapping a point as certain presence. |
+| BANK-010-034 | How can deleted data be recoverable without every deletion being reversible? | Explain storage, backups, synchronization and acquisition limits at a non-operational level. |
+| BANK-010-035 | Why can two devices record different times for the same event? | Compare clock settings, synchronization, application metadata and export transformations before alleging manipulation. |
+| BANK-010-036 | What makes a social-media screenshot insufficient as complete evidence? | Check source account, date, edits, surrounding conversation and authentication through appropriate records. |
+| BANK-010-037 | How should investigators' digital reconstructions be distinguished from raw device records? | Separate acquired artifacts, processing decisions, inferred events and explanatory visualizations. |
+
+## 06. Statistics and causal claims
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-010-038 | Why is a rare event not automatically evidence of a deliberate crime? | Compare base rates, selection, alternative causes and case-specific corroboration without replacing legal judgment with one probability. |
+| BANK-010-039 | How can a forensic likelihood ratio be mistaken for the probability of guilt? | Define the competing propositions and missing prior or contextual evidence using an explicitly illustrative example. |
+| BANK-010-040 | What makes a geographic pattern meaningful rather than a product of selective cases? | Specify inclusion criteria, time range, population and comparison patterns before asserting a cluster. |
+| BANK-010-041 | How should a documentary compare crime rates when definitions or reporting practices change? | Align jurisdictions, denominators, reporting and recording standards rather than ranking raw totals. |
+| BANK-010-042 | What does a wrongful-conviction case establish about a system beyond one correction? | Distinguish documented mechanisms, selection bias and uncertainty about overall prevalence. |
+
+## 07. Victims, families and communities
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-010-043 | How can victim-centered storytelling preserve a person's life beyond the crime? | Use consented accounts, relevant context and agency rather than limiting the person to images of suffering. |
+| BANK-010-044 | When does naming an uninvolved relative create unjustified harm? | Assess public relevance, existing exposure, consent and alternatives that preserve the necessary facts. |
+| BANK-010-045 | How can a film include a community's distrust without presenting all members as holding one view? | Use multiple attributed perspectives and document the institutional history behind concerns. |
+| BANK-010-046 | What does trauma-informed editing change about images and sound? | Evaluate necessity, repetition, warning context and participant impact without treating sensational footage as required evidence. |
+| BANK-010-047 | How should a filmmaker respond when a participant disputes an edit's factual implication? | Review source material, evidentiary meaning and privacy concerns; distinguish correction from disagreement over interpretation. |
+
+## 08. Publication and correction
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-010-048 | What should change when a case is reopened after a video has been released? | Update procedural status, new evidence and unsupported earlier conclusions while retaining a clear correction history. |
+| BANK-010-049 | How can a creator cover an unresolved case without mobilizing viewers against a named suspect? | Keep claims attributed, discourage harassment and use official reporting channels rather than crowdsourced accusations. |
+| BANK-010-050 | What makes a title or thumbnail defamatory in implication even when the narration is cautious? | Review words, imagery and the overall allegation with qualified jurisdiction-specific advice where needed. |
+| BANK-010-051 | How should reenactments and synthetic media be labeled in a crime documentary? | Make clear what was generated or staged; do not create apparent surveillance footage, confessions or victim evidence. |
+| BANK-010-052 | What evidence is needed before claiming that a documentary helped solve a case? | Trace official statements, chronology and independent contributions rather than converting attention or coincidence into causal credit. |
+
+## 09. Historical case files and bounded documentary questions
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-010-053 | What do the Salem trial records reveal about testimony and institutional procedure? | Use traceable primary records and historical scholarship; avoid treating accusations as established supernatural events. |
+| BANK-010-054 | How can the Lindbergh kidnapping trial be studied through its actual evidentiary record? | Separate exhibits, testimony, verdict and later disputed claims. |
+| BANK-010-055 | What does the Dreyfus affair reveal about institutional evidence and public accusation? | Use original proceedings, official reviews and attributed historical analysis. |
+| BANK-010-056 | How did the Birmingham Six appeal record change the legal status of the convictions? | Trace official judgments and findings without extending them to unsupported allegations. |
+| BANK-010-057 | What can the Central Park Five case record establish about wrongful conviction and later exoneration? | Use official court and settlement records; distinguish documented findings from speculative motives. |
