+# 011 Horror / Mystery Stories: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 32 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Horror / Mystery Stories. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Designing original unease
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Create original fiction with explicit world rules, character goals and causal continuity. Label invented events and separate inspiration from claims about actual cultures or living people.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original story notes and world rules; permission-cleared reference material; reader feedback and a continuity audit. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-011-013 | How can a night-shift archive become frightening before anything supernatural appears? | Build ordinary routines, document inconsistencies and a protagonist's responsibility; establish what a changed record would cost. |
+| BANK-011-014 | How can a village of disagreeing clocks sustain a mystery beyond its opening image? | Give different time discrepancies observable rules and consequences; avoid resolving every contradiction through an unexplained twist. |
+| BANK-011-015 | What makes a call from a missing ferry passenger emotionally disturbing? | Establish relationships, verified details and conflicting choices rather than using the voice only as a jump scare. |
+| BANK-011-016 | How can an impossible house remain spatially understandable to the audience? | Define navigable rooms, repeated landmarks and the precise rule broken when its interior expands. |
+
+## 02. Character and vulnerability
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Create original fiction with explicit world rules, character goals and causal continuity. Label invented events and separate inspiration from claims about actual cultures or living people.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original story notes and world rules; permission-cleared reference material; reader feedback and a continuity audit. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-011-017 | How can a cautious protagonist make believable choices inside a horror story? | Give information limits, competing obligations and reasonable options; avoid requiring stupidity to advance the plot. |
+| BANK-011-018 | What changes when two characters interpret the same disturbing event differently? | Build distinct evidence and stakes for each interpretation rather than assigning one character the role of obvious fool. |
+| BANK-011-019 | How can grief shape a horror character without becoming a supernatural diagnosis? | Treat loss as individual experience and separate emotional interpretation from the invented rules of the threat. |
+| BANK-011-020 | How can a character's expertise both help and mislead them? | Let a real skill solve one problem while its assumptions fail under the fictional anomaly. |
+
+## 03. Supernatural rules
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Create original fiction with explicit world rules, character goals and causal continuity. Label invented events and separate inspiration from claims about actual cultures or living people.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original story notes and world rules; permission-cleared reference material; reader feedback and a continuity audit. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-011-021 | What makes a supernatural rule understandable without an exposition lecture? | Reveal it through repeated cause and consequence, then test a boundary that matters to a character. |
+| BANK-011-022 | How can a story change the audience's interpretation of a rule without cheating? | Plant observable evidence that supports both readings and preserve the established consequences. |
+| BANK-011-023 | When does explaining a monster reduce fear rather than deepen the story? | Compare the information needed for meaningful choices with unnecessary biological or historical completeness. |
+| BANK-011-024 | How can a threat remain consistent when it is rarely visible? | Track effects, capabilities, limitations and costs in a continuity record instead of adding powers at convenience. |
+
+## 04. Clues and misdirection
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-011-025 | How can a sound become a fair clue rather than merely atmosphere? | Repeat an identifiable pattern, vary its context and connect the eventual explanation to something viewers could notice. |
+| BANK-011-026 | What distinguishes a red herring from information the story later ignores? | Give misleading evidence a plausible cause and a payoff that remains relevant after the mystery is solved. |
+| BANK-011-027 | How can an unreliable memory be dramatized without invalidating every scene? | Establish specific limits and stable external evidence so the audience can still reason about events. |
+| BANK-011-028 | How should a horror mystery reveal that a trusted account was incomplete? | Expose the missing context through action or records rather than replacing the entire narrative with a late speech. |
+
+## 05. Visual and sonic restraint
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-011-029 | How can a static camera make a familiar space feel unsafe? | Use blocking, duration, offscreen space and a changed background detail while preserving spatial continuity. |
+| BANK-011-030 | When is silence more effective than a sudden sound? | Compare anticipation, sound perspective and the information withheld rather than treating loudness as the only intensity control. |
+| BANK-011-031 | How can lighting conceal a threat without making the scene unreadable? | Preserve action, geography and meaningful visual anchors; test the image at ordinary screen brightness. |
+| BANK-011-032 | What makes an offscreen event consequential enough to frighten viewers? | Show credible effects on characters, objects and choices instead of relying on an unexplained noise alone. |
+
+## 06. Folklore and setting
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Identify the text, edition, translator, commentary, community and ritual context as applicable. Attribute theological and spiritual claims, preserve regional variation and respect public versus restricted knowledge.
+
+**Source types to locate:** primary texts in traceable editions; authorized public teaching and ritual descriptions; consented community accounts and critical historical studies. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-011-033 | How can an original ghost story acknowledge the folklore that inspired it? | Name the source tradition, distinguish borrowed motifs from invented events and respect living community knowledge. |
+| BANK-011-034 | Why should a local sacred site not automatically become a sinister backdrop? | Consider community meaning, consent and fictional alternatives rather than using unfamiliar religious practice as evidence of danger. |
+| BANK-011-035 | How can two regional variants enrich a story without being falsely merged? | Choose the adapted version deliberately and disclose where the fictional narrative departs from its source. |
+| BANK-011-036 | What changes when a story is told by a resident rather than an outsider? | Compare access, assumptions, stakes and community agency instead of repeating an exotic-location template. |
+
+## 07. Structure and endings
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Create original fiction with explicit world rules, character goals and causal continuity. Label invented events and separate inspiration from claims about actual cultures or living people.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original story notes and world rules; permission-cleared reference material; reader feedback and a continuity audit. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-011-037 | How can a horror short resolve its character conflict while leaving the phenomenon unknown? | Close a consequential choice and its cost; leave only the supernatural explanation open. |
+| BANK-011-038 | When does an ambiguous ending feel earned rather than unfinished? | Provide evidence for bounded alternatives and resolve the questions the story explicitly promised to answer. |
+| BANK-011-039 | How can a revelation make an earlier ordinary scene frightening in retrospect? | Preserve the original scene's observable details and change their meaning through later evidence. |
+| BANK-011-040 | What makes a final return of the threat more than a sequel advertisement? | Connect it to an established rule, unresolved consequence or meaningful character action. |
+
+## 08. Audience and editorial checks
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-011-041 | How can a horror thumbnail signal fiction without pretending to show real evidence? | Avoid fabricated surveillance claims, accusations about real people and documentary labels for generated scenes. |
+| BANK-011-042 | How can a content warning be specific without spoiling the entire plot? | Identify relevant themes and intensity while separating accessibility information from promotional suspense. |
+| BANK-011-043 | What should be checked before using a child's perspective in horror? | Review age portrayal, consent in production, audience framing and exploitative treatment of vulnerability. |
+| BANK-011-044 | How can an editor test whether fear depends on an accidental continuity error? | Compare story rules, geography, props and chronology; repair confusion that the narrative does not intentionally establish. |
+
+## Coverage limits and next step
+
+This is an expanded topic-bank draft, not exhaustive scholarship or a researched episode package. Each question has an individually written boundary. Source access, claim verification, current angle-scope review and publication approval remain pending. Add new branches and cases when they fill a real gap; no numeric topic cap applies.
+
+Select an exact case, edition, population, jurisdiction or product version, inspect sources and refine the question before using the existing registry idea/depth planning workflow. These BANK IDs are catalog identities, not approved IDEA IDs. Existing Q1-Q42 defaults, production queues and approval gates are unchanged.
