+# 012 Space / Universe: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 32 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Space / Universe. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Cosmic distance and observation
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-012-013 | How can astronomers measure distance without traveling to an object? | Compare parallax, standard candles and other distance indicators with their scale ranges and calibration chains. |
+| BANK-012-014 | Why does looking farther away also reveal earlier cosmic times? | Explain light travel time within an expanding universe and distinguish different definitions of distance. |
+| BANK-012-015 | What separates an astronomical image's measured signal from its processed colors? | Trace filters, exposure combination and visualization choices without implying every processed image is fabricated. |
+| BANK-012-016 | How do astronomers know that two observations refer to the same changing object? | Examine coordinates, time, resolution and cross-matching uncertainty in a bounded observation set. |
+
+## 02. Stars and their lives
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-012-017 | Why do stars of different masses follow different evolutionary paths? | Compare energy production, pressure, fuel use and modeled lifetimes without one universal stellar lifecycle. |
+| BANK-012-018 | How can a spectrum reveal a star's composition and motion? | Distinguish line identification, temperature, Doppler shifts and model assumptions. |
+| BANK-012-019 | What evidence shows that a stellar nursery contains newly forming stars? | Compare wavelengths, surrounding gas, obscuration and classification uncertainty. |
+| BANK-012-020 | How can observations distinguish a white dwarf, neutron star and stellar black hole? | Use measurable properties and inference limits rather than their appearance in illustrations. |
+
+## 03. Galaxies and large structures
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-012-021 | How do galaxy rotation measurements motivate a mass problem? | Explain velocity data, visible matter estimates and alternative models without treating one plot as the whole dark matter case. |
+| BANK-012-022 | What can a galaxy merger reveal about gravity and star formation? | Compare observed structure, simulation assumptions and different stages inferred from separate systems. |
+| BANK-012-023 | Why do distant galaxies not all look like nearby galaxies seen through worse cameras? | Separate evolution, selection, wavelength shifts and observational limits. |
+| BANK-012-024 | How is the cosmic web mapped when most space contains no bright galaxy? | Compare galaxy surveys, selection effects, redshift measurements and model-based interpretation. |
+
+## 04. Black holes
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-012-025 | What does an event horizon mean in a physical model? | Distinguish the horizon, surrounding matter, observational signatures and the limits of intuitive surface analogies. |
+| BANK-012-026 | How can gas near a black hole become luminous while the hole itself emits no ordinary surface light? | Explain accretion, heating and radiation while keeping the black-hole interior outside observational claims. |
+| BANK-012-027 | What does a black-hole image actually reconstruct from telescope data? | Trace interferometry, sparse sampling, modeling and cross-checks rather than describing it as a normal photograph. |
+| BANK-012-028 | How do black-hole mergers test gravity under extreme conditions? | Compare waveform predictions, detector sensitivity and parameter uncertainty without claiming every alternative theory is ruled out. |
+
+## 05. Planets and habitability
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-012-029 | How do transit and radial-velocity methods detect different properties of exoplanets? | Compare light and motion measurements, selection biases and what needs combined observations. |
+| BANK-012-030 | Why is a planet in a habitable zone not necessarily habitable? | Consider atmosphere, water, stellar activity and geological uncertainty rather than equating orbital distance with life. |
+| BANK-012-031 | What can an exoplanet atmosphere's spectrum establish about its chemistry? | Inspect retrieval models, clouds, noise and competing molecular interpretations. |
+| BANK-012-032 | How could a proposed biosignature arise without life? | Compare biological and abiotic pathways within a defined planetary environment and identify discriminating observations. |
+
+## 06. Cosmic history
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-012-033 | What does the cosmic microwave background show directly? | Separate measured radiation patterns from cosmological parameters inferred through a model. |
+| BANK-012-034 | How do astronomers estimate the universe's age? | Compare expansion history, cosmological assumptions and independent constraints rather than using one simple distance division. |
+| BANK-012-035 | What observations support an early hot, dense universe? | Compare expansion, light-element abundances and background radiation while distinguishing evidence from unanswered origin questions. |
+| BANK-012-036 | Why are inflation and the Big Bang not interchangeable labels? | Clarify what each framework addresses, its evidence and the boundary between established observations and debated models. |
+
+## 07. Hidden components
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-012-037 | How can galaxy clusters test dark matter explanations differently from individual galaxies? | Compare lensing, gas, member motions and modeling uncertainties across the same system. |
+| BANK-012-038 | What does dark energy describe in current cosmological modeling? | Separate the inferred expansion behavior from claims about an identified material or understood mechanism. |
+| BANK-012-039 | How do expansion-rate measurements depend on different calibration routes? | Trace data, distance anchors, model assumptions and uncertainty before interpreting a discrepancy. |
+| BANK-012-040 | What would a convincing search for extraterrestrial technology need to rule out? | Inspect interference, instrumental effects, repeatability and natural explanations before claiming a detection. |
+
+## 08. Knowledge limits and media
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Define the rival claims and their evidence standards. Compare sources on equal terms, separate empirical findings from values or interpretations and do not imply that all positions have equal evidential support.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the original sources behind the compared claims; explicit definitions and measurement methods; independent critiques with traceable evidence. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
