+# 013 Ancient Technology / Lost Knowledge: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 32 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Ancient Technology / Lost Knowledge. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Evidence for ancient technology
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-013-013 | How can an ancient object establish a technique without preserving its instructions? | Compare tool marks, unfinished examples, workshop debris and experimental reconstruction; label inferred stages. |
+| BANK-013-014 | What distinguishes a lost manufacturing skill from an impossible-technology claim? | Specify the artifact, material properties, surviving evidence and tests of proposed production methods. |
+| BANK-013-015 | Why is making a modern replica not sufficient proof of the historical method? | Compare materials, tools, labor and alternatives; distinguish feasibility from demonstrated past use. |
+| BANK-013-016 | How should a technological tradition's continuity be investigated? | Trace dated witnesses, geographic movement and gaps rather than inferring an unbroken chain from similar forms. |
+
+## 02. Measurement and mechanics
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-013-017 | What does the Antikythera mechanism reveal about astronomical computation? | Compare observed inscriptions and gears with reconstructed functions and uncertainties in missing components. |
+| BANK-013-018 | How did ancient builders establish straight lines and levels over large distances? | Compare attested instruments, survey marks and experimental tests without assuming one method everywhere. |
+| BANK-013-019 | What can standardized weights reveal about technical coordination? | Examine tolerances, distributions, use contexts and the difference between shared practice and centralized control. |
+| BANK-013-020 | How should claims of extraordinary precision in ancient stonework be checked? | Define measurement procedures, sample selection, restoration effects and comparison with ordinary workmanship. |
+
+## 03. Materials and metallurgy
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-013-021 | What made some ancient iron objects resist corrosion in particular environments? | Compare composition, manufacture, exposure and conservation rather than calling the material universally rustproof. |
+| BANK-013-022 | How can crucible-steel production be reconstructed from material evidence? | Use slag, furnace remains, metallography and historical texts while separating local practices and later naming. |
+| BANK-013-023 | What does lost-wax casting evidence establish about complex metal sculpture? | Trace molds, cores, joins and finishing without using artistic complexity as proof of unexplained technology. |
+| BANK-013-024 | How do researchers distinguish ancient glass recipes from their weathered surfaces? | Compare sampling, chemical alteration and composition; avoid inferring original properties from appearance alone. |
+
+## 04. Water and buildings
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-013-025 | How did Indian stepwells combine water access, architecture and social space? | Choose a dated example and compare hydrology, construction phases, patronage and actual use. |
+| BANK-013-026 | What can Roman hydraulic concrete studies establish about long-term performance? | Distinguish original materials, marine exposure, chemical observations and claims about modern replication. |
+| BANK-013-027 | How were ancient aqueduct gradients maintained across changing terrain? | Compare surveyed routes, structures and maintenance evidence rather than treating uninterrupted flow as automatic. |
+| BANK-013-028 | What do drainage systems reveal about maintenance as well as construction? | Use access features, sediment and repair phases to investigate continuing labor rather than only original design. |
+
+## 05. Texts and computational knowledge
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-013-029 | How did Indian astronomical tables encode practical calculations? | Select a work and edition; distinguish the computational rule, commentary, manuscript date and later use. |
+| BANK-013-030 | What can a mathematical manuscript's diagrams establish about teaching practice? | Compare layout, annotations, copies and worked problems without treating every drawing as authorial. |
+| BANK-013-031 | How did numerical notation change the cost of calculation? | Use historically grounded examples and separate notation, algorithms and institutions that taught them. |
+| BANK-013-032 | How should translations of technical terms be checked against actual operations? | Compare terminology with described procedures and artifacts rather than relying on a modern word's superficial resemblance. |
+
+## 06. Navigation and transmission
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-013-033 | How did Indian Ocean navigation combine seasonal knowledge and local pilots? | Compare texts, port evidence and attributed practical knowledge without proposing one universal ancient navigation system. |
+| BANK-013-034 | What evidence distinguishes invention from transmission of papermaking? | Track dated material, production evidence and accounts rather than granting priority from one later legend. |
+| BANK-013-035 | How did translation movements alter technical knowledge rather than simply preserve it? | Compare additions, recalculation and commentary in identified works across languages. |
+| BANK-013-036 | What can shipwreck cargo reveal about movement of techniques? | Distinguish circulation of objects, travel of specialists and actual transfer of manufacturing knowledge. |
+
+## 07. Testing popular claims
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Define the rival claims and their evidence standards. Compare sources on equal terms, separate empirical findings from values or interpretations and do not imply that all positions have equal evidential support.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the original sources behind the compared claims; explicit definitions and measurement methods; independent critiques with traceable evidence. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-013-037 | What evidence would an ancient battery interpretation need beyond the object's shape? | Compare context, residues, plausible connections and alternative uses without treating a modern demonstration as proof. |
+| BANK-013-038 | How should pyramid-building hypotheses be compared fairly? | Define transport, lifting, labor and chronology constraints using archaeological evidence rather than one spectacular replica. |
+| BANK-013-039 | Why are ancient electricity claims often based on ambiguous images? | Inspect the complete scene, inscriptions, ritual context and alternative iconographic readings. |
+| BANK-013-040 | How can a false lost-technology claim survive by changing its required evidence? | Trace a specific claim's revisions and distinguish genuine uncertainty from an explanation made immune to testing. |
+
+## 08. Responsible reconstruction
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-013-041 | What must be disclosed when a film visualizes an ancient workshop? | Label excavated features, tested analogies and invented staging; avoid attributing unattested machinery to the site. |
+| BANK-013-042 | How can experimental archaeology show failed attempts usefully? | Record material, tools, labor and failure so the experiment constrains explanations rather than merely produces an impressive artifact. |
+| BANK-013-043 | Why should craft communities be consulted in technology histories? | Compare living skill, historical evidence and changing practice without assuming modern artisans exactly reproduce antiquity. |
+| BANK-013-044 | How can a channel explain ancient achievement without denying uncertainty or crediting aliens? | Center demonstrable skill, organization and evidence while preserving unresolved technical questions. |
+
+## Coverage limits and next step
+
+This is an expanded topic-bank draft, not exhaustive scholarship or a researched episode package. Each question has an individually written boundary. Source access, claim verification, current angle-scope review and publication approval remain pending. Add new branches and cases when they fill a real gap; no numeric topic cap applies.
+
+Select an exact case, edition, population, jurisdiction or product version, inspect sources and refine the question before using the existing registry idea/depth planning workflow. These BANK IDs are catalog identities, not approved IDEA IDs. Existing Q1-Q42 defaults, production queues and approval gates are unchanged.
