+# 014 Geopolitics / World History: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 32 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Geopolitics / World History. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Historical frameworks
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-014-013 | How did the Congress of Vienna organize a balance of power in practice? | Compare settlement terms, major actors and later changes rather than treating the arrangement as permanent peace. |
+| BANK-014-014 | What do colonial boundary records reveal about competing priorities? | Use one border's negotiations, maps and local accounts instead of assuming every line was arbitrary in the same way. |
+| BANK-014-015 | How did the breakup of a named empire produce different forms of successor authority? | Bound time and region; compare institutions, external recognition and unresolved claims. |
+| BANK-014-016 | Why should a present conflict not be explained by a single ancient event? | Trace intervening institutions, decisions and changing interests rather than presenting history as unavoidable destiny. |
+
+## 02. Borders and territory
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-014-017 | How can a map distinguish sovereignty claims from effective control? | Use dated sources, neutral legends and separate layers rather than allowing one boundary style to settle a dispute. |
+| BANK-014-018 | What changes when a border follows a river that moves? | Compare treaty language, demarcation practice and specific jurisdictional responses without a universal legal conclusion. |
+| BANK-014-019 | How do enclaves and exclaves complicate ordinary public services? | Examine transit, emergency access, policing and agreements in one documented case. |
+| BANK-014-020 | What can diplomatic recognition change without determining every aspect of statehood? | Separate legal arguments, practical relations, institutions and international organization membership. |
+
+## 03. Chokepoints and infrastructure
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-014-021 | Why does a maritime chokepoint's importance depend on the cargo and route? | Compare volumes, alternatives, vessel constraints and cost rather than assigning one timeless strategic ranking. |
+| BANK-014-022 | How does closing a canal redistribute shipping pressure? | Trace rerouting, travel time, insurance, port capacity and different effects across commodities. |
+| BANK-014-023 | When does a cross-border railway create influence rather than merely transport capacity? | Examine financing, operating control, demand and contractual relationships with evidence from a named project. |
+| BANK-014-024 | How can a port's commercial role differ from its alleged military significance? | Separate observed facilities, agreements, access and speculation about future use. |
+
+## 04. Trade and sanctions
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use current official terms, filings or macroeconomic data; state jurisdiction, date, fees, taxes, risk, units and assumptions. Use transparent illustrative calculations rather than personal recommendations or return promises.
+
+**Source types to locate:** regulator or official statistical publications; actual contracts, fee schedules and audited filings; original economic studies and disclosed calculation inputs. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-014-025 | How do sanctions affect different participants through different channels? | Compare exact measures, enforcement, substitution and distribution rather than reporting a single national outcome. |
+| BANK-014-026 | What evidence can show trade diversion after a restriction? | Align commodity definitions, partners, dates and reporting gaps; distinguish correlation from a proven evasion route. |
+| BANK-014-027 | How do export controls differ from tariffs in their intended mechanism? | Compare legal scope, licensing, technology access and actual enforcement in one jurisdiction. |
+| BANK-014-028 | Why can economic interdependence coexist with strategic rivalry? | Examine sector-specific dependencies, switching costs and political incentives without assuming trade guarantees peace. |
+
+## 05. Resources and shared environments
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-014-029 | What makes an upstream water project a cross-border political issue? | Compare seasonal flows, treaty provisions, data sharing and downstream exposure rather than asserting inevitable conflict. |
+| BANK-014-030 | How does energy dependence change when fuels, suppliers and infrastructure differ? | Separate import shares, storage, substitutes and transport constraints using a defined period. |
+| BANK-014-031 | What can critical-mineral concentration establish about geopolitical leverage? | Distinguish mining, refining, ownership and substitution rather than treating deposits as immediate usable power. |
+| BANK-014-032 | How should climate migration scenarios be separated from present migration statistics? | State definitions, drivers, assumptions and uncertainty; avoid presenting a projection as a counted population. |
+
+## 06. Alliances and institutions
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-014-033 | How do alliance commitments differ from actual crisis decisions? | Compare treaty text, capabilities, consultation and historical behavior without assuming automatic action. |
+| BANK-014-034 | What does a military base agreement reveal about host-state agency? | Examine terms, renegotiation, domestic debate and practical access rather than depicting the host as passive. |
+| BANK-014-035 | How do regional organizations manage disagreement among their members? | Use a specific procedure and case; separate formal rules from actual political bargaining. |
+| BANK-014-036 | Why does a United Nations vote not necessarily describe a country's entire foreign policy? | Compare resolution text, explanations, abstentions and behavior outside the vote. |
+
+## 07. Evidence and forecasts
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Define the rival claims and their evidence standards. Compare sources on equal terms, separate empirical findings from values or interpretations and do not imply that all positions have equal evidential support.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the original sources behind the compared claims; explicit definitions and measurement methods; independent critiques with traceable evidence. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-014-037 | How should a geopolitical documentary compare official statements with independent records? | Separate public messaging, observable action, reliable reporting and unverified anonymous claims. |
+| BANK-014-038 | What makes a geopolitical forecast testable? | Specify outcome, date range, probability, assumptions and revision criteria rather than using vague inevitability language. |
+| BANK-014-039 | How can demographic figures mislead when used as direct measures of national power? | Compare age structure, participation, skills, institutions and measurement dates instead of raw population alone. |
+| BANK-014-040 | How should a film represent uncertainty about a conflict's casualties? | Identify definitions, source access, estimation methods and ranges without choosing numbers for dramatic effect. |
+
+## 08. Ethical public explanation
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-014-041 | How can a channel explain government actions without stereotyping a population? | Distinguish institutions, leaders, social groups and dissent; attribute positions rather than assigning collective intent. |
+| BANK-014-042 | When does a strategic map omit the people whose lives it describes? | Include settlement, livelihoods, displacement and local agency alongside routes and military symbols. |
+| BANK-014-043 | What should change when a current-event video becomes outdated? | Date the analysis, identify revised facts and preserve the distinction between contemporary uncertainty and later knowledge. |
+| BANK-014-044 | How can historical comparison illuminate a crisis without claiming history repeats exactly? | Define comparable mechanisms, material differences and the limits of the analogy. |
+
+## Coverage limits and next step
+
+This is an expanded topic-bank draft, not exhaustive scholarship or a researched episode package. Each question has an individually written boundary. Source access, claim verification, current angle-scope review and publication approval remain pending. Add new branches and cases when they fill a real gap; no numeric topic cap applies.
+
+Select an exact case, edition, population, jurisdiction or product version, inspect sources and refine the question before using the existing registry idea/depth planning workflow. These BANK IDs are catalog identities, not approved IDEA IDs. Existing Q1-Q42 defaults, production queues and approval gates are unchanged.
