+## 01. Understanding evidence
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Inspect current professional guidance and original studies for the exact population, intervention or measurement. Report comparators, absolute outcomes, uncertainty, harms and limits. Do not diagnose, prescribe or imply that education replaces professional care.
+
+**Source types to locate:** current official clinical or public-health guidance; original trials, cohort studies or diagnostic-validation reports; study registrations, protocols and adverse-event records. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-015-013 | What is the difference between a symptom, a diagnosis and a risk factor? | Use a non-diagnostic example and show why the categories lead to different kinds of evidence and assessment. |
+| BANK-015-014 | How can relative-risk reduction exaggerate the impression of a benefit? | Compare absolute risks, population, time and uncertainty with clearly illustrative numbers. |
+| BANK-015-015 | Why can a screening test produce false positives in people without symptoms? | Explain prevalence, sensitivity, specificity and follow-up; avoid judging an individual's result. |
+| BANK-015-016 | What does a normal reference range establish about a laboratory test? | Distinguish statistical ranges, clinical thresholds, variation and the need for context from a qualified professional. |
+
+## 02. Prevention and public guidance
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Inspect current professional guidance and original studies for the exact population, intervention or measurement. Report comparators, absolute outcomes, uncertainty, harms and limits. Do not diagnose, prescribe or imply that education replaces professional care.
+
+**Source types to locate:** current official clinical or public-health guidance; original trials, cohort studies or diagnostic-validation reports; study registrations, protocols and adverse-event records. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-015-017 | How should a vaccine-effectiveness estimate be interpreted for a particular outcome? | Specify population, exposure period, outcome, comparator and circulating conditions rather than one universal percentage. |
+| BANK-015-018 | What does screening overdiagnosis mean beyond a false-positive test? | Distinguish detection of a real condition from whether finding it improves outcomes; inspect program-specific evidence. |
+| BANK-015-019 | How can health guidance change when evidence improves? | Compare the question, new data, benefit-harm balance and applicability without treating revision as automatic incompetence. |
+| BANK-015-020 | Why do prevention recommendations differ across age groups and circumstances? | Use current official guidance and explain eligibility, baseline risk, values and evidence limits. |
+
+## 03. Sleep and daily health
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Inspect current professional guidance and original studies for the exact population, intervention or measurement. Report comparators, absolute outcomes, uncertainty, harms and limits. Do not diagnose, prescribe or imply that education replaces professional care.
+
+**Source types to locate:** current official clinical or public-health guidance; original trials, cohort studies or diagnostic-validation reports; study registrations, protocols and adverse-event records. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-015-021 | Why are sleep duration and sleep quality different outcomes? | Compare diaries, questionnaires, devices and objective recordings without diagnosing a viewer from one measure. |
+| BANK-015-022 | How can shift work affect research on sleep and wellbeing? | Separate schedules, light exposure, workload and confounding factors in a defined study population. |
+| BANK-015-023 | What can a consumer sleep tracker reliably claim about its estimates? | Check validation against reference methods, software version and populations; distinguish wellness feedback from diagnosis. |
+| BANK-015-024 | When does general sleep-hygiene content become inadequate for persistent problems? | Explain the boundary of education, evidence limitations and the role of qualified assessment without prescribing treatment. |
+
+## 04. Movement and sedentary behavior
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Inspect current professional guidance and original studies for the exact population, intervention or measurement. Report comparators, absolute outcomes, uncertainty, harms and limits. Do not diagnose, prescribe or imply that education replaces professional care.
+
+**Source types to locate:** current official clinical or public-health guidance; original trials, cohort studies or diagnostic-validation reports; study registrations, protocols and adverse-event records. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-015-025 | How is physical activity measured differently from exercise participation? | Compare self-report, devices, duration and intensity; avoid treating a gym visit count as total activity. |
+| BANK-015-026 | Why should sedentary time and insufficient activity not be treated as identical? | Inspect definitions, overlapping behaviors and observational limitations rather than drawing personal risk conclusions. |
+| BANK-015-027 | How can a fitness study's participants differ from the audience watching a video? | Examine age, health, training history, supervision and exclusion criteria before generalizing a result. |
+| BANK-015-028 | What does a claim that exercise improves wellbeing need to specify? | Identify the intervention, comparison, outcome, follow-up and harms rather than promising one routine benefits everyone. |
+
+## 05. Food and nutrition claims
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Inspect current professional guidance and original studies for the exact population, intervention or measurement. Report comparators, absolute outcomes, uncertainty, harms and limits. Do not diagnose, prescribe or imply that education replaces professional care.
+
+**Source types to locate:** current official clinical or public-health guidance; original trials, cohort studies or diagnostic-validation reports; study registrations, protocols and adverse-event records. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-015-029 | Why can a dietary pattern be easier to study meaningfully than a single fashionable nutrient? | Compare substitution, food context, adherence and confounding without presenting one pattern as universally suitable. |
+| BANK-015-030 | How should an observational diet association differ from a causal treatment claim? | Inspect measurement, lifestyle covariates, reverse causation and supporting experimental evidence. |
+| BANK-015-031 | What does a supplement label establish without proving a health benefit? | Separate ingredients, dose, manufacturing claims, regulatory category and actual clinical evidence. |
+| BANK-015-032 | How can a deficiency claim be misleading when no diagnostic context is provided? | Distinguish population prevalence, symptoms, testing and professional interpretation; avoid encouraging self-diagnosis. |
+
+## 06. Stress and coping research
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Inspect current professional guidance and original studies for the exact population, intervention or measurement. Report comparators, absolute outcomes, uncertainty, harms and limits. Do not diagnose, prescribe or imply that education replaces professional care.
+
+**Source types to locate:** current official clinical or public-health guidance; original trials, cohort studies or diagnostic-validation reports; study registrations, protocols and adverse-event records. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-015-033 | What does a stress score measure that a hormone sample does not? | Compare subjective demand, timing, physiological variability and interpretation within a specified study. |
+| BANK-015-034 | How can a wellness intervention's benefits be confused with attention and social support? | Inspect active comparators, contact time, expectations and the actual program components. |
+| BANK-015-035 | Why should everyday distress not automatically be described as a mental disorder? | Respect experience while distinguishing impairment, assessment and the limits of general educational categories. |
+| BANK-015-036 | What evidence is needed before calling a wellness practice a treatment? | Require a defined condition, appropriate study design, meaningful outcomes, harms and current professional context. |
+
+## 07. Digital health and misinformation
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Inspect the actual product version, official documentation, terms and privacy policies. Test claims under stated conditions and distinguish account permissions, data retention, measured functionality and marketing.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official versioned documentation and release notes; dated privacy policies and service terms; reproducible product tests and original validation reports. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-015-037 | How can a symptom-checking application create both useful prompts and misleading confidence? | Examine validation, intended use, missing context and escalation language without testing it on actual private patient records. |
+| BANK-015-038 | What data can a wellness application retain beyond the user's visible diary? | Read dated policies, permissions, integrations and deletion terms rather than relying on a private-mode label. |
+| BANK-015-039 | Why does a compelling health testimonial not establish what caused the improvement? | Consider other care, natural variation, selection and missing comparison outcomes. |
+| BANK-015-040 | How can an AI-generated health citation look credible while failing verification? | Check existence, relevant passages, population and conclusion before treating an answer as sourced. |
+
+## 08. Responsible channel practice
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-015-041 | How should a health video state the population its evidence applies to? | Make age, condition, setting and exclusions explicit rather than placing a generic disclaimer after universal claims. |
+| BANK-015-042 | What distinguishes discussing a red flag from diagnosing the viewer? | Use current official guidance, explain uncertainty and direct the role of professional assessment without assigning a condition. |
+| BANK-015-043 | How can a creator correct health misinformation without amplifying it unnecessarily? | Lead with supported information, explain the error briefly and preserve links to reliable evidence. |
+| BANK-015-044 | What should be reviewed before a thumbnail promises healing, detoxification or a cure? | Inspect the exact claim, evidence, harms and audience vulnerability rather than relying on a disclaimer in the description. |
+
+## Coverage limits and next step
+
+This is an expanded topic-bank draft, not exhaustive scholarship or a researched episode package. Each question has an individually written boundary. Source access, claim verification, current angle-scope review and publication approval remain pending. Add new branches and cases when they fill a real gap; no numeric topic cap applies.
+
+Select an exact case, edition, population, jurisdiction or product version, inspect sources and refine the question before using the existing registry idea/depth planning workflow. These BANK IDs are catalog identities, not approved IDEA IDs. Existing Q1-Q42 defaults, production queues and approval gates are unchanged.
