+# 017 Kids Cartoon Stories: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 32 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Kids Cartoon Stories. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Character goals and feelings
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Create original fiction with explicit world rules, character goals and causal continuity. Label invented events and separate inspiration from claims about actual cultures or living people.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original story notes and world rules; permission-cleared reference material; reader feedback and a continuity audit. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-017-013 | How can a shy cloud learn to ask for help through visible action? | Give it a specific task, a communication attempt and a shared solution rather than an adult lecture disguised as dialogue. |
+| BANK-017-014 | How can a tiger cub apologize without receiving an instant reward? | Show recognition, repair and changed behavior; let the other character retain feelings and choices. |
+| BANK-017-015 | What makes a toy-sharing conflict understandable to a young audience? | Clarify ownership, limited resources and a negotiated outcome without making every disagreement selfishness. |
+| BANK-017-016 | How can a robot express two feelings at the same time? | Use simple situation cues and choices without implying one emotion must be suppressed for the story to end. |
+
+## 02. Everyday problem solving
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Create original fiction with explicit world rules, character goals and causal continuity. Label invented events and separate inspiration from claims about actual cultures or living people.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original story notes and world rules; permission-cleared reference material; reader feedback and a continuity audit. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-017-017 | How can two animal friends solve a broken-bridge problem by combining different skills? | Make each contribution necessary and observable; avoid solving the conflict through an unexplained magical arrival. |
+| BANK-017-018 | What can a seed's journey show about patience without promising all seeds grow? | Use a fictional character while distinguishing real plant needs and uncertainty from the story's personification. |
+| BANK-017-019 | How can a moon rover solve a navigation puzzle at a child's level? | Establish landmarks, a limited choice set and a testable plan rather than overwhelming the scene with technical terms. |
+| BANK-017-020 | How can a river-cleanup story avoid blaming one villain for every environmental problem? | Show specific sources, collective actions and limits of a small project without inventing guaranteed ecological recovery. |
+
+## 03. Social learning through story
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Create original fiction with explicit world rules, character goals and causal continuity. Label invented events and separate inspiration from claims about actual cultures or living people.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original story notes and world rules; permission-cleared reference material; reader feedback and a continuity audit. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-017-021 | How can a character say no to a game while remaining part of the group? | Model boundaries, alternatives and respect without framing participation as the price of friendship. |
+| BANK-017-022 | How can a misunderstanding be resolved through checking rather than guessing intentions? | Let characters ask, clarify and revise an assumption through dialogue and action. |
+| BANK-017-023 | What makes a mistake safe enough for a character to admit? | Show a proportionate response and practical repair rather than humiliation or instant absolution. |
+| BANK-017-024 | How can a new character join an established group without becoming identical to it? | Preserve different interests and strengths while building shared activity and mutual respect. |
+
+## 04. Age-appropriate structure
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-017-025 | How does the intended age range change the number of plot steps? | Compare vocabulary, causal links, duration and repetition using audience testing rather than assuming all children need the same structure. |
+| BANK-017-026 | When does repetition reinforce comprehension instead of merely extending runtime? | Repeat a meaningful pattern with a changed detail or choice and check what children can predict. |
+| BANK-017-027 | How can suspense remain gentle without making the story inert? | Use manageable uncertainty, reassurance and visible options rather than threats beyond the intended audience's understanding. |
+| BANK-017-028 | What helps a bedtime episode settle toward its ending? | Reduce unresolved demands, pacing and sound intensity while avoiding medical promises about sleep. |
+
+## 05. Visual clarity and movement
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-017-029 | How can silhouette distinguish characters when the screen is small? | Test shape, scale, costume and motion at actual viewing size rather than relying on detailed facial decoration. |
+| BANK-017-030 | What makes an animated action legible before a character explains it? | Use anticipation, staging, clear contact and consequences rather than replacing visual logic with narration. |
+| BANK-017-031 | How should backgrounds support attention without overwhelming the main action? | Compare contrast, motion and clutter; preserve information needed to understand the scene. |
+| BANK-017-032 | How can a series maintain believable prop sizes across episodes? | Use scale references, character turnarounds and continuity checks rather than independent attractive designs. |
+
+## 06. Language and accessibility
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-017-033 | How can a story introduce an unfamiliar word without stopping for a definition? | Use action, repetition and context; check comprehension with the intended audience. |
+| BANK-017-034 | What makes captions useful for a children's animation? | Check reading speed, speaker identification, vocabulary and important sounds without assuming captions suit every age equally. |
+| BANK-017-035 | How can dialogue remain understandable across different accents? | Prioritize clarity, context and respectful performance rather than treating one accent as the only correct voice. |
+| BANK-017-036 | How should a bilingual story handle code-switching meaningfully? | Anchor language changes to characters and situations while preserving enough context for the intended viewers. |
+
+## 07. Educational claims and representation
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-017-037 | When does an entertaining educational label overstate what a story teaches? | Define the learning goal and evaluate actual comprehension or transfer rather than viewing time alone. |
+| BANK-017-038 | How can a story represent a disabled child without making disability its only conflict? | Give agency, relationships and ordinary goals; use relevant community input for specific access needs. |
+| BANK-017-039 | What should be reviewed before using cultural dress or rituals in animal-character stories? | Check attribution, context and stereotyped simplification rather than treating a culture as decorative costume. |
+| BANK-017-040 | How can a cartoon distinguish fantasy behavior from real-world actions children may imitate? | Review foreseeable imitation, framing and age appropriateness without turning the story into a warning checklist. |
+
+## 08. Series planning and review
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-017-041 | How can recurring characters grow without contradicting earlier lessons? | Track prior choices and relationships; let new situations complicate understanding rather than erase development. |
+| BANK-017-042 | What makes an episode's lesson emerge from consequence rather than a final speech? | Tie the resolution to an observable decision and preserve emotional nuance. |
+| BANK-017-043 | How can a production review identify accidental scary details? | Check images, sounds, wording and unresolved implications with the intended age group and appropriate adult input. |
+| BANK-017-044 | How can a channel avoid making every child-facing story a moral correction? | Include curiosity, wonder, play, cooperation and open-ended discovery as valid story goals. |
+
+## Coverage limits and next step
+
+This is an expanded topic-bank draft, not exhaustive scholarship or a researched episode package. Each question has an individually written boundary. Source access, claim verification, current angle-scope review and publication approval remain pending. Add new branches and cases when they fill a real gap; no numeric topic cap applies.
+
+Select an exact case, edition, population, jurisdiction or product version, inspect sources and refine the question before using the existing registry idea/depth planning workflow. These BANK IDs are catalog identities, not approved IDEA IDs. Existing Q1-Q42 defaults, production queues and approval gates are unchanged.
