+# 018 Mythological Animation: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 32 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Mythological Animation. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Choosing the source
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Identify the text, edition, translator, commentary, community and ritual context as applicable. Attribute theological and spiritual claims, preserve regional variation and respect public versus restricted knowledge.
+
+**Source types to locate:** primary texts in traceable editions; authorized public teaching and ritual descriptions; consented community accounts and critical historical studies. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-018-013 | How should an animation choose which Ganesha birth account it is adapting? | Identify the text or community tradition, define the selected version and avoid an unacknowledged composite. |
+| BANK-018-014 | What should be preserved when adapting Hanuman's service to Rama? | Use a bounded source episode and retain motivation, speech and consequential choices rather than only feats of strength. |
+| BANK-018-015 | How can Savitri's encounter with Yama be adapted around dialogue rather than spectacle? | Trace the selected source's exchanges, sequence and agency; disclose simplified or invented wording. |
+| BANK-018-016 | Why should Krishna childhood episodes be attributed before character design begins? | Check the source, relationships, setting and narrative purpose so the visuals do not lock in mixed versions. |
+
+## 02. Narrative adaptation
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-018-017 | How can a long epic episode become a short animation without breaking its causal structure? | Choose one conflict, preserve necessary antecedents and distinguish omitted context from changed events. |
+| BANK-018-018 | When can narration replace dialogue without changing a character's position? | Compare the source's actual argument with the paraphrase and retain qualifications that affect meaning. |
+| BANK-018-019 | How can a divine event remain understandable without excessive exposition? | Show the selected narrative's rules, reactions and stakes through clear staging rather than unsupported explanatory additions. |
+| BANK-018-020 | What makes a respectful adaptation different from claiming to reproduce a text verbatim? | Label creative choices and distinguish translated passages, paraphrase and original scenes. |
+
+## 03. Deity iconography
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Identify the text, edition, translator, commentary, community and ritual context as applicable. Attribute theological and spiritual claims, preserve regional variation and respect public versus restricted knowledge.
+
+**Source types to locate:** primary texts in traceable editions; authorized public teaching and ritual descriptions; consented community accounts and critical historical studies. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-018-021 | How should multi-armed deity poses be planned before image generation? | Map attributes, hand actions, support and plausible overlap using an attributed form rather than demanding every hand face forward. |
+| BANK-018-022 | Why does a full-body deity image not require every foot to be exposed? | Preserve complete pose framing and believable concealment by clothing, seat or vahana instead of adding unnatural limbs. |
+| BANK-018-023 | How can animal-faced deities share one illustration style with human-faced deities? | Align linework, eye design, shading and material rendering while preserving the particular deity's anatomy and expression. |
+| BANK-018-024 | What should be checked before assigning a weapon, flower or vahana to a divine form? | Verify the specific manifestation and source tradition rather than copying attributes from a similarly named deity. |
+
+## 04. Performance and emotion
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-018-025 | How can devotion be shown through posture and attention rather than a static glowing pose? | Stage relationships, direction of gaze, offering and response in a source-consistent moment. |
+| BANK-018-026 | How can a fierce divine form remain expressive without defaulting to generic monstrous behavior? | Use attributed emotional and symbolic context; distinguish protective fierceness from invented cruelty. |
+| BANK-018-027 | What makes a blessing scene physically believable? | Plan relative height, hands, contact, clothing and support before adding decorative effects. |
+| BANK-018-028 | How should emotion change across a transformation or revelation scene? | Track each character's knowledge and reaction rather than changing only the lighting and costume. |
+
+## 05. Scene and asset continuity
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-018-029 | How can a deity's attributes remain consistent across generated shots? | Create form-specific references, tracked props and pose checks; inspect each shot rather than trusting a repeated prompt alone. |
+| BANK-018-030 | What prevents a crown or trident from being cut off by the composition? | Frame the complete silhouette with space for movement and verify the intended aspect ratio before generation. |
+| BANK-018-031 | How can a seated vahana scene preserve weight and anatomy? | Plan the contact points, balance, occlusion and scale; do not force hidden limbs into view as proof of completeness. |
+| BANK-018-032 | How should a temple or landscape remain stable during a scene sequence? | Use location references, camera plans and fixed landmarks instead of regenerating the entire setting independently. |
+
+## 06. Voices, language and music
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-018-033 | How can a performance handle Sanskrit or regional names consistently? | Prepare an attributed pronunciation guide and review recurring terms rather than relying on phonetic guesses. |
+| BANK-018-034 | What distinguishes an original devotional score from an unlicensed imitation of a recording? | Track composition, performance, sample and voice rights separately; document permissions and original contributions. |
+| BANK-018-035 | How can translated dialogue preserve honorifics and relationship meaning? | Compare function, register and cultural context rather than replacing every term with a literal English equivalent. |
+| BANK-018-036 | When should silence carry a devotional scene instead of narration? | Use clear action and relationships; avoid adding unsupported theological explanation to fill every pause. |
+
+## 07. Audience and sensitive scenes
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-018-037 | How can epic violence be simplified for children without reversing the episode's moral stakes? | Adjust depiction and detail while retaining the selected source's conflict and consequences. |
+| BANK-018-038 | How should a film handle a disputed narrative detail important to different communities? | Choose and name the adapted version; acknowledge variation without claiming one depiction exhausts the tradition. |
+| BANK-018-039 | What should be avoided when staging worship inside a living community's sacred space? | Respect access, consent, ritual restrictions and the difference between public art and restricted practice. |
+| BANK-018-040 | How can a thumbnail signal the story without inventing a miraculous event absent from it? | Align imagery, title and actual episode content instead of using unsupported spectacle as the central promise. |
+
+## 08. Final review and attribution
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-018-041 | What belongs in an iconography review of an animated deity scene? | Check form, attributes, limbs, pose, relationships and source context; record actual findings rather than a generic approval label. |
+| BANK-018-042 | How can an adaptation credit a text without implying all imagery comes directly from it? | Separate narrative source, visual references, interpretation and original production design. |
+| BANK-018-043 | What should change when a generated asset introduces an incorrect divine attribute? | Correct the affected references and downstream shots rather than hiding the error with a brief cut. |
+| BANK-018-044 | How can a series maintain a coherent style while allowing distinct divine personalities? | Keep rendering and craft consistent while varying sourced expression, pose, symbolism and scene purpose. |
+
+## Coverage limits and next step
+
+This is an expanded topic-bank draft, not exhaustive scholarship or a researched episode package. Each question has an individually written boundary. Source access, claim verification, current angle-scope review and publication approval remain pending. Add new branches and cases when they fill a real gap; no numeric topic cap applies.
+
+Select an exact case, edition, population, jurisdiction or product version, inspect sources and refine the question before using the existing registry idea/depth planning workflow. These BANK IDs are catalog identities, not approved IDEA IDs. Existing Q1-Q42 defaults, production queues and approval gates are unchanged.
