+# 019 AI Cinematic Short Films: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 32 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for AI Cinematic Short Films. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Story before generation
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Create original fiction with explicit world rules, character goals and causal continuity. Label invented events and separate inspiration from claims about actual cultures or living people.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original story notes and world rules; permission-cleared reference material; reader feedback and a continuity audit. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-019-013 | How can a maintenance robot discovering a garden sustain a complete short film? | Define its task, unexpected discovery, decision and consequence before choosing impressive generated environments. |
+| BANK-019-014 | What conflict emerges when a courier delivers letters after a city's evacuation? | Give the delivery a concrete recipient, obstacle and moral choice instead of relying on abandoned-city spectacle. |
+| BANK-019-015 | How can two astronauts with a failing translation system remain emotionally understandable? | Build observable misunderstandings, shared tasks and a changed relationship rather than arbitrary mistranslation jokes. |
+| BANK-019-016 | What rules would make a melody from a future recording a fair mystery? | Define what the signal can contain, how it is verified and the cost of acting on it. |
+
+## 02. Shot planning
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-019-017 | How can a shot list reduce the number of unusable generated clips? | Assign each shot a story function, action, framing and continuity needs before generating variations. |
+| BANK-019-018 | When is a static shot more useful than a complex camera movement? | Compare narrative information, controllability, artifact risk and available edit points for the same scene. |
+| BANK-019-019 | How can screen direction remain consistent across separately generated shots? | Track entrances, exits, eyelines and spatial geography with a simple scene plan. |
+| BANK-019-020 | What makes a generated close-up valuable rather than redundant? | Connect visible reaction or detail to a story change instead of treating every cinematic image as needed coverage. |
+
+## 03. Characters and identity
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-019-021 | How can a character reference separate permanent identity from scene-specific appearance? | Track anatomy, face and proportions independently from clothing, expression, lighting and camera perspective. |
+| BANK-019-022 | Why can a repeated name fail to preserve a generated character? | Compare actual reference conditioning, model settings and outputs rather than assuming narrative labels encode identity. |
+| BANK-019-023 | How can costume changes occur without losing continuity? | Record what changes, when and why; keep stable identity cues and inspect transitions. |
+| BANK-019-024 | What should be checked when generated hands interact with a prop? | Inspect grip, contact, scale, motion and object persistence instead of judging a still frame alone. |
+
+## 04. Environments and lighting
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-019-025 | How can a small number of location references support a coherent film world? | Specify layouts, landmarks, materials and time of day; separate planned variation from accidental redesign. |
+| BANK-019-026 | What makes a lighting change read as story time rather than an asset mismatch? | Track sources, shadows, exposure and motivated transitions across neighboring shots. |
+| BANK-019-027 | How can weather remain consistent while allowing local motion? | Distinguish stable conditions from moving rain, mist, vegetation and reflections; evaluate the sequence rather than isolated clips. |
+| BANK-019-028 | How should depth and scale be checked in a generated establishing shot? | Use character size, perspective, access routes and later coverage as cross-checks against attractive but unusable geometry. |
+
+## 05. Motion and generation constraints
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-019-029 | How can a source image be designed around the action a video model must produce? | Ensure support, pose, space and object relationships make the intended movement plausible before generation. |
+| BANK-019-030 | When does a shorter generated action outperform a long continuous attempt? | Compare controllability, edit coverage, temporal consistency and the information the story actually needs. |
+| BANK-019-031 | How can a loop be tested for motion discontinuity rather than only matching frames? | Inspect velocity, direction, object state, lighting and sound over repeated playback. |
+| BANK-019-032 | What distinguishes deliberate surreal motion from an accidental artifact? | Define the world's rules and audience cues; do not retroactively call every failed generation a creative choice. |
+
+## 06. Voice and sound
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-019-033 | How can sound connect generated shots that lack perfect visual continuity? | Use spatially credible ambience, transitions and motivated offscreen events without falsifying documentary evidence. |
+| BANK-019-034 | What makes synthetic dialogue feel like an exchange rather than separate readings? | Direct timing, intention, listening and overlap while preserving consent and voice-use rights. |
+| BANK-019-035 | How should a film plan for pronunciation errors before final voice generation? | Prepare tested names and alternatives, then check the actual rendered speech rather than the written prompt alone. |
+| BANK-019-036 | When can a silent sequence communicate more clearly than explanatory narration? | Build visible goals, cause and consequence; test understanding without relying on unearned music cues. |
+
+## 07. Editing and finishing
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-019-037 | How should an editor classify a generated clip as usable, repairable or unsuitable? | Evaluate story function, continuity, anatomy, motion and repair cost against the intended screen time. |
+| BANK-019-038 | When does hiding an artifact in a fast cut undermine the scene? | Check comprehension, pacing and viewer attention rather than treating speed as a universal repair method. |
+| BANK-019-039 | How can color management keep shots consistent across multiple generation tools? | Track input profiles, transformations and delivery settings instead of applying one aggressive look to every mismatch. |
+| BANK-019-040 | What should an export review inspect besides resolution? | Check frame timing, audio sync, captions, compression artifacts, aspect ratio and the complete final playback. |
+
+## 08. Rights and production evidence
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-019-041 | How can a film document the rights behind a synthetic actor or voice? | Record the actual consent, licensed scope, retention and restrictions; possession of reference media is insufficient. |
+| BANK-019-042 | What should be disclosed when AI imagery depicts a real historical person? | Distinguish illustration, dramatization and evidence; avoid fabricating apparent recordings or quotations. |
+| BANK-019-043 | How can prompt and asset versioning support a reproducible revision? | Preserve source references, settings, selected outputs and edit decisions without claiming deterministic regeneration. |
+| BANK-019-044 | What makes an AI-film production log useful for the next episode? | Record attempts, failures, costs, continuity decisions and reusable assets rather than only the final successful prompts. |
+
+## Coverage limits and next step
+
+This is an expanded topic-bank draft, not exhaustive scholarship or a researched episode package. Each question has an individually written boundary. Source access, claim verification, current angle-scope review and publication approval remain pending. Add new branches and cases when they fill a real gap; no numeric topic cap applies.
+
+Select an exact case, edition, population, jurisdiction or product version, inspect sources and refine the question before using the existing registry idea/depth planning workflow. These BANK IDs are catalog identities, not approved IDEA IDs. Existing Q1-Q42 defaults, production queues and approval gates are unchanged.
