+# 027 Genetics & Evolution: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 32 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Genetics & Evolution. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Inheritance foundations
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-027-013 | Why do Mendelian ratios depend on particular assumptions? | Compare dominance, linkage, penetrance and sampling rather than treating a simple classroom cross as every inheritance pattern. |
+| BANK-027-014 | How do genotype and phenotype differ when environment affects a trait? | Use a defined example and separate genetic association, mechanism and context. |
+| BANK-027-015 | What makes mitochondrial inheritance distinct from nuclear inheritance? | Explain transmission and variation while avoiding ancestry or health conclusions about a particular family. |
+| BANK-027-016 | How can a genetic variant be common without being beneficial or harmful? | Compare neutral variation, population history, context and evidence of function. |
+
+## 02. Gene regulation
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-027-017 | How can cells with largely the same genome behave differently? | Compare expression, regulatory context and development without implying every cell contains identical sequence in every circumstance. |
+| BANK-027-018 | What does an epigenetic change establish without showing inherited acquired traits? | Distinguish molecular marks, function, persistence and evidence across generations. |
+| BANK-027-019 | How can one gene influence several traits? | Explain a documented mechanism and distinguish direct effects from statistical associations. |
+| BANK-027-020 | Why can several genetic routes produce a similar visible trait? | Compare pathways, environment and the limits of inferring genotype from appearance. |
+
+## 03. Population processes
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-027-021 | How does genetic drift differ from selection in a small population? | Use explicit assumptions and observable predictions rather than identifying every frequency change as adaptation. |
+| BANK-027-022 | What makes a founder effect different from a general population bottleneck? | Compare sampling, subsequent growth and the evidence needed to reconstruct the history. |
+| BANK-027-023 | How can gene flow alter populations without erasing their differences? | Specify migration, reproduction, selection and time rather than treating mixing as an all-or-nothing state. |
+| BANK-027-024 | When does population structure mislead a genetic association study? | Examine sampling, ancestry-related variation, controls and alternative explanations for a correlation. |
+
+## 04. Evolutionary evidence
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-027-025 | How do phylogenetic trees change when different genes tell different histories? | Compare sampling, incomplete lineage sorting, introgression and model uncertainty using a documented case. |
+| BANK-027-026 | What distinguishes evidence of common ancestry from superficial resemblance? | Compare homologous structures, sequence data and competing explanations rather than resemblance alone. |
+| BANK-027-027 | How can ancient DNA test a hypothesis based on archaeological material? | Align samples, dates, preservation, contamination controls and the exact historical claim. |
+| BANK-027-028 | Why should an evolutionary explanation make predictions beyond a plausible story? | Identify measurable alternatives, independent evidence and results that could challenge the proposed account. |
+
+## 05. Complex traits and prediction
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-027-029 | What does heritability describe about a population rather than an individual? | Explain environment, variation and study design without interpreting it as a fixed fraction of personal destiny. |
+| BANK-027-030 | Why can polygenic scores perform differently across populations? | Compare training data, linkage patterns, environment and validation rather than assuming universal portability. |
+| BANK-027-031 | How do gene-environment interactions differ from merely having both genetic and environmental influences? | Use a defined statistical and biological example with explicit interpretation limits. |
+| BANK-027-032 | What can a genetic association establish before a causal variant is identified? | Separate linked markers, functional evidence and the steps from correlation to mechanism. |
+
+## 06. Sexual selection and adaptation
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-027-033 | How can mate-choice hypotheses be tested against alternative explanations? | Compare behavior, fitness measures, environmental context and experimental or observational limits. |
+| BANK-027-034 | What does an apparent evolutionary tradeoff require evidence to establish? | Measure the competing outcomes under specified conditions instead of assuming every cost proves an optimal adaptation. |
+| BANK-027-035 | How can a trait persist after the environment that favored it changes? | Compare time, constraints, drift and current selection rather than treating present usefulness as the only explanation. |
+| BANK-027-036 | Why is a living species not an unchanged ancestor of another living species? | Explain branching, continued evolution and the difference between comparison and direct descent. |
+
+## 07. Genomic methods
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-027-037 | What changes between short-read and long-read genome sequencing? | Compare read structure, errors, assembly and suitable questions rather than declaring one method universally superior. |
+| BANK-027-038 | How can contamination imitate an important ancient genetic finding? | Inspect controls, damage patterns, sample handling and independent replication. |
+| BANK-027-039 | Why can a reference genome make some variants harder to detect? | Compare representation, mapping, structural variation and the populations included in the reference. |
+| BANK-027-040 | What does single-cell sequencing reveal that a bulk sample averages away? | Distinguish cellular variation, technical noise, sampling and interpretation of clusters. |
+
+## 08. Public interpretation
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-027-041 | What do consumer ancestry percentages actually depend on? | Inspect reference panels, algorithms, updates and uncertainty; avoid presenting them as exact cultural identity fractions. |
+| BANK-027-042 | How should a genetics video separate ancestry, nationality and socially defined race? | Use precise definitions and evidence while avoiding biological rankings of human groups. |
+| BANK-027-043 | What consent issues arise when one person's DNA reveals information about relatives? | Examine privacy, unexpected relationships and governance without assuming one person's permission resolves every concern. |
+| BANK-027-044 | How can a channel discuss genetic risk without implying inevitability? | Present baseline risk, effect size, context and evidence limits rather than a deterministic diagnosis. |
+
+## Coverage limits and next step
+
+This is an expanded topic-bank draft, not exhaustive scholarship or a researched episode package. Each question has an individually written boundary. Source access, claim verification, current angle-scope review and publication approval remain pending. Add new branches and cases when they fill a real gap; no numeric topic cap applies.
+
+Select an exact case, edition, population, jurisdiction or product version, inspect sources and refine the question before using the existing registry idea/depth planning workflow. These BANK IDs are catalog identities, not approved IDEA IDs. Existing Q1-Q42 defaults, production queues and approval gates are unchanged.
