+# 028 Ocean & Deep Sea: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 32 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Ocean & Deep Sea. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Deep-ocean environments
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-028-013 | How do pressure, temperature and food availability vary with ocean depth? | Separate physical gradients and regional differences rather than presenting one uniform dark abyss. |
+| BANK-028-014 | Why is a mapped seafloor not the same as a directly observed habitat? | Compare sonar coverage, resolution, cameras and sampling; clarify what each method can establish. |
+| BANK-028-015 | How do abyssal plains and hadal trenches pose different research questions? | Compare geography, sediment, pressure and biology without using deepest as a synonym for every deep-sea environment. |
+| BANK-028-016 | What can one deep-sea specimen reveal without representing an entire population? | Inspect collection context, sample size and preservation before generalizing anatomy or behavior. |
+
+## 02. Energy and food webs
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-028-017 | How do hydrothermal-vent communities obtain energy without depending on local sunlight? | Explain documented chemical and biological relationships while preserving variation among sites. |
+| BANK-028-018 | What distinguishes a cold-seep ecosystem from a hydrothermal vent? | Compare fluid source, chemistry, organisms and time scales rather than grouping both under one mechanism. |
+| BANK-028-019 | How does marine snow connect surface production with the deep ocean? | Trace particles, consumption, decomposition and measurement uncertainty in carbon transfer. |
+| BANK-028-020 | Why can deep-sea food availability change over time? | Compare surface processes, transport, episodic inputs and long-term observations rather than assuming permanent scarcity at one level. |
+
+## 03. Adaptation and behavior
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-028-021 | What makes bioluminescence useful in different deep-sea contexts? | Compare documented signaling, predation and defense examples rather than assigning one purpose to all light production. |
+| BANK-028-022 | How can pressure influence the way researchers interpret animal physiology? | Distinguish observed adaptation from changes caused by bringing specimens to the surface. |
+| BANK-028-023 | What can camera observations establish about rare deep-sea behavior? | Consider lighting, disturbance, sample duration and alternative interpretations of a short encounter. |
+| BANK-028-024 | Why can similar-looking deep-sea animals belong to very different groups? | Compare diagnostic anatomy and genetic evidence instead of identification from spectacle alone. |
+
+## 04. ROVs and autonomous vehicles
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-028-025 | How do ROVs and AUVs trade control, endurance and coverage? | Compare mission requirements, tether limits, power and recovery using documented deployments. |
+| BANK-028-026 | What makes navigation difficult when satellite positioning is unavailable underwater? | Explain acoustic references, inertial estimates, drift and uncertainty without pretending one sensor gives perfect position. |
+| BANK-028-027 | How can vehicle lighting alter what a deep-sea survey records? | Compare visibility, color, animal response and the difference between natural appearance and illuminated footage. |
+| BANK-028-028 | What should be recorded when a robot collects a deep-sea sample? | Track position, depth, time, conditions, disturbance and custody so later analysis remains interpretable. |
+
+## 05. Sampling and measurement
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-028-029 | How can a sediment core preserve a history that a surface image cannot? | Compare layers, dating, disturbance and the specific inference supported by the sampled material. |
+| BANK-028-030 | What is gained by collecting a specimen while retaining pressure? | Inspect preservation aims, apparatus limitations and comparison with conventional recovery. |
+| BANK-028-031 | How should eDNA results be interpreted in an ocean with moving water? | Consider transport, persistence, contamination and the difference between detection and local abundance. |
+| BANK-028-032 | Why do deep-sea biodiversity estimates depend strongly on sampling design? | Compare area, gear, taxonomic resolution and unseen species rather than reporting one exact global count. |
+
+## 06. Disturbance and resource use
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-028-033 | What evidence is needed to evaluate seabed mining effects? | Separate direct removal, sediment plumes, noise, recovery and uncertainty across different proposed sites. |
+| BANK-028-034 | How can a short pilot disturbance fail to represent long-term commercial extraction? | Compare scale, repetition, monitoring duration and environmental conditions before extrapolating. |
+| BANK-028-035 | What does recovery mean for a slow-changing deep-sea community? | Define physical, biological and functional outcomes rather than using one visible species as the whole measure. |
+| BANK-028-036 | How should a documentary distinguish a mineral-resource estimate from a viable mining project? | Inspect concentration, engineering, economics, regulation and environmental evidence separately. |
+
+## 07. Observation networks
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
