+# 029 Dinosaurs & Prehistoric World: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 32 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Dinosaurs & Prehistoric World. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Fossils and reconstruction
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-029-013 | How does a fossil's burial history affect what can be inferred from it? | Trace death, transport, burial, alteration and excavation rather than treating the specimen as an unchanged snapshot. |
+| BANK-029-014 | What separates a dinosaur bone identification from an exact species identification? | Compare diagnostic features, completeness, variation and the possibility of revision. |
+| BANK-029-015 | How do paleontologists reconstruct an animal when only part of its skeleton survives? | Label observed material, comparative anatomy and uncertain additions in the reconstruction. |
+| BANK-029-016 | Why should dinosaurs, marine reptiles and pterosaurs not be treated as one biological group? | Explain relationships and defining evidence while preserving their shared appearance in popular prehistoric settings. |
+
+## 02. Growth and physiology
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-029-017 | What can bone histology reveal about dinosaur growth? | Compare tissue, growth marks, sampling location and interpretation without promising an exact lifespan from every fragment. |
+| BANK-029-018 | How do researchers investigate dinosaur body temperature? | Compare independent proxies, models and uncertainties rather than one universal warm-blooded or cold-blooded label. |
+| BANK-029-019 | What does a giant sauropod's anatomy imply about support and movement? | Examine skeletal evidence, soft-tissue reconstruction and model assumptions without treating a simulation as direct observation. |
+| BANK-029-020 | How can juvenile fossils be mistaken for a different species? | Compare growth series, anatomy and limited sampling while distinguishing unresolved classification debates. |
+
+## 03. Feathers, skin and appearance
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-029-021 | What evidence supports feathers in a particular dinosaur lineage? | Use actual specimens, preservation and relationships; avoid extending one find to every dinosaur without qualification. |
+| BANK-029-022 | How can pigment structures inform a proposed prehistoric color pattern? | Inspect preservation, comparison, sampling and uncertainty rather than presenting every reconstructed color as directly measured. |
+| BANK-029-023 | What can skin impressions establish about coverage and texture? | Distinguish the preserved body region from assumptions about the animal's entire exterior. |
+| BANK-029-024 | Why do new reconstructions of the same dinosaur change over time? | Compare new specimens, anatomy, methods and artistic choices instead of describing every revision as an earlier fraud. |
+
+## 04. Feeding and ecological roles
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-029-025 | What do teeth and wear patterns reveal about a dinosaur's diet? | Combine morphology, wear, residues and associated evidence rather than equating tooth shape with a complete menu. |
+| BANK-029-026 | How did hadrosaur dental batteries function as a system? | Use anatomical studies and models to connect replacement, wear and feeding while identifying reconstructed aspects. |
+| BANK-029-027 | What evidence distinguishes scavenging from active predation in a fossil case? | Compare bite marks, healing, context and alternative explanations rather than declaring one exclusive lifestyle. |
+| BANK-029-028 | How can a prehistoric food web be reconstructed from incomplete remains? | State evidence for links, sampling biases and uncertainties instead of drawing every connection as equally established. |
+
+## 05. Behavior from traces
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-029-029 | What can a trackway reveal about movement without preserving the animal? | Compare stride, substrate, preservation and possible trackmakers; avoid precise speed claims without assumptions. |
+| BANK-029-030 | When do grouped fossils support social behavior rather than shared burial? | Inspect site formation, age structure, orientation and competing explanations. |
+| BANK-029-031 | How can nests and eggs inform reproductive behavior? | Distinguish association, embryonic evidence, nest structure and uncertainty about parental care. |
+| BANK-029-032 | What do healed injuries reveal without proving a particular social story? | Separate survival, biological repair and speculative scenarios about how the injury happened. |
+
+## 06. Changing prehistoric worlds
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-029-033 | How did Triassic communities differ from the familiar dinosaur-dominated image? | Use a bounded region and interval; include other groups and ecological changes rather than one global tableau. |
+| BANK-029-034 | What can the Morrison Formation reveal about regional Jurassic ecosystems? | Compare sites, environments and time averaging instead of placing every known species in one simultaneous scene. |
+| BANK-029-035 | How did flowering-plant expansion change some Cretaceous landscapes? | Inspect geographic and chronological evidence without assuming an immediate universal ecological replacement. |
+| BANK-029-036 | Why should marine and terrestrial fossil records be compared cautiously? | Separate preservation, sampling, habitat and dating rather than interpreting raw fossil totals as population counts. |
+
+## 07. Extinction and survival
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-029-037 | Which evidence links the end-Cretaceous extinction to an impact? | Compare stratigraphy, geochemistry, dating and ecological patterns while distinguishing established links from unresolved mechanisms. |
+| BANK-029-038 | How are volcanism and other stressors evaluated alongside an impact explanation? | Compare timing, magnitude and independent evidence rather than forcing a single-cause debate by default. |
+| BANK-029-039 | Why did some lineages survive an extinction that eliminated many others? | Test traits, environments and sampling limits; avoid claiming survivors were simply stronger or more advanced. |
+| BANK-029-040 | What does ecosystem recovery look like after a mass extinction? | Separate diversity, abundance, function and time scale using defined fossil evidence. |
+
+## 08. Public claims and museum practice
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-029-041 | How should a documentary handle claims of preserved dinosaur soft tissue? | Inspect definitions, original studies, contamination controls and disagreements rather than equating every organic signal with fresh flesh. |
+| BANK-029-042 | What makes a museum mount a scientific interpretation rather than a direct replica? | Identify casts, missing parts, posture decisions and the evidence behind later changes. |
+| BANK-029-043 | How can a film depict dinosaur sound responsibly? | Separate anatomical inference, living comparisons and original sound design; do not present an invented roar as recovered audio. |
+| BANK-029-044 | Why should a discovery story include locality, permits and preparation work? | Credit field teams, collections and research context rather than treating a fossil as a treasure found without obligations. |
+
+## Coverage limits and next step
+
+This is an expanded topic-bank draft, not exhaustive scholarship or a researched episode package. Each question has an individually written boundary. Source access, claim verification, current angle-scope review and publication approval remain pending. Add new branches and cases when they fill a real gap; no numeric topic cap applies.
+
+Select an exact case, edition, population, jurisdiction or product version, inspect sources and refine the question before using the existing registry idea/depth planning workflow. These BANK IDs are catalog identities, not approved IDEA IDs. Existing Q1-Q42 defaults, production queues and approval gates are unchanged.
