+# 032 Space Exploration: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 32 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Space Exploration. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Mission questions and constraints
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-032-013 | How does a scientific question determine a space mission's instruments? | Trace requirements, measurements, precision, resources and alternatives through an actual mission design. |
+| BANK-032-014 | Why can a launch date constrain where a spacecraft can go? | Explain orbital geometry, transfer opportunities and mission-specific limits rather than one universal launch window. |
+| BANK-032-015 | What makes a robotic mission's autonomy different from independence from Earth? | Compare onboard decisions, ground planning, communication delay and failure handling. |
+| BANK-032-016 | How can a mission meet an engineering objective while missing a scientific one? | Separate launch, navigation, instrument function and the data needed to answer the original question. |
+
+## 02. Launch and transfer
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-032-017 | How do staging and payload requirements interact in a launch design? | Use explicit mass and performance assumptions without interpreting a simplified calculation as a complete rocket design. |
+| BANK-032-018 | Why is reaching orbit different from merely reaching space? | Compare trajectory, speed, energy and mission definitions with properly labeled diagrams. |
+| BANK-032-019 | How can gravity assists alter a spacecraft's route and timing? | Trace a documented encounter, reference frames and tradeoffs without calling the maneuver free propulsion in every sense. |
+| BANK-032-020 | What makes a reusable stage's recovery requirements affect its payload mission? | Compare reserves, trajectory, hardware and refurbishment evidence rather than reuse as an isolated feature. |
+
+## 03. Landing on other worlds
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-032-021 | Why do lunar and Martian landings require different descent strategies? | Compare atmosphere, gravity, terrain, sensing and actual mission constraints. |
+| BANK-032-022 | What can terrain-relative navigation improve during a landing? | Explain references, onboard sensing, uncertainty and remaining hazards using official mission descriptions. |
+| BANK-032-023 | How do engineers test a landing system that cannot be fully reproduced on Earth? | Compare simulations, component tests, analog environments and the gaps between them. |
+| BANK-032-024 | Why can a landing site suitable for science be difficult for engineering? | Balance illumination, terrain, communications, hazards and target access rather than choosing by visual interest alone. |
+
+## 04. Rovers and surface science
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-032-025 | How does rover wheel design reflect terrain and lifetime assumptions? | Compare documented loads, materials, damage and operating strategies across identified missions. |
+| BANK-032-026 | What does a rover's drill sample establish about the surrounding region? | Consider location, sampling bias, contamination and interpretation beyond one target. |
+| BANK-032-027 | How can route planning preserve a rover's scientific options? | Compare energy, terrain, communications, instrument use and uncertain findings. |
+| BANK-032-028 | Why does a robotic arm require more than reaching a visible rock? | Trace positioning, contact, force, instrument geometry and calibration in a sourced example. |
+
+## 05. Deep-space operations
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-032-029 | How do communication delay and bandwidth shape mission decisions? | Compare data volume, onboard storage, prioritization and ground contact schedules. |
+| BANK-032-030 | What does a spacecraft do when it loses reliable attitude information? | Explain documented redundancy and safe-state principles without inventing a mission's recovery procedure. |
+| BANK-032-031 | How are software updates managed when physical access is impossible? | Compare verification, transmission, fallback and operational risk in published mission accounts. |
+| BANK-032-032 | Why can a healthy spacecraft still have limited science time? | Inspect power, thermal conditions, communication, pointing and instrument constraints. |
+
+## 06. Mission histories
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-032-033 | How did Apollo navigation depend on cooperation between astronauts and ground teams? | Use mission records to distinguish onboard capability, planning and support rather than a lone-pilot narrative. |
+| BANK-032-034 | What shaped Voyager's instrument and trajectory choices? | Compare original objectives, resource constraints and later extensions without presenting later discoveries as the original plan. |
+| BANK-032-035 | How did Mars rover missions change their approach to mobility and autonomy? | Compare actual generations, tasks, terrain experience and operational lessons. |
+| BANK-032-036 | What can a failed mission teach when the evidence is incomplete? | Separate established causes, contributing factors, uncertainty and changes made afterward. |
+
+## 07. Samples, contamination and protection
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
