+# 034 Exploration & Adventure: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 32 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Exploration & Adventure. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Expedition questions
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-034-013 | What distinguishes exploration from being the first outsider to publish an account? | Examine existing inhabitants, guides and prior knowledge rather than equating European records with discovery. |
+| BANK-034-014 | How can an expedition's scientific purpose differ from its public legend? | Compare plans, funding, field records and later promotional narratives. |
+| BANK-034-015 | What does a travel diary reveal about observation and self-presentation? | Read intended audience, retrospective editing and independent corroboration together. |
+| BANK-034-016 | Why should a route reconstruction show uncertainty? | Compare place names, maps, travel times and missing records rather than drawing a precise path from incomplete text. |
+
+## 02. Historical journeys
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-034-017 | How can Ibn Battuta's travels be studied alongside their textual transmission? | Separate the journey, composition, later copies and corroboration for particular regions. |
+| BANK-034-018 | What do surviving records establish about Zheng He's voyages? | Compare inscriptions, texts, routes and archaeological evidence without treating later global claims as confirmed. |
+| BANK-034-019 | How did Shackleton's expedition depend on logistics and collective work? | Use dated records and participant perspectives rather than a solitary leadership myth. |
+| BANK-034-020 | What made searches for the sources of major rivers politically consequential? | Include local knowledge, mapping, imperial claims and disagreement over definitions of a source. |
+
+## 03. Navigation and measurement
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-034-021 | How did navigators combine instruments with local environmental knowledge? | Use a defined expedition and distinguish observed practice from later generalized explanations. |
+| BANK-034-022 | What makes a map useful even when its position estimates are imperfect? | Compare scale, landmarks, intended route and known error rather than demanding modern accuracy from every historical map. |
+| BANK-034-023 | How can weather observations become part of an expedition's scientific legacy? | Inspect instruments, calibration, location and continuity before combining historical records with modern data. |
+| BANK-034-024 | Why should an expedition record failed measurements and uncertain identifications? | Show how those records constrain later interpretation instead of preserving only successful discoveries. |
+
+## 04. Logistics and support
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-034-025 | How does an expedition translate a route into food and fuel requirements? | State terrain, duration, carrying capacity, resupply and uncertainty without giving a hazardous survival prescription. |
+| BANK-034-026 | What changes when transport depends on animals, porters or seasonal waterways? | Compare care, labor, conditions, timing and capacity using actual expedition records. |
+| BANK-034-027 | How can communications redundancy support a remote team? | Explain coverage, power, protocols and limitations at a planning level rather than promising universal connection. |
+| BANK-034-028 | Why can emergency extraction remain uncertain despite a written plan? | Compare location, weather, access, resources and decision authority in a documented case. |
+
+## 05. Environment and exposure
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-034-029 | How can an expedition plan account for rapidly changing mountain conditions? | Use current authoritative information and uncertainty rather than a fixed route description as safety assurance. |
+| BANK-034-030 | What makes polar logistics different from a cold-weather city journey? | Compare transport, isolation, infrastructure and environmental constraints without implying a simple equipment list resolves risk. |
+| BANK-034-031 | How can remote fieldwork disturb the environment it aims to document? | Examine sampling, waste, wildlife contact and access in the specific research setting. |
+| BANK-034-032 | What does acclimatization research establish and not establish for an individual traveler? | Use professional guidance and population evidence; do not prescribe personal altitude schedules. |
+
+## 06. Guides and communities
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use consented participant accounts, local records and the community's own descriptions. Include differences of position, access and power; testimony is not a representative survey or clinical proof.
+
+**Source types to locate:** consented interviews and oral histories; community publications and local records; field notes with method and consent documented. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-034-033 | How can a documentary credit guides whose expertise shaped a route? | Attribute planning, interpretation and practical decisions rather than naming guides only in the closing credits. |
+| BANK-034-034 | What does meaningful local consent require before expedition filming? | Clarify authority, individual participation, restricted knowledge and intended distribution. |
+| BANK-034-035 | How can expedition income benefit some residents while burdening others? | Compare employment, ownership, access, waste and local disagreements instead of one community-wide impact claim. |
+| BANK-034-036 | When should a sensitive location not be publicly geotagged? | Consider stewardship, privacy, sacred access and ecological pressure using local perspectives and rules. |
+
+## 07. Expedition outcomes
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-034-037 | How can a failed expedition still produce useful knowledge? | Separate objectives, recorded observations, surviving specimens and later use rather than judging only arrival at a destination. |
+| BANK-034-038 | What distinguishes a claimed summit from an independently documented ascent? | Inspect route, records, conditions and category definitions without assigning guilt from missing publicity alone. |
+| BANK-034-039 | How can survival accounts change after a successful rescue? | Compare contemporaneous notes, interviews, trauma-related uncertainty and later narrative shaping. |
+| BANK-034-040 | Why should specimen collections be traced after an expedition ends? | Follow cataloging, ownership, research, return obligations and missing provenance. |
+
+## 08. Modern interpretation
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-034-041 | How can a reenactment communicate historical constraints without claiming exact replication? | Label modern equipment, support, environmental differences and the experiment's actual question. |
+| BANK-034-042 | What should a sponsored adventure film disclose about support outside the frame? | Include route assistance, safety teams and staged repetition where material to the claim. |
+| BANK-034-043 | How can a channel distinguish challenge entertainment from transferable field guidance? | Separate controlled production conditions, expertise and audience assumptions without implying imitation is appropriate. |
+| BANK-034-044 | What makes an expedition's definition of success change over time? | Compare original aims, participants' views, scientific value and consequences for the people encountered. |
+
+## Coverage limits and next step
+
+This is an expanded topic-bank draft, not exhaustive scholarship or a researched episode package. Each question has an individually written boundary. Source access, claim verification, current angle-scope review and publication approval remain pending. Add new branches and cases when they fill a real gap; no numeric topic cap applies.
+
+Select an exact case, edition, population, jurisdiction or product version, inspect sources and refine the question before using the existing registry idea/depth planning workflow. These BANK IDs are catalog identities, not approved IDEA IDs. Existing Q1-Q42 defaults, production queues and approval gates are unchanged.
+
+## Related niche homes
