+## 01. Dynastic evidence and identity
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-036-036 | What makes a dynasty a useful historical category rather than a complete political explanation? | Distinguish family, ruling title, territory, institutions and later historians' labels using a specific transition. |
+| BANK-036-037 | How can inscriptions and court chronicles disagree about royal succession? | Compare dates, patronage, genre and independent evidence rather than forcing a single unqualified king list. |
+| BANK-036-038 | What does a royal genealogy claim about legitimacy? | Read origin stories, marriage links and omissions alongside the political context in which the genealogy circulated. |
+| BANK-036-039 | How should a dynasty's map represent changing degrees of authority? | Separate administration, tribute, alliances, campaigns and claimed influence instead of a permanent solid-color empire. |
+
