+# 037 Castles, Forts & Palaces: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 32 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Castles, Forts & Palaces. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Reading a fortified site
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-037-013 | How can a fort be studied as a changing settlement rather than one finished monument? | Compare building phases, repairs, occupation and later uses before assigning every feature to one ruler. |
+| BANK-037-014 | What separates defensive design from later decorative alteration? | Use construction evidence, records and functional context instead of interpreting appearance alone. |
+| BANK-037-015 | Why should a palace's public route not stand in for the whole historical complex? | Identify restricted, lost, rebuilt and service areas with appropriate evidence. |
+| BANK-037-016 | How can a site's popular legend be presented alongside architectural history? | Attribute oral traditions and compare dated material evidence without making either answer every question. |
+
+## 02. Indian hill and land forts
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-037-017 | How did Chittorgarh's water and terrain support life within its defenses? | Use mapped features, construction phases and evidence of use rather than battle narratives alone. |
+| BANK-037-018 | What did Kumbhalgarh's topography contribute beyond the length of its wall? | Compare access, visibility, settlement and maintenance without treating a superlative as strategic explanation. |
+| BANK-037-019 | How did Daulatabad organize successive barriers and approaches? | Trace actual structures and dates; distinguish historical use from present visitor experience. |
+| BANK-037-020 | What do Golconda's layers reveal about changing political and military priorities? | Compare phases, routes, occupation and records rather than assigning a single complete plan. |
+
+## 03. Fortification systems
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-037-021 | How do moats, walls and gate sequences work as a combined defense? | Use a dated example and distinguish intended function from evidence of actual performance. |
+| BANK-037-022 | What changed when artillery altered the requirements of fortification? | Compare material, geometry, landscape and construction costs rather than a universal abrupt replacement. |
+| BANK-037-023 | Why does supplying a fort matter as much as its wall strength? | Examine water, food, access and population under ordinary and crisis conditions. |
+| BANK-037-024 | How can a coastal fort's role depend on navigation and trade? | Connect position, weapons, harbor access and political control using a bounded historical case. |
+
+## 04. Royal spaces
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-037-025 | How did Topkapi organize access and hierarchy through space? | Compare actual periods, routes, institutions and functions instead of treating the complex as one static household. |
+| BANK-037-026 | How did Versailles connect ceremony with political relationships? | Use court practice and architecture while distinguishing royal intention from every participant's experience. |
+| BANK-037-027 | What does the Forbidden City's spatial organization communicate about authority? | Connect named buildings, ritual and changing uses without assuming one universal symbolic reading. |
+| BANK-037-028 | How did Mysore Palace's design relate to royal display and modern change? | Compare construction, materials, ceremony and the evidence for historic and later interpretation. |
+
+## 05. Daily work and households
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-037-029 | What can palace kitchens and stores reveal about labor and provisioning? | Use records, surviving spaces and material evidence rather than imagining only banquets. |
+| BANK-037-030 | How did service routes shape life inside a court complex? | Distinguish formal circulation, work, privacy and later alterations using identified evidence. |
+| BANK-037-031 | What does a royal residence reveal about people who were not royal? | Include artisans, guards, clerks, attendants and nearby communities with traceable sources. |
+| BANK-037-032 | Why can surviving elite rooms give a distorted impression of an entire settlement? | Compare preservation, restoration priorities and missing ordinary buildings. |
+
+## 06. Conservation and living use
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use consented participant accounts, local records and the community's own descriptions. Include differences of position, access and power; testimony is not a representative survey or clinical proof.
+
+**Source types to locate:** consented interviews and oral histories; community publications and local records; field notes with method and consent documented. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-037-033 | How can residents' needs differ from the heritage narrative offered to visitors? | Use multiple local perspectives, access and livelihood evidence rather than presenting one community position. |
+| BANK-037-034 | What makes restoration different from reconstructing a missing structure? | Identify original fabric, replacement, documentation and the level of conjecture. |
+| BANK-037-035 | How does water damage threaten a fort even after its military role ends? | Compare drainage, materials, vegetation and maintenance evidence without diagnosing a specific site remotely. |
+| BANK-037-036 | When does adaptive reuse preserve a palace while changing its historical meaning? | Examine access, commercial uses, conservation choices and interpretation. |
+
+## 07. Tourism and interpretation
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use dated official access rules, operator schedules and local information. Separate general planning from time-sensitive availability; include accessibility, local perspectives and changing environmental conditions.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official transport operators and destination authorities; current permit, visa and access rules; consented local accounts and independently verified travel costs. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-037-037 | How should a fort visit distinguish official access from informal entry advice? | Verify current routes, restrictions, permissions and local conditions rather than relying on old videos. |
+| BANK-037-038 | What does meaningful accessibility information look like for a hill fort? | Describe surfaces, gradients, distances, transport and facilities with a date and uncertainty. |
+| BANK-037-039 | How can photography create a false impression of an abandoned or empty palace? | Explain framing, public hours and unseen contemporary use rather than equating an empty shot with absence of people. |
+| BANK-037-040 | What should a guide verify before repeating a dramatic siege story? | Trace sources, chronology and variation instead of using the most memorable version as fact. |
+
+## 08. Documentary reconstruction
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-037-041 | How can a digital reconstruction show missing walls without pretending they are known exactly? | Label archaeological evidence, comparative choices and conjecture separately. |
+| BANK-037-042 | What makes a palace floor plan a source rather than a neutral image? | Check date, purpose, surveyed scope and later changes before using it as a historical guide. |
+| BANK-037-043 | How should a film visualize royal ceremony when the surviving descriptions disagree? | Attribute versions and stage only what the chosen evidence supports. |
+| BANK-037-044 | What can a revisit reveal that a monument-only episode misses? | Compare maintenance, residents, access and interpretation over time rather than ending history at construction. |
+
+## Coverage limits and next step
+
+This is an expanded topic-bank draft, not exhaustive scholarship or a researched episode package. Each question has an individually written boundary. Source access, claim verification, current angle-scope review and publication approval remain pending. Add new branches and cases when they fill a real gap; no numeric topic cap applies.
+
+Select an exact case, edition, population, jurisdiction or product version, inspect sources and refine the question before using the existing registry idea/depth planning workflow. These BANK IDs are catalog identities, not approved IDEA IDs. Existing Q1-Q42 defaults, production queues and approval gates are unchanged.
