+# 038 Maps & Geography Mysteries: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 32 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Maps & Geography Mysteries. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Maps as models
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-038-013 | Why can no flat world map preserve every geometric property? | Compare area, angle, distance and direction with explicit projection choices. |
+| BANK-038-014 | How does map scale change which features are visible or generalized? | Use matched regions and explain simplification rather than mistaking missing detail for error. |
+| BANK-038-015 | What makes a geographic center depend on the question being asked? | Define boundaries, weighting and calculation rather than announcing one universal center. |
+| BANK-038-016 | How can a map be mathematically accurate while encouraging a misleading comparison? | Inspect framing, projection, legend, classification and omitted context. |
+
+## 02. Borders and anomalies
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-038-017 | How can a treaty create an enclave without changing the physical landscape? | Trace dated legal decisions, surveying and administration rather than treating borders as natural features. |
+| BANK-038-018 | Why can moving rivers produce disagreement about a boundary's location? | Compare relevant terms, river processes and jurisdiction-specific interpretations. |
+| BANK-038-019 | What makes a territory's legal status different from how a travel map labels it? | Separate sovereignty, administration, claims and publisher conventions. |
+| BANK-038-020 | How should a map show disputed borders without silently choosing one claim? | Use dated source attribution and separate visual layers for claims and control. |
+
+## 03. Oceans, time and routes
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-038-021 | Why does the international date line deviate from a simple longitude? | Compare civil decisions, island groups, commerce and changes over time. |
+| BANK-038-022 | How can the shortest route look curved on a world map? | Distinguish surface geometry, projection and real transport constraints. |
+| BANK-038-023 | What does a coastline's measured length depend on? | Specify resolution, method and included features rather than presenting incompatible measurements as direct rankings. |
+| BANK-038-024 | Why are antipodes less intuitive than opposite sides of a flat map? | Work through latitude, longitude and actual land-ocean distribution using clear examples. |
+
+## 04. Rivers and drainage puzzles
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-038-025 | How can one river divide into channels leading toward different basins? | Distinguish ordinary branching, temporary flooding and documented bifurcation mechanisms. |
+| BANK-038-026 | What makes an endorheic basin different from a river system reaching the sea? | Compare inflows, losses, topography and change rather than defining it as simply a lake without an outlet. |
+| BANK-038-027 | Why can a drainage divide move over geological time? | Explain erosion, uplift and river capture with evidence from a bounded landscape. |
+| BANK-038-028 | How should claims about the longest river be evaluated? | Compare source choice, seasonal channels, mapping method and definitions of the main stem. |
+
+## 05. Historical cartography
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-038-029 | What did portolan charts record accurately for their intended users? | Compare coastal knowledge, navigation conventions, copying and limitations rather than judging only by modern outlines. |
+| BANK-038-030 | How did colonial surveys turn measurement into administrative power? | Use a named survey, categories, local knowledge and consequences instead of treating mapping as purely technical. |
+| BANK-038-031 | Why did early maps include places their makers had never observed? | Trace reports, inherited authorities, speculation and visual convention. |
+| BANK-038-032 | How can a mapping error persist across many later maps? | Follow copying, source credibility and the evidence that eventually corrected the feature. |
+
+## 06. Remote observation
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-038-033 | What can satellite imagery establish about a landscape without a field visit? | Specify resolution, bands, dates, processing and ground-truth limits. |
+| BANK-038-034 | How can shadows or perspective create a false structure in an aerial image? | Compare lighting, terrain, multiple views and alternative explanations. |
+| BANK-038-035 | Why can two elevation models give different heights for the same place? | Check reference surfaces, resolution, interpolation and actual measurement method. |
+| BANK-038-036 | What makes a before-and-after map comparison valid? | Align scale, date, definitions and data quality rather than relying on visual contrast alone. |
+
+## 07. Geographic records and claims
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Define the rival claims and their evidence standards. Compare sources on equal terms, separate empirical findings from values or interpretations and do not imply that all positions have equal evidential support.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the original sources behind the compared claims; explicit definitions and measurement methods; independent critiques with traceable evidence. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-038-037 | How can a highest-point claim change when definitions change? | Separate summit elevation, prominence, base measurement and agreed reference systems. |
+| BANK-038-038 | What does official recognition add to a geographic name? | Distinguish local use, historical names, administrative standards and contested naming. |
+| BANK-038-039 | Why can a surprising map fact be true only under a hidden definition? | State the exact categories and test near-counterexamples rather than repeating a trivia slogan. |
+| BANK-038-040 | How should a channel investigate a claimed magnetic hill? | Compare measured slope, visual cues, instruments and known physics instead of inferring an exotic force from motion alone. |
+
+## 08. Visual explanation
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-038-041 | How can animation explain a border's history without making change seem inevitable? | Show dated decisions, uncertainty and local consequences rather than a single smooth expansion graphic. |
+| BANK-038-042 | What makes a choropleth map misleading when raw counts are used? | Compare population, area, rates, classification and the question the map claims to answer. |
+| BANK-038-043 | How should a map distinguish observed data from an interpolated surface? | Label coverage, method and uncertainty so smooth color does not imply dense measurement. |
+| BANK-038-044 | What should be corrected when a geographic video uses an outdated boundary or name? | Identify the relevant date, source and changed claim without silently substituting a new map. |
+
+## Coverage limits and next step
+
+This is an expanded topic-bank draft, not exhaustive scholarship or a researched episode package. Each question has an individually written boundary. Source access, claim verification, current angle-scope review and publication approval remain pending. Add new branches and cases when they fill a real gap; no numeric topic cap applies.
+
+Select an exact case, edition, population, jurisdiction or product version, inspect sources and refine the question before using the existing registry idea/depth planning workflow. These BANK IDs are catalog identities, not approved IDEA IDs. Existing Q1-Q42 defaults, production queues and approval gates are unchanged.
+
