+# 039 Archaeology: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 32 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Archaeology. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Archaeological questions
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-039-013 | How does a research question change the way a site is sampled? | Compare excavation, survey and preservation choices rather than treating excavation as collecting every object. |
+| BANK-039-014 | What can an artifact reveal only when its context is recorded? | Trace location, association, layer and disturbance; explain what looting removes from interpretation. |
+| BANK-039-015 | Why is an archaeological culture not automatically a named ethnic group? | Distinguish material classification, chronology, language and identity evidence. |
+| BANK-039-016 | How can a site preserve several different histories in the same ground? | Use formation processes, reuse and stratigraphy rather than one event that explains every layer. |
+
+## 02. Excavation and stratigraphy
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-039-017 | How do archaeologists distinguish a cut feature from the deposit filling it? | Use recorded relationships and interpretation checks without assuming every soil difference is a separate occupation. |
+| BANK-039-018 | What does a stratigraphic sequence establish before absolute dates are available? | Explain relative order, uncertainty, disturbance and the limits of correlating separate areas. |
+| BANK-039-019 | Why can removing one layer destroy evidence that cannot be collected again? | Compare recording, sampling, conservation and the irreversible character of excavation. |
+| BANK-039-020 | How do excavation strategies balance detail with the need to understand a larger site? | Discuss area, depth, research aims and resource constraints in a documented project. |
+
+## 03. Dating methods
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-039-021 | How does radiocarbon dating connect a sample to an archaeological event? | Separate organism death, material reuse, contamination, calibration and actual context. |
+| BANK-039-022 | What can an associated coin establish about the date of a deposit? | Distinguish minting, circulation, residual material and the earliest possible date. |
+| BANK-039-023 | Why can pottery chronology require regional rather than universal comparisons? | Compare style, technology, context and the dating evidence behind the sequence. |
+| BANK-039-024 | How should conflicting dates from one site be investigated? | Inspect samples, methods, context and model assumptions rather than selecting the preferred answer. |
+
+## 04. Survey and remote methods
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-039-025 | What does ground-penetrating radar detect before an anomaly is identified archaeologically? | Explain physical contrasts, resolution, processing and excavation or independent confirmation. |
+| BANK-039-026 | How can lidar reveal terrain features while leaving their purpose uncertain? | Separate measured shape, filtering, field verification and interpretation. |
+| BANK-039-027 | What do surface artifact surveys miss? | Compare visibility, vegetation, erosion, collection strategy and buried deposits. |
+| BANK-039-028 | How can geophysical methods complement rather than replace excavation? | Match each method's question, scale and limitations within a research design. |
+
+## 05. Material analysis
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-039-029 | How can residue analysis suggest what a vessel once contained? | Inspect contamination, reference comparisons, preservation and the distinction between a detected compound and a complete recipe. |
+| BANK-039-030 | What can isotopes reveal about movement without identifying an exact birthplace? | Compare tissue formation, baselines, overlapping regions and uncertainty. |
+| BANK-039-031 | How do manufacturing traces distinguish production from later damage? | Use microscopy, experiments and context rather than visual resemblance alone. |
+| BANK-039-032 | What can archaeobotanical evidence establish about cultivation and diet? | Separate preservation, sampling, processing and the difference between plant presence and routine consumption. |
+
+## 06. Human remains and social inference
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-039-033 | What can a burial arrangement reveal without directly exposing personal beliefs? | Compare practice, context, variation and alternative explanations rather than assuming one symbolic meaning. |
+| BANK-039-034 | How should age and biological sex estimates be reported from skeletal evidence? | Use methods, ranges and uncertainty; distinguish estimates from certainty about social identity. |
+| BANK-039-035 | Why do signs of skeletal stress not straightforwardly rank population health? | Consider survival, exposure, sampling and the meanings of the observed markers. |
+| BANK-039-036 | How can ancient DNA and archaeology answer related but different questions? | Align the sampled people, dates and material context instead of treating ancestry as a complete cultural history. |
+
+## 07. Ethics and stewardship
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-039-037 | How does community participation change archaeological decision-making? | Compare authority, goals, access, interpretation and benefits rather than adding consultation only after excavation. |
+| BANK-039-038 | What must be considered before excavating or displaying human remains? | Use applicable laws, descendant perspectives, consent frameworks and institutional responsibilities. |
+| BANK-039-039 | How can repatriation claims involve more than proving a modern ownership document? | Trace acquisition, colonial context, law, cultural relationships and evidential gaps. |
+| BANK-039-040 | Why does preventive conservation matter before an object leaves the ground? | Explain environmental change, materials, treatment planning and specialist involvement without prescribing conservation procedures. |
+
+## 08. Public archaeology
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-039-041 | How can a reconstruction show uncertainty without becoming visually unreadable? | Separate known foundations, comparative inference and conjectural detail through consistent labels. |
+| BANK-039-042 | What makes a sensational archaeological announcement premature? | Inspect context, methods, dating, specialist review and the strength of the exact claimed conclusion. |
+| BANK-039-043 | How should a channel distinguish a new excavation from a new interpretation of an old find? | State what changed in evidence, methods or argument rather than presenting every story as discovery of a new object. |
+| BANK-039-044 | What can an archaeological documentary conclude when competing interpretations remain? | Compare support and missing evidence without forcing a winner or implying all hypotheses are equal. |
