+# 041 Languages & Linguistics: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 32 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Languages & Linguistics. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Language evidence and classification
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-041-013 | What evidence supports grouping languages into a family? | Compare systematic sound correspondences, inherited morphology and shared innovations while separating borrowing from descent. |
+| BANK-041-014 | Why can a language family tree hide sustained contact? | Use one contact zone to compare tree models with diffusion, bilingualism and overlapping innovations. |
+| BANK-041-015 | How do linguists investigate a language with no surviving speakers? | Distinguish inscriptions, comparative reconstruction, transcriptions and the limits of each evidence type. |
+| BANK-041-016 | What makes the boundary between a language and a dialect contested? | Compare intelligibility, standardization, political identity and speakers' own descriptions in a defined region. |
+
+## 02. Sounds and speech
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-041-017 | How does a phoneme differ from a physically recorded speech sound? | Compare meaningful contrasts, contextual variants and acoustic measurements in one language. |
+| BANK-041-018 | Why do borrowed words change pronunciation after entering another language? | Trace adaptation to sound inventories and syllable patterns without treating an accent as defective speech. |
+| BANK-041-019 | How do tone and intonation convey different kinds of meaning? | Use annotated examples to separate lexical contrasts, sentence melody and regional variation. |
+| BANK-041-020 | What does a historical sound change explain beyond a single word? | Test a proposed correspondence across related vocabulary and account for conditioning and exceptions. |
+
+## 03. Grammar and meaning
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-041-021 | How can languages organize information without the same word order? | Compare case marking, agreement and discourse context through translated examples with explicit limitations. |
+| BANK-041-022 | Why do grammatical categories resist one-to-one translation? | Examine tense, aspect or evidentiality in two languages without assuming categories are universally identical. |
+| BANK-041-023 | How does a word acquire a new meaning without losing every older sense? | Trace dated usage, metaphor, specialization and competing interpretations in a documented lexical history. |
+| BANK-041-024 | What can pragmatics explain that a literal sentence translation misses? | Compare context, politeness, implication and speaker relationships rather than inventing universal cultural rules. |
+
+## 04. Scripts and writing systems
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-041-025 | How do alphabetic, syllabic and abugida systems represent speech differently? | Compare actual spelling conventions and mixed features rather than assigning every script to a rigid pure type. |
+| BANK-041-026 | How did Brahmi-derived scripts develop distinct regional forms? | Follow selected dated inscriptions, materials and scribal practices while marking disputed genealogies. |
+| BANK-041-027 | Why can the direction or shape of writing change over time? | Investigate writing tools, surfaces, layout and transmission through a specific script history. |
+| BANK-041-028 | What must a decipherment explain before it becomes persuasive? | Evaluate repeated sign patterns, bilingual evidence, phonetic readings and independent checks instead of isolated visual resemblance. |
+
+## 05. South Asian language histories
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-041-029 | How should Sanskrit's textual registers be distinguished across periods? | Compare a limited set of Vedic, epic and classical usages without describing Sanskrit as unchanging. |
+| BANK-041-030 | What do the Prakrits reveal about literary language and social setting? | Separate named literary conventions, inscriptional usage and uncertain relationships to everyday speech. |
+| BANK-041-031 | How did Tamil literary traditions interact with changing political institutions? | Use dated texts, patronage and inscriptional evidence while separating literary chronology from later origin narratives. |
+| BANK-041-032 | How can Indo-Aryan and Dravidian contact be studied without racial assumptions? | Evaluate specific lexical or grammatical proposals, chronology and alternative explanations. |
+
+## 06. Multilingual lives
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use consented participant accounts, local records and the community's own descriptions. Include differences of position, access and power; testimony is not a representative survey or clinical proof.
+
+**Source types to locate:** consented interviews and oral histories; community publications and local records; field notes with method and consent documented. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-041-033 | Why does a bilingual speaker switch languages within a conversation? | Analyze audience, topic, identity and grammatical patterns through consented examples rather than labeling switching as confusion. |
+| BANK-041-034 | How do schools help establish a standard language? | Trace textbooks, teacher training, examination systems and the treatment of regional varieties. |
+| BANK-041-035 | What changes when a language moves into a diaspora setting? | Compare household transmission, community institutions, new vocabulary and differences across generations. |
+| BANK-041-036 | How does translation reshape a culturally specific concept? | Compare multiple published translations, intended audiences and explanatory choices rather than declaring a single perfect equivalent. |
+
+## 07. Documentation and revitalization
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use consented participant accounts, local records and the community's own descriptions. Include differences of position, access and power; testimony is not a representative survey or clinical proof.
+
+**Source types to locate:** consented interviews and oral histories; community publications and local records; field notes with method and consent documented. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-041-037 | What should a community-controlled language archive preserve? | Examine consent, access, orthography, speakers' priorities and the difference between documentation and public release. |
+| BANK-041-038 | Why is a dictionary insufficient for restoring everyday language use? | Compare family transmission, teaching resources, domains of use and community-defined measures of success. |
+| BANK-041-039 | How can researchers record variation without choosing one speaker as the standard? | Plan sampling across generations, settings and varieties with transparent limitations and participant review. |
+| BANK-041-040 | What does a useful language vitality assessment need beyond speaker counts? | Consider transmission, daily use, institutions, attitudes and the quality of the underlying survey. |
+
+## 08. Language myths and digital tools
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Inspect the actual product version, official documentation, terms and privacy policies. Test claims under stated conditions and distinguish account permissions, data retention, measured functionality and marketing.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official versioned documentation and release notes; dated privacy policies and service terms; reproducible product tests and original validation reports. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-041-041 | Can word counts meaningfully identify the richest language? | Compare dictionary inclusion rules, inflection and specialized vocabulary to explain why simple rankings fail. |
+| BANK-041-042 | How should claims that language determines thought be evaluated? | Separate experimental findings, task effects and stronger deterministic claims without equating correlation with causation. |
+| BANK-041-043 | Why can speech recognition perform unevenly across dialects? | Examine training coverage, evaluation design and real usage conditions without using one benchmark as a universal verdict. |
+| BANK-041-044 | What makes an online etymology convincing or misleading? | Check historical attestations, sound correspondences and borrowing routes against attractive but unsupported word similarities. |
+
+## Coverage limits and next step
+
+This is an expanded topic-bank draft, not exhaustive scholarship or a researched episode package. Each question has an individually written boundary. Source access, claim verification, current angle-scope review and publication approval remain pending. Add new branches and cases when they fill a real gap; no numeric topic cap applies.
+
+Select an exact case, edition, population, jurisdiction or product version, inspect sources and refine the question before using the existing registry idea/depth planning workflow. These BANK IDs are catalog identities, not approved IDEA IDs. Existing Q1-Q42 defaults, production queues and approval gates are unchanged.
