+# 042 Art & Civilization: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 32 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Art & Civilization. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Objects and historical context
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-042-013 | How can an artwork reveal a civilization without representing everyone in it? | Compare patronage, intended audience, survival bias and alternative evidence from the same period. |
+| BANK-042-014 | What changes when an object is studied as ritual equipment rather than decoration? | Investigate original setting, handling, function and later museum presentation through a named object. |
+| BANK-042-015 | How do inscriptions alter the interpretation of an image? | Compare the text's date, placement, language and relationship to the object rather than treating every inscription as a title. |
+| BANK-042-016 | Why can an artwork's current color differ from its original appearance? | Examine pigments, weathering, restoration and analytical evidence while marking speculative reconstructions. |
+
+## 02. South Asian sacred and courtly arts
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-042-017 | How do regional temple sculptures express distinct visual conventions? | Compare selected dated sites, workshop choices and ritual settings without treating all Hindu imagery as interchangeable. |
+| BANK-042-018 | How did Buddhist imagery change across patronage networks? | Trace a bounded group of objects, materials and donors while avoiding a single linear evolution narrative. |
+| BANK-042-019 | What can Jain manuscript painting reveal about its reading communities? | Connect format, illustration, donation records and use rather than isolating images from their texts. |
+| BANK-042-020 | How did Mughal painting workshops combine collaboration and individual attribution? | Compare documented production stages, inscriptions and attribution uncertainty in selected manuscripts. |
+
+## 03. Materials and making
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-042-021 | How does lost-wax casting shape the design of a bronze image? | Explain the process using workshop documentation while distinguishing historical evidence from modern demonstration. |
+| BANK-042-022 | Why do stone types influence sculptural detail and weathering? | Compare material properties, tools, transport and environmental exposure in two documented sites. |
+| BANK-042-023 | What can underdrawing and revisions reveal about a painter's decisions? | Use conservation imaging to distinguish observable changes from uncertain claims about intention. |
+| BANK-042-024 | How did portable pigments connect artists to long-distance trade? | Trace one material's identification, sources and movement without assuming every occurrence came through the same route. |
+
+## 04. Images, power and institutions
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-042-025 | How do royal portraits construct authority beyond physical likeness? | Examine pose, costume, inscriptions, distribution and viewing setting in a specific court. |
+| BANK-042-026 | What can public monuments reveal about competing claims to memory? | Compare commissioning, placement, reception and later reinterpretation through documented viewpoints. |
+| BANK-042-027 | How did religious institutions organize artistic labor? | Investigate funding, workshops, contracts and devotional purposes without assuming one patron controlled every decision. |
+| BANK-042-028 | What happens to an image when a new regime changes its meaning? | Trace alteration, reuse, concealment or destruction with dated evidence rather than a universal conquest narrative. |
+
+## 05. Civilizations in contact
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-042-029 | How can artistic exchange be distinguished from superficial resemblance? | Test chronology, travel, material routes and workshop evidence before proposing direct influence. |
+| BANK-042-030 | What does Gandharan art reveal about multiple visual traditions? | Compare specific objects and scholarly interpretations without assigning every feature to one external origin. |
+| BANK-042-031 | How did Indian Ocean commerce move artistic forms between ports? | Follow selected objects, makers and trading communities while accounting for local adaptation. |
+| BANK-042-032 | How can colonial exhibitions reshape the categories used to describe art? | Examine catalogues, display choices and institutional power alongside the terminology used by makers. |
+
+## 06. Everyday and underrepresented arts
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use consented participant accounts, local records and the community's own descriptions. Include differences of position, access and power; testimony is not a representative survey or clinical proof.
+
+**Source types to locate:** consented interviews and oral histories; community publications and local records; field notes with method and consent documented. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-042-033 | Why do textiles and other fragile arts survive unevenly in historical records? | Compare climate, use, preservation and collecting practices before drawing conclusions about cultural importance. |
+| BANK-042-034 | How should a documentary credit a living folk-art tradition? | Identify practitioners, community variation and permissions rather than using anonymous decorative imagery. |
+| BANK-042-035 | What can household objects reveal that elite commissions leave out? | Compare wear, repair, distribution and archaeological context while avoiding assumptions about owners' identities. |
+| BANK-042-036 | How do women artists become obscured in workshop records? | Investigate attribution practices, family production, training access and surviving named counterexamples. |
+
+## 07. Conservation and ownership
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-042-037 | What does an object's provenance establish and leave uncertain? | Separate documented ownership, gaps, excavation context and unsupported dealer narratives. |
+| BANK-042-038 | How can restoration choices change the story viewers see? | Compare reversibility, surviving material, reconstruction and the treatment's published rationale. |
+| BANK-042-039 | What evidence matters in an artwork repatriation discussion? | Present documented acquisition, community claims, institutional responses and case-specific history without inventing a legal outcome. |
+| BANK-042-040 | How do digital replicas change access while leaving physical questions unresolved? | Compare scale, texture, color accuracy, permissions and the information lost through reproduction. |
+
+## 08. Interpretation and documentary craft
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-042-041 | How can an art film distinguish iconographic identification from interpretation? | Label observable attributes, textual associations and debated meanings separately for one object. |
+| BANK-042-042 | Why can a museum label's date range be more honest than an exact year? | Explain stylistic comparison, inscriptional evidence and scientific dating uncertainty. |
+| BANK-042-043 | How should modern national borders be handled in a film about ancient art? | Map historical regions, present locations and changing identities without projecting current states backward. |
+| BANK-042-044 | What makes a visual comparison persuasive instead of merely striking? | Match scale, date, function and selection criteria while showing relevant differences and counterexamples. |
+
+## Coverage limits and next step
+
+This is an expanded topic-bank draft, not exhaustive scholarship or a researched episode package. Each question has an individually written boundary. Source access, claim verification, current angle-scope review and publication approval remain pending. Add new branches and cases when they fill a real gap; no numeric topic cap applies.
+
+Select an exact case, edition, population, jurisdiction or product version, inspect sources and refine the question before using the existing registry idea/depth planning workflow. These BANK IDs are catalog identities, not approved IDEA IDs. Existing Q1-Q42 defaults, production queues and approval gates are unchanged.
