+# 045 Racing: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 32 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Racing. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Racing disciplines and evidence
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-045-013 | Why do different racing categories reward different engineering choices? | Compare a defined set of regulations, course conditions and performance objectives rather than one universal fastest vehicle. |
+| BANK-045-014 | What does a lap time reveal and conceal about a racing performance? | Account for traffic, tyres, weather, fuel and timing method before comparing laps. |
+| BANK-045-015 | How can race strategy be reconstructed from incomplete public data? | Separate recorded stops and timing from inferred intentions and unavailable team information. |
+| BANK-045-016 | Why do rule changes make cross-era car comparisons difficult? | Track the permitted design, tyres, circuit layout and race format for every comparison. |
+
+## 02. Vehicle dynamics
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-045-017 | How do grip and load transfer influence a racing car through a corner? | Explain conceptual relationships and measured examples without turning the topic into road-driving instructions. |
+| BANK-045-018 | Why can aerodynamic downforce improve cornering while increasing other costs? | Compare drag, balance, sensitivity and circuit demands within a documented ruleset. |
+| BANK-045-019 | How does tyre temperature affect the choices visible during a race? | Use published engineering explanation and event context without inventing private operating figures. |
+| BANK-045-020 | What makes braking performance depend on more than brake size? | Compare tyre grip, load, energy, cooling and regulations using a specific racing category. |
+
+## 03. Powertrains and endurance
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-045-021 | How do endurance regulations change the balance between speed and reliability? | Compare stint length, fuel or energy limits, component life and actual race outcomes. |
+| BANK-045-022 | What engineering tradeoffs distinguish racing hybrid systems? | Explain energy recovery, deployment, mass and regulation without claiming every series uses the same architecture. |
+| BANK-045-023 | Why can a slower qualifying car become competitive over a long race? | Examine consistency, tyre wear, stops and incidents using complete event evidence. |
+| BANK-045-024 | How has endurance racing managed competition between different vehicle classes? | Compare class rules, traffic, classification and the interpretation of overall versus class results. |
+
+## 04. Strategy and race operations
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-045-025 | When can an early pit stop gain track position and when can it lose it? | Compare tyre warm-up, traffic, pit loss and rival response in a documented race scenario. |
+| BANK-045-026 | How do changing weather conditions complicate tyre decisions? | Reconstruct available forecasts, track evolution and decision timing without hindsight certainty. |
+| BANK-045-027 | What can a safety-car period change about an otherwise stable race? | Apply the event's specific rules to gaps, stops and restart order using official records. |
+| BANK-045-028 | How do team orders change the interpretation of a finishing result? | Separate radio evidence, published policy and inferred priorities while including the rules in force. |
+
+## 05. Drivers, riders and teams
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-045-029 | How can a driver's contribution be studied when machinery differs? | Compare teammates, conditions, consistency and qualitative evidence while acknowledging incomplete controls. |
+| BANK-045-030 | What skills distinguish rally competition from circuit racing? | Compare navigation, changing surfaces, stage structure and team roles at a documentary level. |
+| BANK-045-031 | How do motorcycle racers' technical demands differ across racing formats? | Contrast selected disciplines, machine rules and physical task demands without providing risky riding instruction. |
+| BANK-045-032 | What does a mechanic's or engineer's career reveal about racing teamwork? | Trace responsibilities, collaboration and documented decisions rather than attributing a result to one hero. |
+
+## 06. Safety and regulation
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-045-033 | How did a specific racing incident change safety design or procedure? | Use investigation findings, dated reforms and evidence of implementation without graphic spectacle. |
+| BANK-045-034 | Why must a racing safety device be assessed as part of a larger system? | Compare testing, installation, vehicle compatibility and documented limitations. |
+| BANK-045-035 | How do officials distinguish a sporting penalty from a technical disqualification? | Read the relevant regulations and published decision for one case without generalizing across series. |
+| BANK-045-036 | What does track modification reveal about evolving racing priorities? | Compare layouts, speed, spectator access and safety evidence across dated versions of a venue. |
+
+## 07. Business and motorsport culture
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use current official terms, filings or macroeconomic data; state jurisdiction, date, fees, taxes, risk, units and assumptions. Use transparent illustrative calculations rather than personal recommendations or return promises.
+
+**Source types to locate:** regulator or official statistical publications; actual contracts, fee schedules and audited filings; original economic studies and disclosed calculation inputs. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-045-037 | How does sponsorship shape a racing team's ability to compete? | Compare revenue arrangements, costs and dependence using available records without guessing confidential contract terms. |
+| BANK-045-038 | Why can a manufacturer enter a racing series and later withdraw? | Trace public objectives, regulation, spending and organizational decisions while separating statements from inference. |
+| BANK-045-039 | How did broadcasting transform a racing championship's audience? | Compare coverage, scheduling, access and measured audience changes within a defined period. |
+| BANK-045-040 | What can regional racing histories add to a championship-centered narrative? | Recover local circuits, participants and institutions rather than treating international visibility as the only significance. |
+
+## 08. Data and disputed narratives
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Inspect the actual product version, official documentation, terms and privacy policies. Test claims under stated conditions and distinguish account permissions, data retention, measured functionality and marketing.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official versioned documentation and release notes; dated privacy policies and service terms; reproducible product tests and original validation reports. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-045-041 | How should a racing documentary visualize telemetry it cannot directly obtain? | Use public timing accurately, label illustrative data and avoid fabricating cockpit or engineering traces. |
+| BANK-045-042 | What makes a claimed circuit record comparable across sessions? | Specify layout, vehicle class, official status, conditions and timing authority. |
+| BANK-045-043 | How can a controversial collision be explained without assuming intent? | Compare synchronized views, official findings and uncertainty about driver perception. |
+| BANK-045-044 | Why do simulation results differ from what happens on race day? | Examine model assumptions, calibration and changing conditions while treating simulations as conditional evidence. |
+
+## Coverage limits and next step
+
+This is an expanded topic-bank draft, not exhaustive scholarship or a researched episode package. Each question has an individually written boundary. Source access, claim verification, current angle-scope review and publication approval remain pending. Add new branches and cases when they fill a real gap; no numeric topic cap applies.
+
+Select an exact case, edition, population, jurisdiction or product version, inspect sources and refine the question before using the existing registry idea/depth planning workflow. These BANK IDs are catalog identities, not approved IDEA IDs. Existing Q1-Q42 defaults, production queues and approval gates are unchanged.
+
