+# 046 Disaster & Survival: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 32 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Disaster & Survival. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Risk and event reconstruction
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-046-013 | How does a natural hazard become a human disaster? | Compare exposure, vulnerability, infrastructure and governance rather than attributing all losses to physical magnitude. |
+| BANK-046-014 | What makes an event timeline reliable when early reports conflict? | Separate timestamps, eyewitness estimates, instrument records and later corrected accounts. |
+| BANK-046-015 | Why do disasters with similar physical measurements cause different losses? | Compare settlement, building conditions, warnings and reporting completeness using bounded cases. |
+| BANK-046-016 | How should a documentary distinguish a trigger from underlying causes? | Build a sourced causal sequence that separates immediate events, accumulated vulnerabilities and unsupported blame. |
+
+## 02. Earthquakes and geologic hazards
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-046-017 | What do magnitude and local shaking describe differently? | Compare source energy measures, ground motion and site effects using authoritative event records. |
+| BANK-046-018 | How can liquefaction change the damage pattern of an earthquake? | Explain soil conditions and documented observations without diagnosing a property or providing engineering advice. |
+| BANK-046-019 | Why do some volcanic crises unfold through multiple hazards? | Trace an identified eruption's sequence and monitoring evidence rather than presenting one universal eruption pattern. |
+| BANK-046-020 | How can landslide histories be reconstructed after the landscape changes? | Compare terrain surveys, rainfall records, satellite images and eyewitness evidence with explicit gaps. |
+
+## 03. Water and weather disasters
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-046-021 | Why can flooding occur far from where the heaviest rain falls? | Trace a specific catchment's runoff, river timing and urban conditions using official hydrological records. |
+| BANK-046-022 | How do storm surge and rainfall flooding create different problems? | Separate coastal water movement, drainage and event timing without offering real-time evacuation instructions. |
+| BANK-046-023 | What can past droughts reveal about water-system vulnerability? | Compare climate indicators, demand, storage and social effects without treating rainfall alone as a complete explanation. |
+| BANK-046-024 | How did a tsunami's source and coastline shape its local impacts? | Use scientific reconstruction to distinguish wave propagation, local topography and documented observations. |
+
+## 04. Infrastructure and industrial failures
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-046-025 | How does a failure investigation separate a broken component from a failed system? | Compare design, maintenance, operation and organizational factors through an official case report. |
+| BANK-046-026 | What can a blackout reveal about interconnected infrastructure? | Trace a documented cascade, protection actions and recovery sequence without providing exploitable operational details. |
+| BANK-046-027 | Why can warnings inside an organization fail to produce action? | Use records and inquiry findings to examine communication, authority and incentives rather than speculation about motives. |
+| BANK-046-028 | How should a disaster film depict uncertainty in an unfinished investigation? | Date every finding, distinguish provisional statements and provide a clear boundary between evidence and hypothesis. |
+
+## 05. Warning and response systems
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-046-029 | What makes an early warning useful beyond detecting a hazard? | Compare reach, clarity, trust, accessibility and practical capacity to respond in a documented event. |
+| BANK-046-030 | How can evacuation records reveal unequal access to safety? | Study transport, disability access, housing and institutional planning through official findings and affected people's accounts. |
+| BANK-046-031 | Why can two agencies describe the same emergency differently? | Compare mandates, information available at each time and later reconciliation rather than assuming deliberate deception. |
+| BANK-046-032 | How are spontaneous volunteers incorporated into an organized response? | Investigate a documented coordination system, role boundaries and lessons without directing readers into active disaster zones. |
+
+## 06. Survivor-centered storytelling
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use consented participant accounts, local records and the community's own descriptions. Include differences of position, access and power; testimony is not a representative survey or clinical proof.
+
+**Source types to locate:** consented interviews and oral histories; community publications and local records; field notes with method and consent documented. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-046-033 | How can a survival story preserve the survivor's agency? | Use consented testimony, corroborated chronology and the person's chosen framing while avoiding sensational reenactment. |
+| BANK-046-034 | What does survivor bias obscure in celebrated escape stories? | Compare the surviving accounts with broader exposure and loss data without assigning moral superiority to survival. |
+| BANK-046-035 | How can a documentary discuss missing people without turning uncertainty into a mystery gimmick? | Use verified records, family preferences and clearly bounded unresolved questions. |
+| BANK-046-036 | What can community networks explain about recovery after a disaster? | Compare mutual aid, local institutions and uneven access to assistance without replacing policy analysis with individual heroism. |
+
+## 07. Recovery and public memory
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-046-037 | Why can rebuilding restore structures while leaving livelihoods disrupted? | Compare housing, employment, schooling and service access over a defined recovery period. |
+| BANK-046-038 | How do official death tolls change after a disaster? | Explain definitions, direct and indirect effects, registration gaps and revision methods without treating every discrepancy as concealment. |
+| BANK-046-039 | What makes a memorial represent some experiences more visibly than others? | Examine consultation, design, names and excluded perspectives through documented debate. |
+| BANK-046-040 | How should anniversary coverage assess whether lessons were implemented? | Compare inquiry recommendations, published follow-up and actual changes rather than repeating promises as outcomes. |
+
+## 08. Evidence, ethics and preparedness claims
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-046-041 | How can reconstructed disaster scenes be labeled without misleading viewers? | Separate archival footage, simulation, reenactment and illustrative material at the moment each appears. |
+| BANK-046-042 | Why should a historical survival technique not be presented as universal advice? | Explain event-specific circumstances and limits while directing any practical guidance topic to current authoritative sources. |
+| BANK-046-043 | How can satellite imagery help quantify disaster damage without identifying every loss? | Compare resolution, cloud cover, classification and ground verification in a documented assessment. |
+| BANK-046-044 | What makes a disaster comparison fair across countries and decades? | Align population exposure, definitions, inflation, reporting quality and uncertainty before ranking impacts. |
+
+## Coverage limits and next step
+
+This is an expanded topic-bank draft, not exhaustive scholarship or a researched episode package. Each question has an individually written boundary. Source access, claim verification, current angle-scope review and publication approval remain pending. Add new branches and cases when they fill a real gap; no numeric topic cap applies.
+
+Select an exact case, edition, population, jurisdiction or product version, inspect sources and refine the question before using the existing registry idea/depth planning workflow. These BANK IDs are catalog identities, not approved IDEA IDs. Existing Q1-Q42 defaults, production queues and approval gates are unchanged.
+
