+| BANK-047-014 | Why can aircraft with very different wing shapes all meet useful design goals? | Compare speed, load, range, handling requirements and documented design choices. |
+| BANK-047-015 | What does an aircraft's published range assume about its mission? | Examine payload, fuel reserves, conditions and stated test assumptions rather than treating range as a fixed guarantee. |
+| BANK-047-016 | How should an aviation documentary distinguish a prototype from an operational aircraft? | Trace testing, certification, production and actual service with dated records. |
+
+## 02. Design and propulsion histories
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-047-017 | How did the transition from piston engines to jets change airline operations? | Compare selected routes, maintenance, economics and airport requirements without assuming immediate universal replacement. |
+| BANK-047-018 | Why do propellers remain useful for some modern aircraft missions? | Compare operating speed, field requirements and mission economics rather than ranking propulsion by apparent age. |
+| BANK-047-019 | What engineering tradeoffs shaped early supersonic passenger aircraft? | Discuss materials, propulsion, noise, fuel and route constraints using documented programme history. |
+| BANK-047-020 | How did composite structures change aircraft design and inspection? | Compare material behavior, manufacturing and maintenance practice through official engineering sources. |
+
+## 03. Navigation and air traffic systems
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-047-021 | How did pilots navigate long routes before satellite navigation? | Compare historical radio, celestial and inertial methods at a descriptive level with period-specific limitations. |
+| BANK-047-022 | What does air traffic control coordinate beyond telling aircraft where to turn? | Explain sequencing, separation, airspace responsibilities and information exchange without providing operational instructions. |
+| BANK-047-023 | How do instrument procedures help manage low-visibility operations? | Describe the role of published procedures, equipment and qualifications without teaching their execution. |
+| BANK-047-024 | Why can an airport's runway capacity differ from its number of runways? | Compare geometry, weather, traffic mix and airspace constraints in a documented airport study. |
+
+## 04. Safety investigation
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-047-025 | What is the difference between an aviation safety investigation and a criminal proceeding? | Compare the mandates and published findings of a named case while avoiding assumptions across jurisdictions. |
+| BANK-047-026 | How can flight recorders and wreckage support different parts of an investigation? | Explain evidence correlation, limitations and chain of custody without treating any single recording as complete context. |
+| BANK-047-027 | Why do aviation investigations examine organizational conditions as well as crew actions? | Trace a published report's causal analysis, training, design and oversight findings. |
+| BANK-047-028 | How should a film handle an aviation event before the final report exists? | Date information, separate confirmed facts from speculation and avoid presenting dramatic reconstructions as evidence. |
+
+## 05. Human factors and professional work
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use consented participant accounts, local records and the community's own descriptions. Include differences of position, access and power; testimony is not a representative survey or clinical proof.
+
+**Source types to locate:** consented interviews and oral histories; community publications and local records; field notes with method and consent documented. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-047-029 | How did crew resource management change ideas about cockpit authority? | Trace training history, communication research and documented implementation without reducing safety to a catchphrase. |
+| BANK-047-030 | What does an aircraft maintenance record reveal about professional decision-making? | Discuss documented inspections, responsibilities and recordkeeping without diagnosing an aircraft from isolated entries. |
+| BANK-047-031 | How do cabin crews contribute to aviation safety beyond passenger service? | Describe training responsibilities and published case evidence without issuing emergency instructions. |
+| BANK-047-032 | Why does fatigue management involve scheduling systems as well as individual sleep? | Compare institutional research and roster constraints at a general level without medical advice or worker blame. |
+
+## 06. Airlines and infrastructure
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use current official terms, filings or macroeconomic data; state jurisdiction, date, fees, taxes, risk, units and assumptions. Use transparent illustrative calculations rather than personal recommendations or return promises.
+
+**Source types to locate:** regulator or official statistical publications; actual contracts, fee schedules and audited filings; original economic studies and disclosed calculation inputs. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-047-033 | Why can a full aircraft operate on an unprofitable route? | Compare yield, costs, connections and accounting boundaries using dated public evidence. |
+| BANK-047-034 | How did hub-and-spoke networks change passenger journeys and airline planning? | Compare connections, frequency, congestion and alternative network strategies within a defined market. |
+| BANK-047-035 | What happens when an airport outgrows its original location? | Trace expansion choices, environmental issues, access and community perspectives through a documented case. |
+| BANK-047-036 | How can aircraft leasing separate an airline's fleet from its asset ownership? | Explain the business model with public records while avoiding investment recommendations. |
+
+## 07. Aviation's wider histories
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
