+# 049 Pirates & Outlaws: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 32 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Pirates & Outlaws. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Categories and historical sources
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-049-013 | Who had the power to label someone a pirate rather than a privateer? | Compare commissions, jurisdiction, wartime status and contemporary disputes within a named case. |
+| BANK-049-014 | How do trial records shape what survives about historical outlaws? | Examine questioning, translation, institutional motives and missing testimony rather than treating transcripts as neutral biography. |
+| BANK-049-015 | Why do outlaw legends grow after the person's death? | Trace dated ballads, print, film and political reuse separately from contemporary evidence. |
+| BANK-049-016 | How should a documentary distinguish documented violence from a romantic reputation? | Compare victims' accounts, court findings and later storytelling without using charisma as exoneration. |
+
+## 02. Piracy in different seas
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-049-017 | How did Caribbean piracy depend on imperial competition and trade routes? | Bound the period, compare institutions and avoid presenting all seaborne raiding as one timeless culture. |
+| BANK-049-018 | What does piracy in the Indian Ocean reveal about overlapping jurisdictions? | Use a specific voyage or prosecution to examine merchant interests, rulers and contested authority. |
+| BANK-049-019 | How have historians reassessed maritime raiders on the Malabar coast? | Compare regional archives, rival labels and political context without repeating colonial terminology as settled description. |
+| BANK-049-020 | What can East Asian maritime raiding reveal about trade restrictions? | Investigate a bounded network of participants, policy and commerce while separating heterogeneous groups from a single ethnic label. |
+
+## 03. Life and organization aboard raiding vessels
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use consented participant accounts, local records and the community's own descriptions. Include differences of position, access and power; testimony is not a representative survey or clinical proof.
+
+**Source types to locate:** consented interviews and oral histories; community publications and local records; field notes with method and consent documented. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-049-021 | What evidence supports claims about pirate agreements and crew governance? | Compare surviving articles, testimony and later generalization rather than assuming every ship used identical rules. |
+| BANK-049-022 | How did raiding crews recruit and retain members? | Examine documented coercion, voluntary entry, labor conditions and uncertainty in individual accounts. |
+| BANK-049-023 | What did pirates do with captured cargo beyond searching for gold? | Trace resale markets, supplies and documented goods without offering modern criminal methods. |
+| BANK-049-024 | How did injury and compensation appear in surviving pirate records? | Study historical arrangements and the quality of their evidence without romanticizing dangerous labor. |
+
+## 04. Named lives and contested reputations
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-049-025 | How did Henry Every's raid become an international diplomatic crisis? | Compare documented commercial consequences, prosecutions and later legends while avoiding invented treasure conclusions. |
+| BANK-049-026 | What can Ching Shih's career reveal about maritime coalitions and negotiation? | Use dated sources to distinguish documented organization from modern embellishment. |
+| BANK-049-027 | How should Kanhoji Angre's maritime power be described across rival archives? | Compare Maratha administration, European commercial interests and contested labels in their historical setting. |
+| BANK-049-028 | What do the surviving records establish about Anne Bonny and Mary Read? | Separate trial documentation, early published narratives and unsupported later biographical detail. |
+
+## 05. Land outlaws and social conditions
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-049-029 | When does the idea of a social bandit illuminate history and when does it romanticize it? | Compare scholarly models with a documented local case, including coercion and victims' experiences. |
+| BANK-049-030 | How did frontier institutions influence the careers of American outlaws? | Examine transport, policing, land and commerce through a bounded biography rather than a lawless-west stereotype. |
+| BANK-049-031 | How did colonial records describe resistance as criminality? | Compare administrative categories with community accounts and specific actions without assuming every accusation was fabricated. |
+| BANK-049-032 | What can rural banditry reveal about protection and state authority? | Investigate documented relationships among villagers, elites, police and armed groups without providing criminal guidance. |
+
+## 06. Policing, trials and punishment
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-049-033 | How did public trials turn pirates into cautionary spectacles? | Compare legal records, published accounts and intended audiences while distinguishing law from entertainment. |
+| BANK-049-034 | What made an amnesty attractive or unreliable for historical outlaws? | Trace the terms, implementation and individual outcomes of a named policy rather than generalizing across rulers. |
+| BANK-049-035 | How did new transport and communication systems affect the pursuit of outlaws? | Use documented institutional changes without explaining contemporary evasion tactics. |
+| BANK-049-036 | Why can a historical conviction require interpretation rather than a simple modern retelling? | Explain the procedure, evidence standards and context while keeping findings and later criticism distinct. |
+
+## 07. Popular culture and memory
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-049-037 | Where did the familiar visual costume of a pirate come from? | Trace dated illustrations, theatre and film against surviving descriptions without treating costume conventions as documentary evidence. |
+| BANK-049-038 | How did ballads transform a regional outlaw into a folk hero? | Compare versions, performance settings and audiences alongside the historical record. |
+| BANK-049-039 | Why do hidden-treasure stories attach to famous fugitives? | Follow the earliest documented claim and later retellings without encouraging unauthorized searching. |
+| BANK-049-040 | How can an adaptation acknowledge folklore while retaining its dramatic power? | Label invented scenes, uncertain episodes and documented events clearly within a narrative plan. |
+
+## 08. Research ethics and comparison
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-049-041 | How should an outlaw biography include people harmed by the protagonist? | Find recoverable victims' perspectives, distinguish verified losses and avoid treating unnamed people as scenery. |
+| BANK-049-042 | What makes comparisons between historical piracy and modern maritime crime misleading? | Separate institutions, technology, jurisdiction and economic conditions rather than assuming direct continuity. |
+| BANK-049-043 | How can missing archival records produce false confidence about a famous disappearance? | Compare search coverage, survival gaps and plausible limits without filling absence with conspiracy. |
+| BANK-049-044 | What should a responsible pirate documentary say when two primary accounts conflict? | Present provenance, incentives, chronology and unresolved disagreement instead of choosing the more cinematic version. |
+
+## Coverage limits and next step
+
+This is an expanded topic-bank draft, not exhaustive scholarship or a researched episode package. Each question has an individually written boundary. Source access, claim verification, current angle-scope review and publication approval remain pending. Add new branches and cases when they fill a real gap; no numeric topic cap applies.
+
+Select an exact case, edition, population, jurisdiction or product version, inspect sources and refine the question before using the existing registry idea/depth planning workflow. These BANK IDs are catalog identities, not approved IDEA IDs. Existing Q1-Q42 defaults, production queues and approval gates are unchanged.
