+# 050 Treasure Hunting: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 32 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Treasure Hunting. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Treasure claims and evidence
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-050-013 | What counts as a treasure claim that can be investigated historically? | Identify the alleged object, date, location claim and earliest traceable source before discussing later speculation. |
+| BANK-050-014 | How can a repeated treasure story appear well-sourced when every version shares one origin? | Map publication dependence, missing citations and changes in wording rather than counting repetitions as corroboration. |
+| BANK-050-015 | What makes a historical inventory different from evidence that valuables remain hidden? | Separate documented possession, later movement, loss and unsupported assumptions about survival. |
+| BANK-050-016 | How should a documentary distinguish discovery, identification and valuation? | Treat each as a separate claim supported by different evidence and explicitly dated estimates. |
+
+## 02. Archives, maps and oral accounts
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-050-017 | How can an old map be tested for its original purpose and date? | Compare paper, provenance, cartographic conventions and archival context without giving unauthorized search directions. |
+| BANK-050-018 | What can land and estate records reveal about a treasure legend? | Trace ownership, building changes and documented transfers while respecting access restrictions. |
+| BANK-050-019 | How do oral traditions preserve meaning even when they do not establish a find location? | Present speakers' context, variation and cultural significance without treating testimony as a coordinate puzzle. |
+| BANK-050-020 | Why can a translated place name send a treasure narrative in the wrong direction? | Compare historical spelling, language change and multiple candidate locations with uncertainty clearly mapped. |
+
+## 03. Archaeological hoards
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-050-021 | What can coin dates establish about when a hoard was buried? | Explain earliest possible deposition, circulation and missing evidence without equating the newest coin with an exact burial date. |
+| BANK-050-022 | How do containers and arrangement help interpret a hoard? | Compare excavation context, wear, grouping and alternative explanations for deposition. |
+| BANK-050-023 | Why can a small archaeological find matter more than its market price? | Examine dating, trade, diet or manufacturing evidence while separating research significance from monetary value. |
+| BANK-050-024 | How can a hoard reflect repeated deposits rather than one emergency? | Compare stratigraphy, composition and documented site history instead of assuming every cache was hidden during invasion. |
+
+## 04. Shipwreck cargo and recovery stories
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-050-025 | How can a documented cargo be confused with what actually reached a wreck site? | Compare manifests, loading changes, salvage records and post-loss dispersal with the limits of each source. |
+| BANK-050-026 | What does an earlier salvage operation change about a later treasure claim? | Trace recovery reports, uncertainty and remaining context without presuming a wreck is untouched. |
+| BANK-050-027 | How should competing claims to a recovered wreck cargo be presented? | Use case-specific ownership histories and published decisions without offering legal advice or declaring discovery to be title. |
+| BANK-050-028 | Why can underwater footage make a cargo identification seem more certain than it is? | Separate visual appearance, measured artifacts, laboratory identification and securely documented context. |
+
+## 05. Lost objects and famous legends
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-050-029 | How did the Amber Room's documented wartime movement become mixed with later speculation? | Build a verified timeline and distinguish physical evidence from recurring unsupported location claims. |
+| BANK-050-030 | What would a responsible investigation of a lost royal jewel require? | Trace inventories, descriptions, recutting, transfers and uncertain identifications rather than relying on visual similarity. |
+| BANK-050-031 | How should pirate-treasure legends be compared with documented pirate loot? | Review the earliest sources and cargo evidence without assuming buried chests were standard practice. |
+| BANK-050-032 | What can a missing art collection's catalogue reveal about its possible dispersal? | Compare item-level descriptions, provenance gaps and confirmed returns while avoiding accusations against unverified holders. |
+
+## 06. Hoaxes, forgery and incentives
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-050-033 | How can a supposed treasure document be examined without sensational conclusions? | Compare provenance, materials, language and independent expert analysis while separating suspicious features from a proven forgery. |
+| BANK-050-034 | Why do treasure claims attract investment before physical evidence appears? | Examine promotional incentives, selective disclosure and dated outcomes without advising speculation. |
+| BANK-050-035 | What happens when a television search becomes part of the evidence people cite? | Trace original claims, staged reconstructions and later circular references to the programme itself. |
+| BANK-050-036 | How should an investigator correct a treasure claim after new evidence contradicts it? | Preserve the original wording, show the new evidence and explain the revised conclusion without quietly rewriting the timeline. |
+
+## 07. Heritage, communities and permission
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-050-037 | Why can removing an object destroy information even when the object survives? | Explain archaeological context and professional documentation rather than providing digging or detecting instructions. |
+| BANK-050-038 | How should sacred or funerary objects change a treasure film's framing? | Center documented community perspectives, cultural meaning and appropriate research boundaries instead of market spectacle. |
+| BANK-050-039 | What must a filming plan establish before entering a historic site? | Use site-specific authorization, conservation requirements and local consultation without assuming public visibility permits access. |
+| BANK-050-040 | How can a treasure story acknowledge displaced owners and wartime looting? | Trace documented dispossession, restitution efforts and unresolved gaps with care for affected families. |
+
+## 08. Measurement and responsible conclusions
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-050-041 | What can non-invasive survey methods detect and leave ambiguous? | Compare published instrument capabilities, interpretation limits and professional ground verification without planning unauthorized searches. |
+| BANK-050-042 | Why does an estimated treasure value change across reports? | Separate metal content, provenance, rarity, realized sales, currency date and speculative headline figures. |
+| BANK-050-043 | What evidence would justify concluding that a treasure claim remains unproven? | Specify the claim, searches actually documented, evidence quality and why absence of a find does not establish a conspiracy. |
+| BANK-050-044 | How can a treasure documentary end meaningfully without a discovery? | Resolve the historical questions that evidence supports, identify genuine uncertainties and explain the cultural or archival findings. |
+
+## Coverage limits and next step
+
+This is an expanded topic-bank draft, not exhaustive scholarship or a researched episode package. Each question has an individually written boundary. Source access, claim verification, current angle-scope review and publication approval remain pending. Add new branches and cases when they fill a real gap; no numeric topic cap applies.
+
+Select an exact case, edition, population, jurisdiction or product version, inspect sources and refine the question before using the existing registry idea/depth planning workflow. These BANK IDs are catalog identities, not approved IDEA IDs. Existing Q1-Q42 defaults, production queues and approval gates are unchanged.
+
