+# 052 Architecture: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 32 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Architecture. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Reading buildings
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-052-013 | How can a building be studied as evidence of daily life? | Compare plans, circulation, use, alterations and occupants' accounts rather than treating the facade as the whole story. |
+| BANK-052-014 | What is the difference between a building's style and its structural system? | Use paired examples to separate visual conventions, load paths and construction materials. |
+| BANK-052-015 | Why can an architect's original drawing differ from the completed building? | Trace revisions, budget, contractors, regulation and site discoveries through a documented project. |
+| BANK-052-016 | How do buildings communicate status without explicit inscriptions? | Examine scale, access, materials, siting and audience while marking interpretation rather than assuming intention. |
+
+## 02. South Asian architectural histories
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-052-017 | How do regional temple plans organize movement and ritual differently? | Compare identified sites, dates and lived use without collapsing diverse traditions into one diagram. |
+| BANK-052-018 | What does an Indo-Islamic building reveal about workshops and local materials? | Trace construction evidence, patronage and regional practice rather than assigning every feature to one civilizational source. |
+| BANK-052-019 | How did stepwells combine water access with spatial design? | Compare seasonal function, geology, patronage and social access through selected documented examples. |
+| BANK-052-020 | How did colonial planning interact with existing Indian urban settlements? | Compare a specific city's land, infrastructure and administrative decisions with residents' perspectives. |
+
+## 03. Structure, material and construction
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-052-021 | Why can masonry arches, vaults and domes require different forms of support? | Explain load paths and construction conditions using documented examples rather than visual resemblance alone. |
+| BANK-052-022 | How did reinforced concrete expand architectural possibilities and maintenance obligations? | Compare spanning, formwork, reinforcement and deterioration evidence at a general technical level. |
+| BANK-052-023 | What can a timber building reveal about joinery and local resources? | Connect structural choices, craft, climate and maintenance through an identified tradition. |
+| BANK-052-024 | How does a curtain wall differ from a load-bearing exterior wall? | Compare structural roles, enclosure performance and connection requirements without reducing facade design to appearance. |
+
+## 04. Climate and environmental performance
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-052-025 | How do courtyards influence light, airflow and social space? | Compare measured performance, geometry, climate and actual occupation rather than assuming all courtyards cool buildings equally. |
+| BANK-052-026 | When can passive design reduce dependence on mechanical conditioning? | State climate, occupancy, comfort criteria and limitations using a documented building study. |
+| BANK-052-027 | Why does a highly glazed building require careful energy analysis? | Compare solar gain, daylight, orientation, shading and mechanical systems within explicit assumptions. |
+| BANK-052-028 | How should embodied and operational carbon be compared in a building project? | Define lifecycle stages, service life, energy scenarios and uncertainty without treating one metric as a complete verdict. |
+
+## 05. Housing and public life
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use consented participant accounts, local records and the community's own descriptions. Include differences of position, access and power; testimony is not a representative survey or clinical proof.
+
+**Source types to locate:** consented interviews and oral histories; community publications and local records; field notes with method and consent documented. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-052-029 | How does a housing layout affect privacy, care and shared space? | Compare actual household use, adaptable rooms and circulation without declaring one family model universal. |
+| BANK-052-030 | What does the history of social housing reveal about policy and maintenance? | Study a named programme's design, finance, allocation and resident experience over time. |
+| BANK-052-031 | How can public-building entrances include or exclude different users? | Investigate accessibility, security, signage and actual routes using documented user perspectives. |
+| BANK-052-032 | Why do informal settlements require more than a visual architectural reading? | Examine tenure, services, livelihoods and resident organization without aestheticizing insecure housing. |
+
+## 06. Cities, landscape and institutions
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-052-033 | How did zoning change the form of a particular city? | Trace dated rules, development patterns, exceptions and effects without presenting current legal advice. |
+| BANK-052-034 | What happens when a landmark building becomes a tool of urban branding? | Compare commissioning goals, financing, local access and observed outcomes rather than tourist images alone. |
+| BANK-052-035 | How do transport networks shape architectural density? | Examine station access, land value, planning and actual travel patterns through a bounded urban case. |
+| BANK-052-036 | What can a designed landscape reveal about control of land and water? | Compare ownership, maintenance, ecology and public access rather than treating landscape as decorative background. |
+
+## 07. Adaptation and conservation
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-052-037 | How can an old building gain a new use without losing its important fabric? | Compare significance assessment, access, structural constraints and documented conservation decisions. |
+| BANK-052-038 | Why do successive extensions matter to a building's history? | Read joints, plans and records as evidence of changing needs instead of treating later work only as damage. |
+| BANK-052-039 | How do conservators distinguish repair from conjectural reconstruction? | Use documented material, reversibility and uncertainty in a named project without claiming one rule resolves every case. |
+| BANK-052-040 | What can demolition debates reveal about whose heritage is recognized? | Present occupants, authorities, researchers and affected communities through verified case-specific records. |
+
+## 08. Criticism and visual storytelling
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
