+# 053 Future Civilization: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 32 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Future Civilization. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Building disciplined scenarios
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Declare a conditional scenario, not a prediction. Anchor present-day capabilities in evidence, make physical and institutional assumptions explicit, and trace second-order consequences and failure conditions.
+
+**Source types to locate:** current original research on the enabling technologies; physical and economic constraints with explicit inputs; published scenario methods and relevant legal frameworks. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-053-013 | How can a future-civilization scenario be distinguished from a prediction? | State assumptions, time horizon, uncertainty and alternative pathways rather than presenting one imagined world as inevitable. |
+| BANK-053-014 | What limits should a civilization model include before adding spectacular technology? | Define energy, materials, land, institutions and maintenance as explicit constraints. |
+| BANK-053-015 | Why do technological breakthroughs spread unevenly through societies? | Compare affordability, infrastructure, skills and political choices using historical analogies with their limits labeled. |
+| BANK-053-016 | How can a future-history timeline show branching possibilities rather than false precision? | Use conditional milestones and dependencies without inventing exact dates for uncertain discoveries. |
+
+## 02. Energy and material foundations
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-053-017 | What would abundant low-carbon energy change and leave constrained? | Explore a stated scenario's industrial possibilities alongside material, land, ecological and governance limits. |
+| BANK-053-018 | How might a civilization organize repair when products last much longer? | Compare design, labor, ownership and resource-flow assumptions in alternative institutional models. |
+| BANK-053-019 | What can circular-economy scenarios realistically recover from waste streams? | Distinguish collection, material losses, quality and energy requirements rather than assuming perfect recycling. |
+| BANK-053-020 | How would energy storage choices shape a settlement's resilience? | Compare duration, demand and system assumptions conceptually without presenting an unverified engineering design. |
+
+## 03. Cities, food and ecological limits
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Declare a conditional scenario, not a prediction. Anchor present-day capabilities in evidence, make physical and institutional assumptions explicit, and trace second-order consequences and failure conditions.
+
+**Source types to locate:** current original research on the enabling technologies; physical and economic constraints with explicit inputs; published scenario methods and relevant legal frameworks. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-053-021 | How might a future city respond to extreme heat under different resource conditions? | Compare shaded public space, housing, infrastructure and unequal access through clearly labeled scenarios. |
+| BANK-053-022 | What would widespread controlled-environment agriculture change about food systems? | Examine energy, crops, water, labor and distribution without treating indoor farming as a complete substitute for agriculture. |
+| BANK-053-023 | How could coastal communities choose among protection, adaptation and relocation? | Explore conditional pathways, community priorities and long-term maintenance rather than prescribing a decision for a real location. |
+| BANK-053-024 | What would a civilization organized around ecosystem recovery have to measure? | Compare biodiversity, livelihoods, land rights and timescales with explicit tradeoffs and uncertain feedbacks. |
+
+## 04. Institutions and collective decisions
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-053-025 | How might very large societies coordinate decisions without sharing one government? | Compare federations, networks and treaty-like arrangements as models rather than endorsing a single political blueprint. |
+| BANK-053-026 | What would intergenerational representation change in public planning? | Explore proposed institutions, accountability and conflicts between present needs and uncertain future claims. |
+| BANK-053-027 | How could future societies preserve meaningful consent in automated administration? | Compare appeal, explanation, participation and the ability to contest decisions in a hypothetical service system. |
+| BANK-053-028 | Why does a technically efficient civilization still need legitimate institutions? | Examine allocation, conflict, trust and value disagreement rather than assuming optimization removes politics. |
+
+## 05. Work, learning and culture
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use consented participant accounts, local records and the community's own descriptions. Include differences of position, access and power; testimony is not a representative survey or clinical proof.
+
+**Source types to locate:** consented interviews and oral histories; community publications and local records; field notes with method and consent documented. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-053-029 | How might work change if many routine tasks become automated? | Construct multiple employment and ownership scenarios without treating job disappearance or universal abundance as established outcomes. |
+| BANK-053-030 | What would lifelong education require beyond access to information? | Explore mentorship, time, assessment, inclusion and institutions under clearly stated social assumptions. |
+| BANK-053-031 | How could cultural diversity persist in a highly connected civilization? | Compare local institutions, language transmission, platform incentives and unequal influence without predicting cultural uniformity. |
+| BANK-053-032 | What happens to expertise when decision-support systems become widespread? | Examine accountability, verification, apprenticeship and skill retention in a hypothetical professional setting. |
+
+## 06. Space settlements and long horizons
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-053-033 | What would make an off-world settlement dependent on Earth despite local production? | Map assumed supply chains, specialist skills, spare parts and failure recovery without claiming demonstrated self-sufficiency. |
+| BANK-053-034 | How might communication delays affect the governance of distant settlements? | Compare local autonomy, delegation and disputes under physically bounded communication assumptions. |
+| BANK-053-035 | What constraints distinguish a rotating space habitat from an artistic rendering? | Identify radiation, atmosphere, rotation, materials and maintenance questions without implying an engineering-qualified design. |
+| BANK-053-036 | How should civilization-scale energy classifications be used cautiously? | Explain what energy consumption metrics capture and omit about wellbeing, knowledge and political organization. |
+
+## 07. Resilience, decline and continuity
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Declare a conditional scenario, not a prediction. Anchor present-day capabilities in evidence, make physical and institutional assumptions explicit, and trace second-order consequences and failure conditions.
+
+**Source types to locate:** current original research on the enabling technologies; physical and economic constraints with explicit inputs; published scenario methods and relevant legal frameworks. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-053-037 | How can redundancy protect a civilization while increasing everyday costs? | Compare backup capacity, diversity and maintenance across explicit hypothetical systems. |
+| BANK-053-038 | What would a civilization need to preserve knowledge through a long disruption? | Explore media durability, language, practical training and institutional continuity rather than a single indestructible archive. |
+| BANK-053-039 | Why might a highly connected civilization be vulnerable to cascading failures? | Map bounded dependencies and recovery options conceptually without exposing actionable weaknesses in real infrastructure. |
+| BANK-053-040 | How can a future scenario include decline without assuming total collapse? | Distinguish regional contraction, institutional change, population shifts and continuity in multiple conditional paths. |
+
+## 08. Evaluating visions of the future
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-053-041 | How do utopian and dystopian stories choose which people remain visible? | Compare whose labor, needs and agency appear within named fictional scenarios rather than treating fiction as prediction. |
+| BANK-053-042 | What can historical failed forecasts teach a future-civilization documentary? | Examine their assumptions, missing institutions and unexpected developments without claiming hindsight makes forecasting easy. |
+| BANK-053-043 | How should speculative technologies be labeled when only some components exist? | Separate demonstrated capability, laboratory work, theoretical possibility and pure invention throughout the presentation. |
+| BANK-053-044 | What would count as evidence that a scenario's assumptions are becoming less plausible? | Specify observable indicators, alternative explanations and revision triggers without converting them into guaranteed forecasts. |
+
+## Coverage limits and next step
+
+This is an expanded topic-bank draft, not exhaustive scholarship or a researched episode package. Each question has an individually written boundary. Source access, claim verification, current angle-scope review and publication approval remain pending. Add new branches and cases when they fill a real gap; no numeric topic cap applies.
+
+Select an exact case, edition, population, jurisdiction or product version, inspect sources and refine the question before using the existing registry idea/depth planning workflow. These BANK IDs are catalog identities, not approved IDEA IDs. Existing Q1-Q42 defaults, production queues and approval gates are unchanged.
