+# 056 Puzzles & Intellectual Mysteries: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 32 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Puzzles & Intellectual Mysteries. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. What makes a good puzzle
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Define the rival claims and their evidence standards. Compare sources on equal terms, separate empirical findings from values or interpretations and do not imply that all positions have equal evidential support.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the original sources behind the compared claims; explicit definitions and measurement methods; independent critiques with traceable evidence. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-056-013 | How does a well-posed puzzle differ from an unanswered factual question? | Specify rules, allowed information and the form of a valid solution before introducing examples. |
+| BANK-056-014 | Why can an unstated assumption make a puzzle appear impossible? | Compare multiple readings, identify the missing premise and distinguish ambiguity from genuine difficulty. |
+| BANK-056-015 | What separates proving a solution from finding one plausible answer? | Explain completeness, uniqueness and counterexamples using a self-contained logical puzzle. |
+| BANK-056-016 | How can a puzzle be challenging without hiding necessary information? | Compare clue sufficiency, misleading presentation and fair constraints rather than treating obscurity as depth. |
+
+## 02. Logic and deduction
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Define the rival claims and their evidence standards. Compare sources on equal terms, separate empirical findings from values or interpretations and do not imply that all positions have equal evidential support.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the original sources behind the compared claims; explicit definitions and measurement methods; independent critiques with traceable evidence. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-056-017 | How do truth-teller and liar puzzles depend on exact statement structure? | Build a small explicit example and check every allowed assignment instead of relying on a verbal trick. |
+| BANK-056-018 | Why can a self-referential sentence create a paradox rather than a contradiction in the story? | Separate language levels, truth assumptions and proposed responses without claiming one casual reformulation settles every version. |
+| BANK-056-019 | How do constraint grids turn scattered clues into a deductive chain? | Show defined entities, exclusions and a complete checked solution with no unstated associations. |
+| BANK-056-020 | What does a counterexample accomplish in a logical argument? | Use precise claims to demonstrate the difference between disproving universality and disproving every possible case. |
+
+## 03. Probability and decision puzzles
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-056-021 | Why can a probability answer change when the information-generating process changes? | State the sampling and disclosure procedure explicitly before comparing apparently similar questions. |
+| BANK-056-022 | How does the Monty Hall puzzle depend on the host's behavior? | Compare clearly specified host policies with exact reasoning or transparent simulation rather than one unexplained formula. |
+| BANK-056-023 | What makes the birthday problem about pairs rather than one chosen birthday? | State independence and distribution assumptions and distinguish calculation from real demographic complications. |
+| BANK-056-024 | How can a sequence of individually plausible choices lead to a poor collective outcome? | Construct a bounded decision puzzle with incentives and alternatives rather than treating a toy model as a full account of society. |
+
+## 04. Mathematics and open problems
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-056-025 | How does an unsolved mathematical problem differ from a statement known to be undecidable in a system? | Define the scope of proof and formal assumptions without using unsolved and impossible interchangeably. |
+| BANK-056-026 | Why can testing millions of examples fail to prove a conjecture? | Contrast finite evidence, counterexamples and general arguments through a transparent numerical example. |
+| BANK-056-027 | What makes an invariant useful in an impossible-transformation puzzle? | Identify a property preserved by every legal move and demonstrate why the target violates it. |
+| BANK-056-028 | How do infinite sets challenge ordinary intuitions about size? | Compare explicit correspondences and their limits without relying only on a surprising story. |
+
+## 05. Codes, texts and decipherment
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-056-029 | How can a historical cipher be studied without confusing it with an unknown language? | Compare encipherment, script identification and language evidence using a documented non-sensitive example. |
+| BANK-056-030 | What would distinguish a meaningful decipherment of the Voynich manuscript from pattern matching? | Specify repeatability, coverage, independent validation and the handling of failed predictions without endorsing a proposed solution. |
+| BANK-056-031 | How do acrostics become persuasive evidence rather than accidental letter selection? | Define the extraction rule in advance and test alternative passages and selection freedom. |
+| BANK-056-032 | Why do short encrypted texts often support many apparent solutions? | Explain limited constraints and competing plaintexts through a fictional example rather than declaring a famous mystery solved. |
+
+## 06. Historical and scientific mysteries
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
