+# 057 Secret Societies: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 32 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Secret Societies. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Defining secrecy and evidence
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-057-013 | What makes an organization secret rather than simply private? | Compare membership visibility, concealed activities, restricted knowledge and changing historical context without assuming criminality. |
+| BANK-057-014 | How can historians study a group whose records were deliberately limited? | Triangulate surviving documents, external accounts and material evidence while making archival gaps explicit. |
+| BANK-057-015 | Why should initiation symbolism be distinguished from evidence of political control? | Separate documented ritual, organizational authority and unsupported causal claims. |
+| BANK-057-016 | What can an exposed membership list establish and leave unknown? | Check date, provenance, authenticity and the difference between listed membership and coordinated action. |
+
+## 02. Fraternal organizations
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-057-017 | How did Freemasonry develop differently across national and local settings? | Compare a bounded set of lodges, constitutions and documented activities rather than treating the movement as one command structure. |
+| BANK-057-018 | What did mutual aid and sociability contribute to fraternal membership? | Examine dues, support, meeting practice and member testimony beyond sensationalized ritual descriptions. |
+| BANK-057-019 | How did gender and class affect access to fraternal institutions? | Use specific rules, exceptions and parallel organizations while avoiding assumptions across all branches. |
+| BANK-057-020 | Why did some states or religious authorities oppose particular fraternities? | Compare the actual objections, political setting and consequences using dated primary records. |
+
+## 03. Historical political and revolutionary networks
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-057-021 | How did clandestine organization affect a specific independence movement? | Study documented communication, membership boundaries and political aims without providing operational clandestine guidance. |
+| BANK-057-022 | What do the Bavarian Illuminati's surviving records support about their historical reach? | Separate the eighteenth-century organization, its suppression and later unrelated conspiracy narratives. |
+| BANK-057-023 | How did the Carbonari combine political organization with initiation forms? | Compare a bounded period's documents, regional differences and evidence of actual activity. |
+| BANK-057-024 | When did secrecy protect dissent and when did it concentrate unaccountable power? | Compare clearly identified historical cases without assuming all hidden organization has the same moral role. |
+
+## 04. Religious and esoteric associations
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Identify the text, edition, translator, commentary, community and ritual context as applicable. Attribute theological and spiritual claims, preserve regional variation and respect public versus restricted knowledge.
+
+**Source types to locate:** primary texts in traceable editions; authorized public teaching and ritual descriptions; consented community accounts and critical historical studies. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-057-025 | How should restricted religious knowledge be described without treating it as sinister? | Use community-authorized sources, distinguish access conventions and avoid disclosing material outside appropriate permission. |
+| BANK-057-026 | What can Rosicrucian manifestos reveal apart from claims of a single continuous organization? | Separate early texts, interpretation, later groups and uncertain institutional links. |
+| BANK-057-027 | How do historians distinguish an esoteric text's symbolic claims from a group's documented practices? | Compare genres, audience and independent evidence rather than reading every metaphor literally. |
+| BANK-057-028 | Why do modern organizations claim ancient lineages? | Examine the documentary chain, symbolic meaning and institutional incentives without assuming either proof or fraud from the claim alone. |
+
+## 05. Clubs, campuses and elite networks
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-057-029 | What can an exclusive club reveal about access to influence? | Compare verifiable membership, social connections and documented decisions without treating association as proof of a plot. |
+| BANK-057-030 | How did university societies acquire reputations beyond their recorded activities? | Trace publicity, alumni narratives and actual institutional records with privacy boundaries respected. |
+| BANK-057-031 | What is the difference between an informal network and an organization issuing coordinated instructions? | Specify the evidence needed for each claim through a historical case with independently documented actions. |
+| BANK-057-032 | How can a documentary investigate elite access without inventing hidden omnipotence? | Follow public records, funding and decisions while marking missing evidence and alternative explanations. |
+
+## 06. Symbols and public accusations
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-057-033 | Why do widely shared symbols become falsely treated as proof of common membership? | Trace earlier uses, multiple meanings and the absence of a demonstrated organizational connection. |
+| BANK-057-034 | How did fabricated texts fuel antisemitic conspiracy narratives? | Analyze documented forgery, circulation and harm without reproducing propaganda claims as plausible alternatives. |
+| BANK-057-035 | What changes when a political opponent is labeled part of a secret cabal? | Examine a bounded campaign's rhetoric, evidence and consequences without amplifying accusations about current individuals. |
+| BANK-057-036 | How do selective photographs create misleading narratives about coordination? | Check event context, dates, captions and routine explanations before inferring a secret relationship. |
+
+## 07. Methods for testing hidden-power claims
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-057-037 | What evidence would a claim of coordinated secret action need? | Define actors, actions, timing and independently verifiable links rather than using absence of evidence as confirmation. |
+| BANK-057-038 | How can a closed explanatory system make a conspiracy claim impossible to falsify? | Show how every counterexample is reclassified and contrast this with testable historical hypotheses. |
+| BANK-057-039 | Why does a documented conspiracy not validate unrelated conspiracy theories? | Compare the specific evidence, scale and mechanism of each claim independently. |
+| BANK-057-040 | What does an archive's silence mean when record survival is uneven? | Distinguish missing documentation, deliberate concealment and unsupported speculation through a defined research question. |
+
+## 08. Ethical documentary construction
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-057-041 | How can a secret-society film create curiosity without implying guilt by association? | Structure questions around verified practices, provenance and concrete decisions rather than ominous imagery alone. |
+| BANK-057-042 | What should a programme do when ritual footage lacks a reliable source? | Check origin and permission, label reenactments and exclude footage whose implied identity cannot be established. |
+| BANK-057-043 | How should conflicting insider memoirs be compared? | Evaluate publication date, access, incentives and corroboration while preserving unresolved differences. |
+| BANK-057-044 | What makes a conclusion about institutional influence appropriately bounded? | State the period, jurisdiction, documented mechanism and limits rather than escalating a local finding into universal control. |
+
+## Coverage limits and next step
+
+This is an expanded topic-bank draft, not exhaustive scholarship or a researched episode package. Each question has an individually written boundary. Source access, claim verification, current angle-scope review and publication approval remain pending. Add new branches and cases when they fill a real gap; no numeric topic cap applies.
+
+Select an exact case, edition, population, jurisdiction or product version, inspect sources and refine the question before using the existing registry idea/depth planning workflow. These BANK IDs are catalog identities, not approved IDEA IDs. Existing Q1-Q42 defaults, production queues and approval gates are unchanged.
