+# 058 Folklore & Legends: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 32 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Folklore & Legends. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Folklore as living tradition
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Identify the text, edition, translator, commentary, community and ritual context as applicable. Attribute theological and spiritual claims, preserve regional variation and respect public versus restricted knowledge.
+
+**Source types to locate:** primary texts in traceable editions; authorized public teaching and ritual descriptions; consented community accounts and critical historical studies. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-058-013 | How does a folktale differ from a historical chronicle without becoming culturally unimportant? | Explain genre, performance and social purpose while separating narrative meaning from factual verification. |
+| BANK-058-014 | Why can several contradictory versions belong to the same tradition? | Compare storytellers, audiences, language and setting rather than assuming one version must be the original truth. |
+| BANK-058-015 | How do folklorists document a performance beyond transcribing its words? | Include voice, gesture, audience response, occasion, consent and contextual notes. |
+| BANK-058-016 | What changes when an oral story is first published in print? | Trace editorial selection, language standardization, attribution and the relationship with living storytellers. |
+
+## 02. South Asian narrative landscapes
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Identify the text, edition, translator, commentary, community and ritual context as applicable. Attribute theological and spiritual claims, preserve regional variation and respect public versus restricted knowledge.
+
+**Source types to locate:** primary texts in traceable editions; authorized public teaching and ritual descriptions; consented community accounts and critical historical studies. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-058-017 | How do regional Ramayana retellings frame the same episode differently? | Compare identified texts or performances with community context rather than ranking them against one presumed universal version. |
+| BANK-058-018 | What can local guardian-deity legends reveal about place and belonging? | Use community-authorized accounts, shrine history and variations without treating devotion as a puzzle to debunk. |
+| BANK-058-019 | How do river legends connect ecological observation and sacred geography? | Compare named traditions, landscape change and ritual practice while keeping symbolic and scientific explanations distinct. |
+| BANK-058-020 | What does a village foundation legend reveal about claims to land and ancestry? | Present multiple community perspectives and documented history without adjudicating present ownership through a tale. |
+
+## 03. Tale types, motifs and comparison
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-058-021 | How can two stories share a motif without proving direct borrowing? | Compare dates, routes, narrative structure and independent development before proposing transmission. |
+| BANK-058-022 | What do tale-type catalogues help researchers compare and what do they flatten? | Examine classification, local meaning and the risk of treating an index label as a complete interpretation. |
+| BANK-058-023 | How do trickster figures challenge social rules in different settings? | Compare specific stories and audiences without assuming tricksters have an identical role across cultures. |
+| BANK-058-024 | Why do forbidden-room and broken-taboo stories invite multiple interpretations? | Present textual details, performance context and competing readings rather than a single universal psychological answer. |
+
+## 04. Performance and community
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use consented participant accounts, local records and the community's own descriptions. Include differences of position, access and power; testimony is not a representative survey or clinical proof.
+
+**Source types to locate:** consented interviews and oral histories; community publications and local records; field notes with method and consent documented. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-058-025 | How does a storytelling festival change the relationship between performer and audience? | Compare local practice, curated stages, payment and decisions about what becomes publicly shareable. |
+| BANK-058-026 | What can children's games preserve that formal literary archives miss? | Document rules, songs, variation and intergenerational transmission with consent and without ranking versions by purity. |
+| BANK-058-027 | How do migrant communities adapt stories to new places? | Compare language, setting, audience and identity across documented versions rather than describing adaptation as simple loss. |
+| BANK-058-028 | Who receives credit when a collective tradition is adapted by a named artist? | Trace sources, permissions, attribution and community perspectives through a specific adaptation. |
+
+## 05. Legends attached to places and events
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-058-029 | How does a historic ruin become associated with a ghost legend? | Build separate timelines for the structure, documented events and the earliest recorded supernatural narrative. |
+| BANK-058-030 | What can a battle legend reveal about later community memory? | Compare historical evidence and successive retellings without using a legend as direct proof of combat details. |
+| BANK-058-031 | Why do travelers' tales attach wonders to distant places? | Examine audience expectations, translation, observation and literary convention in identified travel accounts. |
+| BANK-058-032 | How should a documentary handle a local legend tied to a recent tragedy? | Respect affected people, verify chronology and avoid turning identifiable loss into entertainment without consent. |
+
+## 06. Collections and colonial archives
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-058-033 | How did collectors' questionnaires shape the folklore they recorded? | Inspect the collection method, language mediation and omitted genres rather than treating archives as unfiltered community speech. |
+| BANK-058-034 | What changes when a colonial category becomes the title of a folklore collection? | Compare administrative assumptions, community names and later reinterpretations without repeating derogatory labels unnecessarily. |
+| BANK-058-035 | How can researchers recover the voices of unnamed storytellers? | Use field notes, correspondence and community collaboration while acknowledging identities that remain uncertain. |
+| BANK-058-036 | Why do translations of folklore need their own provenance? | Track source version, translator, editorial changes and intended readership rather than treating every translation as equivalent. |
+
+## 07. Modern legends and digital circulation
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Inspect the actual product version, official documentation, terms and privacy policies. Test claims under stated conditions and distinguish account permissions, data retention, measured functionality and marketing.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official versioned documentation and release notes; dated privacy policies and service terms; reproducible product tests and original validation reports. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-058-037 | How can an urban legend spread as a personal experience nobody directly witnessed? | Trace narrative chains, recurring forms and verifiable origins without exposing private individuals. |
+| BANK-058-038 | What happens when an internet meme becomes part of a local legend? | Compare dated posts, offline retellings and changing meanings while preserving uncertainty about earliest creation. |
+| BANK-058-039 | How do warning stories turn everyday anxieties into memorable plots? | Analyze specific narratives and their social context without repeating harmful rumors as factual warnings. |
+| BANK-058-040 | What distinguishes a documented ritual from an online challenge falsely presented as ancient folklore? | Check early attestations, community testimony and platform circulation rather than assuming age from dramatic presentation. |
+
+## 08. Adaptation and ethical interpretation
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-058-041 | How can animation preserve a tale's ambiguity instead of inventing a definitive version? | Choose and credit a source, label adaptation choices and explain where other versions differ. |
+| BANK-058-042 | When should a folklore project refrain from publishing restricted material? | Follow community-defined access, consent and cultural boundaries rather than assuming availability equals permission. |
+| BANK-058-043 | How should supernatural claims be framed in a cultural documentary? | Attribute beliefs and experiences accurately while separating their significance from unverified causal conclusions. |
+| BANK-058-044 | What makes a folklore series useful to the communities whose stories it presents? | Plan language access, practitioner credit, review opportunities and return of recordings alongside audience appeal. |
+
+## Coverage limits and next step
+
+This is an expanded topic-bank draft, not exhaustive scholarship or a researched episode package. Each question has an individually written boundary. Source access, claim verification, current angle-scope review and publication approval remain pending. Add new branches and cases when they fill a real gap; no numeric topic cap applies.
+
+Select an exact case, edition, population, jurisdiction or product version, inspect sources and refine the question before using the existing registry idea/depth planning workflow. These BANK IDs are catalog identities, not approved IDEA IDs. Existing Q1-Q42 defaults, production queues and approval gates are unchanged.
