+# 059 Mythical Creatures: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 32 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Mythical Creatures. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Creatures, sources and categories
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Identify the text, edition, translator, commentary, community and ritual context as applicable. Attribute theological and spiritual claims, preserve regional variation and respect public versus restricted knowledge.
+
+**Source types to locate:** primary texts in traceable editions; authorized public teaching and ritual descriptions; consented community accounts and critical historical studies. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-059-013 | How do mythical creatures differ from deities, spirits and unusual animals in their own traditions? | Use community-specific categories and source context rather than imposing one universal monster taxonomy. |
+| BANK-059-014 | Why can a creature's appearance change across texts and images? | Trace dated representations, artistic convention and local variation without assuming an original fixed anatomy. |
+| BANK-059-015 | What does the earliest surviving reference establish about a creature's history? | Separate attestation from invention and account for lost oral or written traditions. |
+| BANK-059-016 | How should a creature encyclopedia distinguish a traditional account from modern fantasy? | Record source, place, period and adaptation status for every description. |
+
+## 02. South Asian beings and symbolism
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Identify the text, edition, translator, commentary, community and ritual context as applicable. Attribute theological and spiritual claims, preserve regional variation and respect public versus restricted knowledge.
+
+**Source types to locate:** primary texts in traceable editions; authorized public teaching and ritual descriptions; consented community accounts and critical historical studies. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-059-017 | How do naga traditions connect water, protection and local sacred places? | Compare identified textual and regional accounts without reducing diverse naga traditions to one serpent-monster profile. |
+| BANK-059-018 | What roles does Garuda play across religious and artistic settings? | Distinguish narrative, theological, iconographic and regional uses while crediting the sources selected. |
+| BANK-059-019 | How do yaksha and yakshi representations change across periods and traditions? | Compare a bounded set of texts and objects rather than assigning one timeless moral character. |
+| BANK-059-020 | What do makara images combine and how do their meanings vary by setting? | Examine actual architectural or ritual uses without treating a composite visual form as a zoological report. |
+
+## 03. Dragons, serpents and composite beings
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Identify the text, edition, translator, commentary, community and ritual context as applicable. Attribute theological and spiritual claims, preserve regional variation and respect public versus restricted knowledge.
+
+**Source types to locate:** primary texts in traceable editions; authorized public teaching and ritual descriptions; consented community accounts and critical historical studies. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-059-021 | Why do dragon traditions resist a single worldwide definition? | Compare named regional terms, roles and source histories rather than assuming all dragons share one origin. |
+| BANK-059-022 | How did European dragon imagery change through religious storytelling and heraldry? | Trace documented images and narratives while keeping later fantasy designs separate. |
+| BANK-059-023 | What can Chinese long traditions reveal about authority and environmental symbolism? | Use specific periods and contexts instead of translating every representation into a generic Western dragon. |
+| BANK-059-024 | How should similarities among composite creatures be investigated? | Compare contact evidence, form, chronology and function without proposing ancient global contact from resemblance alone. |
+
+## 04. Water and wilderness beings
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Identify the text, edition, translator, commentary, community and ritual context as applicable. Attribute theological and spiritual claims, preserve regional variation and respect public versus restricted knowledge.
+
+**Source types to locate:** primary texts in traceable editions; authorized public teaching and ritual descriptions; consented community accounts and critical historical studies. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-059-025 | How do mermaid narratives differ across sailors' accounts, folklore and visual art? | Separate genres, named traditions and later commercial imagery rather than merging every aquatic being into one type. |
+| BANK-059-026 | What social meanings surround shape-shifting animal beings in a chosen tradition? | Use specific stories and community interpretations without universal claims about fear of nature. |
+| BANK-059-027 | How can forest-creature legends express boundaries of settlement and knowledge? | Compare narrative setting, ecology and social rules while treating supernatural causation as attributed belief. |
+| BANK-059-028 | Why are reports of unknown animals different from established zoological evidence? | Distinguish testimony, images, specimens and independently verified observations without mocking witnesses or claiming discovery. |
+
+## 05. Creatures in material and textual culture
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-059-029 | What can a bestiary reveal about moral teaching rather than animal science? | Study genre, audience and selected entries without judging the text solely by modern zoological accuracy. |
+| BANK-059-030 | How did travelers' descriptions contribute to unfamiliar creature imagery? | Trace observation, translation, illustration and retelling through a documented chain. |
+| BANK-059-031 | Why can the same creature appear protective on one object and threatening in another story? | Compare function, audience, location and specific narrative roles instead of assigning one symbolic meaning. |
+| BANK-059-032 | What do creature depictions on seals or coins permit historians to infer? | Separate visible form, secure context and debated identification without forcing a named myth onto ambiguous imagery. |
+
+## 06. Monsters and social boundaries
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use consented participant accounts, local records and the community's own descriptions. Include differences of position, access and power; testimony is not a representative survey or clinical proof.
+
+**Source types to locate:** consented interviews and oral histories; community publications and local records; field notes with method and consent documented. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-059-033 | How do monster stories mark ideas about outsiders? | Analyze particular texts, power relations and historical reception without repeating dehumanizing descriptions as valid categories. |
+| BANK-059-034 | What changes when a feared being becomes a community mascot? | Trace commercial use, local identity and negotiation with older meanings through a documented case. |
+| BANK-059-035 | How do children's versions soften or transform frightening creatures? | Compare editions, illustrations and audience assumptions rather than treating adaptation as simple decline. |
+| BANK-059-036 | Why do some creature legends persist after a proposed natural explanation appears? | Explore storytelling, identity and multiple meanings without claiming believers ignore all evidence. |
+
+## 07. Modern media and invented lore
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-059-037 | How did film production constraints help shape a modern monster's familiar design? | Trace concept art, practical effects and creator testimony for an identified screen creature. |
+| BANK-059-038 | What distinguishes a modern invented creature with online lore from an older folk tradition? | Document authorship, first publication and community adaptation without falsely assigning ancient origins. |
+| BANK-059-039 | How can games combine creatures from different traditions responsibly? | Track source-specific meanings, adaptation choices, credit and community concerns rather than using names as interchangeable skins. |
+| BANK-059-040 | Why do fan-made creature charts often imply relationships unsupported by historical sources? | Separate fictional worldbuilding from comparative folklore and label invented genealogies clearly. |
+
+## 08. Evidence and visual reconstruction
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-059-041 | How should a documentary reconstruct a creature described inconsistently across sources? | Show source-specific variants and label artist choices instead of presenting one design as recovered fact. |
+| BANK-059-042 | What would a biological claim about a legendary creature need beyond eyewitness accounts? | Explain verifiable evidence, chain of custody and independent analysis without designing a wildlife capture expedition. |
+| BANK-059-043 | How can a fossil discovery become linked to a creature myth without proving it caused the story? | Compare documented historical awareness, chronology and alternative explanations rather than assuming retrospective resemblance establishes origin. |
+| BANK-059-044 | What makes a creature episode respectful, engaging and evidentially clear? | Present narrative meaning, source history and verified physical claims as distinct layers with appropriate credits. |
+
+## Coverage limits and next step
+
+This is an expanded topic-bank draft, not exhaustive scholarship or a researched episode package. Each question has an individually written boundary. Source access, claim verification, current angle-scope review and publication approval remain pending. Add new branches and cases when they fill a real gap; no numeric topic cap applies.
+
+Select an exact case, edition, population, jurisdiction or product version, inspect sources and refine the question before using the existing registry idea/depth planning workflow. These BANK IDs are catalog identities, not approved IDEA IDs. Existing Q1-Q42 defaults, production queues and approval gates are unchanged.
