+# 064 Home & Lifestyle: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 29 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Home & Lifestyle. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Household needs and everyday space
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Use consented participant accounts, local records and the community's own descriptions. Include differences of position, access and power; testimony is not a representative survey or clinical proof.
+
+**Source types to locate:** consented interviews and oral histories; community publications and local records; field notes with method and consent documented. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-064-013 | How can a household identify which rooms are failing its daily routines? | Map waking, cooking, work, care and rest through a consented day-in-the-life exercise; distinguish residents' priorities from an aspirational room photograph. |
+| BANK-064-014 | What changes when a small room must support several activities? | Compare storage, circulation, furniture movement and transitions in one measured layout; keep structural alterations outside the proposed makeover. |
+| BANK-064-015 | How can shared homes negotiate clutter without imposing one person's standard? | Document different definitions of useful possessions, shared zones and decision authority; use consented examples rather than diagnosing occupants from their belongings. |
+| BANK-064-016 | Why can a visually tidy space still be difficult to use? | Trace access height, retrieval effort, labels and return paths for everyday objects; include disability and household variation rather than only decorative symmetry. |
+
+## 02. Cleaning and material care
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-064-017 | How should a cleaning method be matched to the surface rather than a viral tip? | Consult manufacturer care guidance for wood, stone, textiles and coatings; compare compatibility and test-area limits without inventing chemical mixtures. |
+| BANK-064-018 | What is the difference between cleaning a surface and making a disinfection claim? | Compare intended purpose, product labeling, contact conditions and evidence requirements from official guidance; exclude homemade formulations and universal germ-killing promises. |
+| BANK-064-019 | How can a realistic cleaning routine prioritize limited time and energy? | Separate high-use zones, access needs, household roles and maintenance frequency; use an illustrative routine without implying a clinical cleanliness standard. |
+| BANK-064-020 | Why can laundry results depend on more than the detergent chosen? | Examine fabric labels, load size, water conditions, temperature and drying; verify appliance instructions and avoid unqualified stain-removal recipes. |
+
+## 03. Kitchen, food storage and domestic work
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use consented participant accounts, local records and the community's own descriptions. Include differences of position, access and power; testimony is not a representative survey or clinical proof.
+
+**Source types to locate:** consented interviews and oral histories; community publications and local records; field notes with method and consent documented. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-064-021 | How does a kitchen workflow change for one cook versus several cooks? | Follow preparation, cooking, serving and cleanup in a measured existing kitchen; include shared access and exclude electrical or plumbing modification advice. |
+| BANK-064-022 | What makes a household food inventory useful without creating extra waste? | Compare visibility, date labeling, meal planning and realistic purchase quantities; refer any storage-duration claims to current food-safety authorities. |
+| BANK-064-023 | How can meal preparation account for shift work and irregular schedules? | Map available time, equipment, storage and household preferences; distinguish organizational strategies from individualized dietary or medical recommendations. |
+| BANK-064-024 | Why is the hidden planning behind domestic work difficult to divide fairly? | Separate noticing, scheduling, purchasing and execution through consented household accounts; avoid assuming equality from the number of visible chores alone. |
+
+## 04. Light, sound and comfort
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-064-025 | How can lighting be arranged around tasks in a rented room? | Compare glare, shadows, fixture positions and permitted portable options; use product instructions and keep rewiring outside the episode. |
+| BANK-064-026 | What can soft furnishings change about room sound and what can they not change? | Separate reverberation, sound transmission and source noise with a bounded measurement example; avoid representing decorative panels as proven structural soundproofing. |
+| BANK-064-027 | How should a home-comfort complaint be documented before buying a device? | Record room use, temperature, humidity and timing with stated sensor limits; distinguish comfort observations from diagnosing damp, mold or building defects. |
+
+## 05. Housing transitions and spending
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use current official terms, filings or macroeconomic data; state jurisdiction, date, fees, taxes, risk, units and assumptions. Use transparent illustrative calculations rather than personal recommendations or return promises.
+
+**Source types to locate:** regulator or official statistical publications; actual contracts, fee schedules and audited filings; original economic studies and disclosed calculation inputs. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-064-028 | How can a first-home essentials list separate immediate needs from optional upgrades? | Build a transparent illustrative budget around sleeping, cooking, hygiene and access; include recurring costs without using undated shopping recommendations. |
+| BANK-064-029 | What should be compared when renting, buying or borrowing an infrequently used household item? | Calculate storage, maintenance, fees and frequency for one item; label assumptions and avoid treating convenience as identical for every household. |
+| BANK-064-030 | How can a move be planned around fragile items, records and service continuity? | Trace inventory, labeling, access windows and handover responsibilities; require dated local operator terms for costs and avoid exposing private addresses. |
+| BANK-064-031 | What does the total cost of a home appliance include beyond purchase price? | Compare energy, consumables, service, useful life and financing under explicit assumptions; defer current tariffs and model claims to official documentation. |
+
+## 06. Accessibility and stages of life
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use consented participant accounts, local records and the community's own descriptions. Include differences of position, access and power; testimony is not a representative survey or clinical proof.
+
+**Source types to locate:** consented interviews and oral histories; community publications and local records; field notes with method and consent documented. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-064-032 | How can storage be adapted for a resident with changing mobility? | Begin with consented reach and movement preferences, stable placement and access paths; refer structural or clinical adaptations to qualified assessment. |
+| BANK-064-033 | What changes when a home must accommodate guests and ongoing care work? | Examine privacy, resting space, bathroom access and caregiver routines; avoid inferring a family's needs from age or diagnosis alone. |
+| BANK-064-034 | How can a sensory-friendly room be evaluated by its actual occupant? | Document lighting, textures, noise and control preferences through consented feedback; distinguish individual comfort from treatment claims. |
+
+## 07. Seasonal maintenance and household resilience
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-064-035 | How can a renter distinguish routine upkeep from a repair to report? | Use a named jurisdiction's tenancy guidance and a documented responsibility checklist; keep fault diagnosis and legal conclusions separate from reporting observations. |
+| BANK-064-036 | What belongs in a household emergency plan for a specific local hazard? | Use current local emergency-management instructions to cover contacts, access needs and supplies; exclude generalized survival promises and unverified hazard forecasts. |
+| BANK-064-037 | How can households track maintenance without collecting unnecessary personal data? | Compare a paper log with a private digital record for warranties, service and recurring checks; use fictitious identifiers and define retention boundaries. |
+| BANK-064-038 | What should happen to unwanted household goods before they become mixed waste? | Trace repair, donation acceptance, reuse and authorized disposal requirements locally; do not assume charities accept every item or every material is recyclable. |
+
+## 08. Lifestyle images and consumption
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-064-039 | How do staged home images alter perceptions of ordinary domestic life? | Compare crop, lighting, absent possessions and sponsorship with permission-cleared examples; distinguish aesthetic choices from evidence about residents' actual routines. |
+| BANK-064-040 | Can a low-buy household experiment measure useful change without becoming a moral scorecard? | Define a voluntary baseline, spending categories, repair attempts and reported tradeoffs; avoid equating reduced purchasing with universal wellbeing or virtue. |
+| BANK-064-041 | How can a home makeover be documented honestly when only a small budget is available? | Show retained items, labor, borrowed tools and full costs through an original room example; disclose gifted products and exclude unsafe modifications. |
+
+## Coverage limits and next step
+
+This is an expanded topic-bank draft, not exhaustive scholarship or a researched episode package. Each question has an individually written boundary. Source access, claim verification, current angle-scope review and publication approval remain pending. Add new branches and cases when they fill a real gap; no numeric topic cap applies.
+
+Select an exact case, edition, population, jurisdiction or product version, inspect sources and refine the question before using the existing registry idea/depth planning workflow. These BANK IDs are catalog identities, not approved IDEA IDs. Existing Q1-Q42 defaults, production queues and approval gates are unchanged.
