+# 065 Gardening & Plants: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**9 categories · 37 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Gardening & Plants. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Plant needs and observation
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-065-013 | How can a gardener distinguish a plant's light exposure from a room that looks bright? | Compare duration, orientation, shade and a documented measurement method for selected plants; separate visual impressions from species-specific horticultural requirements. |
+| BANK-065-014 | Why does watering frequency change when the pot, soil or season changes? | Trace drainage, root volume, substrate and evaporation through labeled observations; avoid issuing one schedule for all plants and climates. |
+| BANK-065-015 | What does a plant's growth habit reveal before it is planted in a small space? | Use authoritative descriptions of mature size, root behavior and support needs; distinguish a nursery specimen from its likely developed form. |
+| BANK-065-016 | How can a garden journal make plant changes easier to interpret? | Record weather, watering, growth stages and interventions for a defined plot; keep observations separate from causal conclusions drawn from one season. |
+
+## 02. Soil, substrates and fertility
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-065-017 | What can a soil test tell a home gardener that color and texture cannot? | Compare pH, nutrient measurements, sampling and laboratory interpretation; use local extension guidance and exclude guessing amendments from appearance alone. |
+| BANK-065-018 | How do raised-bed soil, potting mix and garden soil serve different settings? | Examine drainage, compaction, organic components and container conditions; avoid equating product names with standardized composition. |
+| BANK-065-019 | Why does adding fertilizer fail to solve every case of poor growth? | Compare root conditions, light, water and confirmed nutrient findings; require crop-specific recommendations and avoid escalating applications without evidence. |
+| BANK-065-020 | How can compost maturity affect the decision to use it around plants? | Review feedstocks, temperature records, stability and contamination guidance; exclude disposal of hazardous material and do not treat all dark organic matter as finished compost. |
+
+## 03. Seeds, propagation and establishment
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-065-021 | What changes between direct sowing and starting seedlings for transplanting? | Compare crop sensitivity, season length, root disturbance and labor in a local plan; verify species-specific guidance rather than presenting one method as best. |
+| BANK-065-022 | How can seed-germination records distinguish a storage problem from unsuitable growing conditions? | Compare lot age, temperature, moisture and emergence using a small controlled sample; avoid diagnosing seed quality from a single failed tray. |
+| BANK-065-023 | Why do some cuttings need different treatment from other cuttings? | Compare selected plant tissues, timing, rooting environment and documented methods; keep unsupported hormone recipes and protected-plant collection outside scope. |
+| BANK-065-024 | How should a newly purchased plant be introduced into an existing collection? | Inspect visible condition, isolation options, watering changes and pest monitoring using extension advice; distinguish precaution from a guarantee against infestation. |
+
+## 04. Edible gardens
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-065-025 | How can a vegetable calendar be built for one climate rather than copied from another country? | Use local frost, heat and rainfall records with crop maturity ranges; mark forecasts as uncertain and distinguish sowing dates from harvest guarantees. |
+| BANK-065-026 | Why can tomatoes set flowers without producing the expected fruit? | Investigate documented effects of temperature, pollination, nutrition and cultivar characteristics; avoid declaring one cause from photographs alone. |
+| BANK-065-027 | What limits the usefulness of companion-planting claims in a vegetable bed? | Separate plausible mechanisms, field evidence, spacing and crop management; compare named claims without assuming tradition or a small observation proves effectiveness. |
+| BANK-065-028 | How can a kitchen herb garden be designed around repeated harvesting? | Compare selected species' growth habits, light, container size and cutting guidance; keep identification and food-safety checks separate from decorative use. |
+| BANK-065-029 | When does crop rotation help a small garden and when are its options constrained? | Map plant families, disease history, bed size and soil records; use extension recommendations and avoid promising that rotation prevents every pest problem. |
+
+## 05. Pests, disease and diagnosis
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-065-030 | How can leaf damage be documented before deciding it is an insect problem? | Compare feeding patterns, weather injury, watering stress and symptom distribution; collect clear observations rather than diagnosing from one detached leaf. |
+| BANK-065-031 | What does integrated pest management change about the order of gardening decisions? | Trace identification, monitoring, thresholds and nonchemical options for a bounded pest case; require official label compliance if controls are later discussed. |
+| BANK-065-032 | Why can beneficial insects be harmed by an apparently targeted garden intervention? | Map exposure, timing and food-web relationships with primary or extension evidence; exclude homemade pesticide mixtures and claims that natural means harmless. |
+| BANK-065-033 | How can an unfamiliar plant be checked for invasive status before planting? | Consult current regional invasive-species lists, botanical identification and local restrictions; distinguish ecological risk from merely vigorous growth. |
+
+## 06. Trees, shrubs and perennial design
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-065-034 | What should be learned about a tree before choosing its planting location? | Compare mature canopy, roots, utility clearance, local ecology and site conditions through municipal or arboricultural guidance; exclude hazardous tree-work procedures. |
+| BANK-065-035 | How does pruning depend on plant growth and flowering habit? | Compare selected shrubs with documented bud development and seasonal response; avoid turning a general explanation into instructions for all woody plants. |
+| BANK-065-036 | Why can a newly planted tree struggle in a regularly watered lawn? | Examine root-zone conditions, planting depth, competition and watering distribution; use case records and do not infer a diagnosis from trunk appearance alone. |
+| BANK-065-037 | How can a perennial border be planned for seasonal change rather than one photograph? | Map flowering, foliage, mature spread and maintenance across a local year; include verified plant requirements and distinguish design preference from ecological benefit. |
+
+## 07. Containers, balconies and indoor plants
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-065-038 | What makes a balcony planting plan depend on the building as well as the plants? | Consider permitted loading, wind, drainage and safe placement before species selection; require building-specific guidance instead of estimating structural capacity from images. |
+| BANK-065-039 | Why can repotting help one houseplant and stress another? | Compare root occupancy, substrate condition, season and observed growth; use identified species and avoid treating larger pots as an automatic improvement. |
+| BANK-065-040 | How should indoor grow-light claims be matched to a plant-growing task? | Compare spectrum, intensity at canopy, duration and measured coverage; rely on manufacturer safety instructions and exclude DIY electrical modifications. |
+| BANK-065-041 | What does a self-watering container change about moisture management? | Trace reservoir, capillary movement, substrate and overflow through an identified design; include failure conditions rather than claiming maintenance-free cultivation. |
+
+## 08. Water, habitat and climate adaptation
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-065-042 | How can a garden water budget be estimated without relying on fixed daily amounts? | Combine planted area, rainfall, soil observations and locally recommended irrigation methods; label assumptions and distinguish estimates from measured demand. |
+| BANK-065-043 | What evidence distinguishes a pollinator garden from a bed with many flowers? | Examine local species, flowering continuity, nesting, pesticide exposure and observed visitors; avoid assuming every ornamental supports the same animals. |
+| BANK-065-044 | How can rainwater collection be evaluated for a particular garden? | Check local rules, roof materials, storage, overflow and intended use; leave drinking-water claims and structural installation to qualified local guidance. |
+| BANK-065-045 | Why can native-plant recommendations differ within the same country? | Compare ecoregion, soil, provenance and conservation objectives using botanical authorities; distinguish political borders from ecological suitability. |
+
+## 09. Evidence, access and responsible growing
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Define the rival claims and their evidence standards. Compare sources on equal terms, separate empirical findings from values or interpretations and do not imply that all positions have equal evidential support.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the original sources behind the compared claims; explicit definitions and measurement methods; independent critiques with traceable evidence. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-065-046 | How can two fertilizer or growing-method claims be tested fairly in a home plot? | Specify comparable plants, replication, measurements and weather records; acknowledge small-sample uncertainty and avoid marketing conclusions from a single harvest. |
+| BANK-065-047 | How can community gardens balance shared rules with different growing traditions? | Use consented accounts, plot agreements, water access and seed choices; distinguish local negotiation from a universal model of communal gardening. |
+| BANK-065-048 | What should a gardener verify before buying a rare or unusually named plant online? | Check botanical identity, provenance, import restrictions and propagation claims through official records; exclude trade or collection instructions for protected species. |
+| BANK-065-049 | How can an accessible garden plan prioritize the gardener's actual tasks? | Observe reach, pathways, tools and rest needs with the user's consent; compare workable adaptations without prescribing clinical solutions or assuming raised beds fit everyone. |
+
+## Coverage limits and next step
+
+This is an expanded topic-bank draft, not exhaustive scholarship or a researched episode package. Each question has an individually written boundary. Source access, claim verification, current angle-scope review and publication approval remain pending. Add new branches and cases when they fill a real gap; no numeric topic cap applies.
+
+Select an exact case, edition, population, jurisdiction or product version, inspect sources and refine the question before using the existing registry idea/depth planning workflow. These BANK IDs are catalog identities, not approved IDEA IDs. Existing Q1-Q42 defaults, production queues and approval gates are unchanged.
