+# 068 Pets: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 33 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Pets. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Choosing and welcoming a companion animal
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Use consented participant accounts, local records and the community's own descriptions. Include differences of position, access and power; testimony is not a representative survey or clinical proof.
+
+**Source types to locate:** consented interviews and oral histories; community publications and local records; field notes with method and consent documented. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-068-013 | What should a household learn about a species before deciding it fits daily life? | Compare typical lifespan, social needs, housing, care time and local access to qualified veterinary services; avoid recommending an animal solely from appearance or a short temperament label. |
+| BANK-068-014 | How can adoption expectations differ from what a shelter can confidently know? | Discuss observation context, incomplete history, transition support and consented staff accounts; distinguish a behavior seen in a kennel from a guaranteed lifelong trait. |
+| BANK-068-015 | What makes introducing a new pet a process rather than a single meeting? | Use species-specific welfare guidance for separation options, supervision and gradual adjustment; exclude forced encounters and promises that every pairing will become compatible. |
+| BANK-068-016 | How can children participate in pet care without receiving sole responsibility for welfare? | Map age-appropriate supervised tasks, adult oversight and hygiene guidance; avoid treating an animal as a tool for teaching responsibility at any cost. |
+
+## 02. Everyday care and species-specific environments
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-068-017 | Why can the same home environment meet a dog's needs but fail a rabbit's needs? | Compare movement, hiding, social contact and species-specific housing from recognized welfare authorities; avoid using a single enclosure checklist across species. |
+| BANK-068-018 | What should a cat's environment provide beyond food and a litter tray? | Examine choice, resting areas, scratching, play and resource placement through feline welfare guidance; separate environmental enrichment from a diagnosis or cure for behavior problems. |
+| BANK-068-019 | How can bird housing account for flight, social interaction and sensory stimulation? | Consult species-specific husbandry and veterinary sources on space, contact and hazards; avoid assuming a cage dimension or toy makes every bird's needs satisfied. |
+| BANK-068-020 | Why do reptiles require species-specific temperature and lighting plans? | Compare documented habitat requirements, measurement and equipment maintenance for a named species; require specialist guidance rather than supplying a universal setup recipe. |
+| BANK-068-021 | What makes aquarium care an ecosystem-management task rather than simply changing water? | Outline species compatibility, nitrogen cycling, water monitoring and welfare sources; exclude stocking or chemical dosing prescriptions unsupported by the exact tank conditions. |
+
+## 03. Feeding, grooming and preventive care
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Inspect current professional guidance and original studies for the exact population, intervention or measurement. Report comparators, absolute outcomes, uncertainty, harms and limits. Do not diagnose, prescribe or imply that education replaces professional care.
+
+**Source types to locate:** current official clinical or public-health guidance; original trials, cohort studies or diagnostic-validation reports; study registrations, protocols and adverse-event records. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-068-022 | How can a pet-food label be interpreted without relying on promotional ingredient stories? | Review the applicable labeling authority, intended species, life stage and nutritional-adequacy statement; leave individualized feeding decisions to qualified veterinary assessment. |
+| BANK-068-023 | Why should weight trends be considered alongside an animal's appearance? | Compare repeated measurements, species-appropriate body-condition methods and professional interpretation; exclude calorie prescriptions or diagnosis from photographs. |
+| BANK-068-024 | How can grooming requirements differ between coat types and species? | Use veterinary or professional care guidance to compare brushing, handling tolerance and skin observation; exclude painful restraint or treatment of suspected disease. |
+| BANK-068-025 | What can an owner prepare before a routine veterinary visit? | Plan records, observed changes, medication lists and transport using clinic guidance; distinguish preparation from deciding which tests or treatments the animal needs. |
+
+## 04. Learning, communication and behavior
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-068-026 | What distinguishes reinforcement from a pet simply receiving a treat? | Trace timing, observable behavior and learning consequences in a humane original example; consult qualified behavior sources and avoid punishment-based demonstrations. |
+| BANK-068-027 | How can an owner's interpretation of body language miss fear or discomfort? | Compare species-specific context, posture and behavior sequences using expert-reviewed footage; do not label a pet happy or guilty from a single facial expression. |
+| BANK-068-028 | Why can unwanted behavior persist when people think they are discouraging it? | Investigate consistency, environment and inadvertent reinforcement in a bounded hypothetical case; keep pain or illness assessment within veterinary care. |
+| BANK-068-029 | What does effective enrichment require beyond buying more toys? | Compare choice, novelty, species-typical behavior and observed engagement; avoid assuming constant stimulation or a specific product benefits every animal. |
+
+## 05. Handling, travel and household hazards
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Inspect current professional guidance and original studies for the exact population, intervention or measurement. Report comparators, absolute outcomes, uncertainty, harms and limits. Do not diagnose, prescribe or imply that education replaces professional care.
+
+**Source types to locate:** current official clinical or public-health guidance; original trials, cohort studies or diagnostic-validation reports; study registrations, protocols and adverse-event records. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-068-030 | How can a pet learn to tolerate routine handling with less distress? | Outline gradual consent-aware handling principles from qualified behavior guidance; exclude forced restraint, sedation advice and procedures that require trained professionals. |
+| BANK-068-031 | What should be checked before transporting a pet by road or public transport? | Use current local rules, carrier guidance, temperature considerations and the animal's clinical needs; distinguish planning from guaranteed fitness to travel. |
+| BANK-068-032 | How can household hazards be mapped without turning the episode into an unverified toxicity list? | Choose a few substances or objects and consult official veterinary toxicology sources for species-specific risk; direct actual exposure decisions to veterinary services rather than home remedies. |
+| BANK-068-033 | Why does emergency preparation need an animal-specific plan? | Include identification records, safe transport, essential care information and official local shelter policies; avoid prescribing emergency treatment or assuming shelters accept all species. |
+
+## 06. Health literacy and veterinary evidence
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Inspect current professional guidance and original studies for the exact population, intervention or measurement. Report comparators, absolute outcomes, uncertainty, harms and limits. Do not diagnose, prescribe or imply that education replaces professional care.
+
+**Source types to locate:** current official clinical or public-health guidance; original trials, cohort studies or diagnostic-validation reports; study registrations, protocols and adverse-event records. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-068-034 | How can owners record a change in behavior so a veterinarian receives useful information? | Document onset, context, frequency, appetite and existing medications without collecting unnecessary identifying data; avoid interpreting a diary as a diagnosis. |
+| BANK-068-035 | Why can advice for a dog be dangerous when copied for another species? | Investigate documented differences in metabolism, husbandry and medication approval through veterinary authorities; exclude dosing guidance or experiments with household products. |
+| BANK-068-036 | What does a pet health screening result mean before further assessment? | Review test purpose, population, false results and clinical context with authoritative veterinary sources; do not make individual treatment or prognosis claims. |
+| BANK-068-037 | How should claims for supplements or alternative pet therapies be evaluated? | Inspect species, dose studied, comparator, outcomes, harms and regulatory status in original research; keep evidence appraisal separate from a recommendation to administer anything. |
+
+## 07. Aging, access and difficult decisions
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use consented participant accounts, local records and the community's own descriptions. Include differences of position, access and power; testimony is not a representative survey or clinical proof.
+
+**Source types to locate:** consented interviews and oral histories; community publications and local records; field notes with method and consent documented. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-068-038 | How can a household adapt routines as a companion animal ages? | Compare access, rest, toileting, observation and veterinary review using species-specific welfare guidance; avoid presenting age alone as a diagnosis of reduced quality of life. |
+| BANK-068-039 | What can a quality-of-life discussion with a veterinarian include? | Explore comfort, daily function, changing needs and caregiver observations with professional sources; exclude remote determinations about euthanasia or an individual animal's prognosis. |
+| BANK-068-040 | How can pet-care costs be planned without implying predictable medical spending? | Separate routine care, contingencies, insurance terms and local service availability using illustrative figures; verify current contracts before discussing coverage and exclusions. |
+| BANK-068-041 | What supports might matter to someone grieving a companion animal? | Use consented experiences and recognized support resources to explore differing grief responses; avoid fixed recovery timelines or treating a new pet as a prescribed solution. |
+
+## 08. Responsible ownership and public life
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-068-042 | How do identification, registration and microchipping serve different purposes? | Consult the exact jurisdiction's current rules and official registry procedures; distinguish proof of identity, legal duties and contact-data maintenance. |
+| BANK-068-043 | What makes responsible rehoming more than placing an advertisement? | Consider welfare assessment, honest history, appropriate organizations and local law; exclude secret transfers, misleading descriptions or assuming payment proves suitability. |
+| BANK-068-044 | How can pet content avoid rewarding behavior that causes distress? | Audit staging, restraint, anthropomorphic captions and audience incentives in permission-cleared examples; favor documented welfare indicators over viral appeal. |
+| BANK-068-045 | Why should a pet-content creator distinguish service, assistance and ordinary companion roles? | Use current official definitions for a named jurisdiction and attributed owner perspectives; avoid universal access claims or instructions for misrepresenting an animal's status. |
