+| BANK-073-019 | How should feeding-content claims account for infant age and individual medical needs? | Use current authoritative infant-feeding guidance, study populations and explicit limits; exclude individualized schedules, formula modifications or treatment recommendations. |
+| BANK-073-020 | Why can caregiver exhaustion be a family-support issue rather than a failure of discipline? | Explore care distribution, practical support and access through consented accounts; keep infant sleep-safety guidance authoritative and avoid diagnosing or prescribing sleep interventions. |
+
+## 03. Play, language and early learning
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-073-021 | How can pretend play be studied without assuming every imagined scene teaches the same skill? | Compare operational definitions, adult involvement and measured developmental outcomes; distinguish enjoyable play from proven transfer to unrelated abilities. |
+| BANK-073-022 | What can shared reading offer beyond recognizing printed words? | Investigate conversation, joint attention, vocabulary and child participation in original studies; account for language and access without guaranteeing reading attainment. |
+| BANK-073-023 | Why should bilingual language development be discussed separately from myths about confusion? | Consult current professional guidance and original multilingual-development research; distinguish language exposure, assessment language and conditions needing qualified evaluation. |
+| BANK-073-024 | How can a caregiver choose play materials without equating expense with learning value? | Compare open-ended use, age appropriateness, safety standards and the child's engagement; verify product suitability rather than endorsing a toy category universally. |
+| BANK-073-025 | What changes when an adult follows a child's interest during a learning activity? | Review scaffolding, autonomy and task goals with original observational or experimental evidence; avoid claiming that unstructured choice replaces all adult guidance. |
+
+## 04. Routines, emotions and behavior
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-073-026 | How can co-regulation be distinguished from expecting a young child to manage distress alone? | Use qualified developmental sources to describe adult support and gradual skill development; exclude restraint, punitive demonstrations and promises of immediate calm. |
+| BANK-073-027 | Why can the same behavior have different meanings across settings? | Compare task demand, sensory conditions, fatigue and communication in fictional cases; avoid assigning a diagnosis or intention from a single action. |
+| BANK-073-028 | What makes a household limit understandable and consistent for a child? | Explore concrete expectations, predictable responses and caregiver coordination using nonviolent examples; do not turn the topic into a universal discipline prescription. |
+| BANK-073-029 | How can transitions between activities be investigated before labeling a child defiant? | Map warning cues, language demands, motivation and environmental context; retain medical or developmental concerns for qualified assessment. |
+| BANK-073-030 | What does research say about praise when effort, strategy and outcome are described differently? | Inspect original study wording, participant age and later tasks; avoid a blanket rule that one phrase reliably changes every child's motivation. |
+
+## 05. School years and peer life
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use consented participant accounts, local records and the community's own descriptions. Include differences of position, access and power; testimony is not a representative survey or clinical proof.
+
+**Source types to locate:** consented interviews and oral histories; community publications and local records; field notes with method and consent documented. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-073-031 | How can caregivers learn about a school difficulty without interrogating the child? | Compare open questions, teacher communication and specific observations in a fictional scenario; preserve trust and distinguish a child's report from a completed investigation. |
+| BANK-073-032 | What should a bullying episode distinguish from ordinary disagreement? | Use current official safeguarding definitions, patterns, power and reporting routes for a named school system; exclude retaliation advice or identification of minors. |
+| BANK-073-033 | How can homework routines reflect the child's actual learning and access needs? | Map instructions, materials, breaks, language and caregiver availability; separate organizational support from doing assessed work for the child. |
+| BANK-073-034 | Why can friendship changes in school-age children require several perspectives? | Use consented professional or anonymized accounts of inclusion, conflict and context; avoid labeling a child from peer descriptions or prescribing forced friendship. |
+
+## 06. Adolescence, privacy and growing autonomy
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Inspect current professional guidance and original studies for the exact population, intervention or measurement. Report comparators, absolute outcomes, uncertainty, harms and limits. Do not diagnose, prescribe or imply that education replaces professional care.
+
+**Source types to locate:** current official clinical or public-health guidance; original trials, cohort studies or diagnostic-validation reports; study registrations, protocols and adverse-event records. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-073-035 | How can adolescence be explained without reducing every disagreement to hormones? | Compare biological development, social context and increasing autonomy using original research; avoid deterministic brain-age claims or diagnosing normal variation. |
+| BANK-073-036 | What should a family discuss before introducing a smartphone or online account? | Review platform age rules, contacts, spending, data, reporting and shared expectations; use dated official information and do not imply monitoring eliminates harm. |
+| BANK-073-037 | How can caregivers balance privacy with a specific safeguarding concern? | Consult current professional guidance, proportionality and local obligations; use fictional cases and exclude secret surveillance instructions. |
+| BANK-073-038 | What can help a parent listen when a teenager describes a value or identity differently from family expectations? | Use consented perspectives on respect, questions and autonomy; avoid conversion aims, diagnosis or treating difference itself as a safety problem. |
+
+## 07. Disability, family resources and support
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use consented participant accounts, local records and the community's own descriptions. Include differences of position, access and power; testimony is not a representative survey or clinical proof.
+
+**Source types to locate:** consented interviews and oral histories; community publications and local records; field notes with method and consent documented. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-073-039 | How can a parenting episode include disability without treating one child as a universal example? | Center consent, access needs, qualified sources and differences within diagnoses; distinguish family experience from clinical or educational recommendations. |
+| BANK-073-040 | What changes when caregiving is shared across separated households? | Explore communication, predictable routines and child privacy using neutral fictional cases; keep custody, safety and legal issues jurisdiction-specific. |
+| BANK-073-041 | How can extended family support be discussed without assuming it is available or uncomplicated? | Compare time, distance, resources, boundaries and cultural expectations through consented accounts; avoid shaming families who rely on different arrangements. |
+| BANK-073-042 | Why should advice about parental involvement account for shift work, income and language access? | Use official time-use or education data and ethical accounts; distinguish barriers from lack of care and avoid a single model of the ideal parent. |
+
+## 08. Research, rights and parent-media ethics
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Define the rival claims and their evidence standards. Compare sources on equal terms, separate empirical findings from values or interpretations and do not imply that all positions have equal evidential support.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the original sources behind the compared claims; explicit definitions and measurement methods; independent critiques with traceable evidence. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-073-043 | How can a parenting claim be assessed when it is supported mainly by personal success stories? | Compare original evidence, confounding, selection and the promised outcome; avoid blaming caregivers for results outside their control. |
+| BANK-073-044 | What should be checked before uploading a child's image or personal story? | Consider privacy, assent, future identifiability, platform rules and current local law; do not assume guardian permission resolves every ethical or legal issue. |
+| BANK-073-045 | How can an episode cover child screen use without treating all screen activities as identical? | Separate content, age, context, displacement and co-use in current official guidance and original studies; avoid unsupported universal causal claims. |
+| BANK-073-046 | What evidence is needed before claiming a parenting method caused a later adult outcome? | Examine longitudinal design, confounding, retrospective reports and uncertainty; distinguish association from proof and avoid assigning blame to an individual family. |
