+# 074 Personal Travel: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 30 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Personal Travel. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Defining a personal trip
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Use dated official access rules, operator schedules and local information. Separate general planning from time-sensitive availability; include accessibility, local perspectives and changing environmental conditions.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official transport operators and destination authorities; current permit, visa and access rules; consented local accounts and independently verified travel costs. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-074-013 | How can a traveler turn a vague destination wish into a workable trip brief? | Specify purpose, dates, pace, budget assumptions, access needs and companions; distinguish preferences from verified availability and leave current bookings for dated checks. |
+| BANK-074-014 | What changes when a trip is planned around one base rather than several stops? | Compare transfer time, luggage, accommodation changes and flexibility using an illustrative itinerary; avoid claiming one pace suits all travelers. |
+| BANK-074-015 | How can a destination shortlist account for more than famous attractions? | Compare season, transport, local daily life, accessibility and permitted activities from official sources; do not turn undated impressions into current recommendations. |
+
+## 02. Budgets, bookings and consumer terms
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use current official terms, filings or macroeconomic data; state jurisdiction, date, fees, taxes, risk, units and assumptions. Use transparent illustrative calculations rather than personal recommendations or return promises.
+
+**Source types to locate:** regulator or official statistical publications; actual contracts, fee schedules and audited filings; original economic studies and disclosed calculation inputs. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-074-016 | How can a travel budget include costs that do not appear in the advertised fare? | Map taxes, baggage, transfers, meals, deposits and currency fees using illustrative figures; require dated official terms before presenting actual prices. |
+| BANK-074-017 | What should be compared between refundable and nonrefundable bookings? | Inspect cancellation deadlines, no-show rules, change fees and refund method for named terms; distinguish flexibility from guaranteed recovery under every disruption. |
+| BANK-074-018 | Why can booking through an intermediary change how a disruption is handled? | Trace contracting parties, support routes, payment and ticket conditions using current official policies; avoid assuming the platform or operator always bears the same obligation. |
+| BANK-074-019 | How can a traveler assess an insurance policy without treating its title as complete coverage? | Read jurisdiction, exclusions, limits, existing-condition rules and claims procedure; use official policy text and exclude individualized insurance advice. |
+
+## 03. Routes, documents and timing
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use dated official access rules, operator schedules and local information. Separate general planning from time-sensitive availability; include accessibility, local perspectives and changing environmental conditions.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official transport operators and destination authorities; current permit, visa and access rules; consented local accounts and independently verified travel costs. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-074-020 | What does a realistic connection need beyond the timetable's arrival and departure times? | Include terminal changes, immigration, baggage, accessibility and operator rules; distinguish official minimum connection data from a promise the transfer will succeed. |
+| BANK-074-021 | How can a traveler verify entry and transit requirements for a specific passport? | Use current destination and transit authorities, route details and document validity; do not infer eligibility from nationality alone or repeat cached visa advice. |
+| BANK-074-022 | Why should rail or bus planning include station location and the final local transfer? | Map official stops, operating dates, arrival hours and last-mile access; separate scheduled service from live availability and include fallback planning. |
+| BANK-074-023 | What can a traveler prepare when mobile data or navigation fails? | Compare permitted offline maps, saved booking details, local contacts and power options; avoid exposing private itineraries or suggesting unauthorized access to networks. |
+
+## 04. Accommodation and everyday logistics
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use dated official access rules, operator schedules and local information. Separate general planning from time-sensitive availability; include accessibility, local perspectives and changing environmental conditions.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official transport operators and destination authorities; current permit, visa and access rules; consented local accounts and independently verified travel costs. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-074-024 | How can accommodation photos be checked against the details needed for a particular stay? | Verify exact room type, access, bathroom, noise disclosures and current property information; distinguish promotional images from confirmed facilities. |
+| BANK-074-025 | What should an accessibility inquiry ask beyond whether a property is accessible? | Define the traveler's specific measurements, routes, equipment and assistance needs; obtain direct current confirmation without assuming a symbol covers every disability. |
+| BANK-074-026 | How can food preferences and allergies be discussed during trip planning? | Separate preference from medically important requirements using professional guidance and verified provider communication; avoid promising that a translation card or label eliminates risk. |
+| BANK-074-027 | Why can luggage choices affect transport access as much as airline fees? | Compare carrying distances, steps, storage and handling capacity through a route plan; verify operator size rules and avoid universal packing prescriptions. |
+
+## 05. Different travelers and changing needs
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use consented participant accounts, local records and the community's own descriptions. Include differences of position, access and power; testimony is not a representative survey or clinical proof.
+
+**Source types to locate:** consented interviews and oral histories; community publications and local records; field notes with method and consent documented. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-074-028 | How does a solo trip change communication, decisions and social opportunities? | Use consented traveler accounts with differing access needs and experiences; avoid gender stereotypes or implying a checklist guarantees personal safety. |
+| BANK-074-029 | What should travel companions agree on before sharing bookings? | Discuss spending, pace, privacy, cancellations and decision authority through an original scenario; distinguish an informal agreement from legal enforceability. |
+| BANK-074-030 | How can family travel account for children's routines without making every day child-centered? | Compare rest, food, transport and caregiver workloads in an illustrative plan; verify current age rules and exclude individualized health recommendations. |
+| BANK-074-031 | What changes when older travelers have specific mobility or medical-access requirements? | Start from individual preferences, professional care planning and confirmed local facilities; avoid treating age alone as evidence of limitation or fitness to travel. |
+
+## 06. Health, disruption and contingency
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Inspect current professional guidance and original studies for the exact population, intervention or measurement. Report comparators, absolute outcomes, uncertainty, harms and limits. Do not diagnose, prescribe or imply that education replaces professional care.
+
+**Source types to locate:** current official clinical or public-health guidance; original trials, cohort studies or diagnostic-validation reports; study registrations, protocols and adverse-event records. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-074-032 | What travel-health questions need professional advice before departure? | Use current official destination guidance on immunization, medication access and relevant exposures; provide a research checklist rather than personal prophylaxis or treatment instructions. |
+| BANK-074-033 | How can a traveler prepare for heat, altitude or environmental conditions without overestimating fitness? | Review destination-specific official health guidance, warning information and access to care; exclude acclimatization prescriptions or reassurance based on age or exercise history. |
+| BANK-074-034 | What records can help resolve a cancellation or lost-baggage claim? | Map receipts, booking terms, incident reports and deadlines under current operator and jurisdiction rules; avoid guaranteeing compensation or sharing real identity documents. |
+| BANK-074-035 | How should a trip plan change when official warnings or local closures appear? | Trace verified authority updates, operator changes and cancellation options for the actual place and date; distinguish cautionary planning from predicting events. |
+
+## 07. Local relationships and responsible visits
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use consented participant accounts, local records and the community's own descriptions. Include differences of position, access and power; testimony is not a representative survey or clinical proof.
+
+**Source types to locate:** consented interviews and oral histories; community publications and local records; field notes with method and consent documented. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-074-036 | How can a traveler learn site etiquette without treating a community as one uniform culture? | Use local authorities, hosts and consented accounts for dress, photography and conduct; attribute differences and respect restricted spaces. |
+| BANK-074-037 | What makes a wildlife-tourism activity require welfare checks beyond a good review score? | Inspect operator permits, species-specific authority guidance, handling practices and transparent policies; avoid encouraging direct contact or assuming rescue branding proves welfare. |
+| BANK-074-038 | How can a visitor's spending be examined for local benefit without invented impact claims? | Trace ownership, labor, supply and fees through verifiable records and consented accounts; distinguish a plausible local purchase from measured community-wide outcomes. |
+| BANK-074-039 | What should be considered before geotagging a small or environmentally sensitive place? | Check access restrictions, landowner consent, ecological sensitivity and local views; avoid disclosing protected locations or presenting obscurity as permission to enter. |
+
+## 08. After the trip and evidence literacy
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-074-040 | How can a personal travel review remain useful after prices and access change? | Label travel date, tested conditions, subjective judgments and details requiring recheck; avoid presenting a past experience as current verified service. |
+| BANK-074-041 | What should a traveler disclose when a hotel stay or excursion was sponsored? | Identify payment, gifts, editorial access and applicable disclosure requirements; separate commercial relationships from evidence about the experience. |
+| BANK-074-042 | How can photos and stories be shared without exposing other travelers or local residents? | Plan consent, anonymization and removal of identifying information; distinguish permission to take a photo from permission for monetized publication. |
