+# 075 Pilgrimage & Sacred Places: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**9 categories · 37 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Pilgrimage & Sacred Places. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Sacred geography and attributed meaning
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Identify the text, edition, translator, commentary, community and ritual context as applicable. Attribute theological and spiritual claims, preserve regional variation and respect public versus restricted knowledge.
+
+**Source types to locate:** primary texts in traceable editions; authorized public teaching and ritual descriptions; consented community accounts and critical historical studies. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-075-013 | How does a place become understood as a pilgrimage destination within a named tradition? | Identify primary narratives, ritual use, patronage and living community accounts; distinguish devotional meaning from historical evidence and avoid ranking sacred authenticity. |
+| BANK-075-014 | What is the difference between visiting a shrine and undertaking a pilgrimage as participants describe it? | Compare intention, route, preparation and communal practice through attributed public teachings and consented accounts; do not decide whose journey counts as spiritually valid. |
+| BANK-075-015 | How can a sacred-place episode keep origin narratives separate from construction history? | Present traceable text editions, inscriptions, archaeology and oral traditions in labeled strands; avoid either flattening theology into fact or dismissing it as failed chronology. |
+| BANK-075-016 | Why can the same sacred landscape hold meaning for more than one community? | Use attributed histories and current public accounts to examine overlapping use and memory; distinguish perspectives and refrain from adjudicating ownership through mythic narrative. |
+
+## 02. Indian pilgrimage circuits
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Identify the text, edition, translator, commentary, community and ritual context as applicable. Attribute theological and spiritual claims, preserve regional variation and respect public versus restricted knowledge.
+
+**Source types to locate:** primary texts in traceable editions; authorized public teaching and ritual descriptions; consented community accounts and critical historical studies. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-075-017 | How do the Himalayan Char Dham and the wider four-dham circuit differ? | Verify named sites, historical usage and tradition-specific descriptions through authorized sources; distinguish circuits without assuming all pilgrims use the terms identically. |
+| BANK-075-018 | What makes the twelve Jyotirlinga traditions more than a list of temple locations? | Compare traceable textual accounts, public liturgy, regional identification and site histories; attribute contested identifications and avoid asserting every modern structure has the same antiquity. |
+| BANK-075-019 | How do Shakti Peetha lists vary across texts and regional traditions? | Identify editions, names, numbers and local identifications with attributed commentary; preserve variation instead of manufacturing one universally accepted map. |
+| BANK-075-020 | What organizes a Varkari pilgrimage to Pandharpur beyond the final temple visit? | Study publicly documented dindis, songs, walking rhythms, service and participant perspectives; verify terminology and avoid portraying a living tradition as only a spectacle. |
+| BANK-075-021 | How does the Panch Kedar circuit connect narrative, terrain and public ritual? | Use identified narrative sources, temple authorities and historical records; distinguish sacred explanation from geological or archaeological claims. |
+| BANK-075-022 | How can the Narmada Parikrama be explained without turning one itinerary into a universal rule? | Compare attributed practices, route variation, season, public guidance and participant accounts; respect restricted knowledge and defer current access to local authorities. |
+
+## 03. Sacred rivers, water and landscape
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Identify the text, edition, translator, commentary, community and ritual context as applicable. Attribute theological and spiritual claims, preserve regional variation and respect public versus restricted knowledge.
+
+**Source types to locate:** primary texts in traceable editions; authorized public teaching and ritual descriptions; consented community accounts and critical historical studies. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-075-023 | What meanings do public sources attach to a tirtha as a crossing place? | Examine selected Sanskrit passages, reliable translations and public ritual contexts; separate lexical interpretation from a claim that every water site has identical significance. |
+| BANK-075-024 | How can a documentary discuss bathing at a sacred river while distinguishing devotion from water-quality evidence? | Attribute religious meaning and separately use dated official environmental and health records; do not infer safety or purification of pollutants from spiritual claims. |
+| BANK-075-025 | What does a confluence's pilgrimage history reveal about changing river channels and settlement? | Combine maps, dated records, archaeology and attributed sacred narratives for one site; keep reconstructed geography and devotional identification distinct. |
+| BANK-075-026 | How can a mountain be filmed as a sacred landscape without reducing it to an adventure destination? | Center attributed religious relationships, access restrictions and local stewardship; exclude unsafe route advice or entry to prohibited areas. |
+
+## 04. Shrines, ritual and public access
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Identify the text, edition, translator, commentary, community and ritual context as applicable. Attribute theological and spiritual claims, preserve regional variation and respect public versus restricted knowledge.
+
+**Source types to locate:** primary texts in traceable editions; authorized public teaching and ritual descriptions; consented community accounts and critical historical studies. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-075-027 | How do public temple routines shape a pilgrim's experience across a day? | Consult the exact institution's public guidance on openings, offerings, queues and services; distinguish observation from restricted ritual instruction and recheck current arrangements. |
+| BANK-075-028 | What does prasad mean in a specific shrine's public practice? | Use authorized descriptions and consented participant accounts to distinguish offering, distribution and devotional significance; keep food-allergy and hygiene questions evidence-based and separate. |
+| BANK-075-029 | Why can dress, photography and entry rules differ between sacred sites? | Read official site-specific requirements and attributed explanations; avoid assuming a national custom or another temple's policy applies everywhere. |
+| BANK-075-030 | How can the roles of priests, volunteers and pilgrims be described without collapsing them into one experience? | Use consented interviews and public institutional records on responsibilities, access and service; attribute disagreements and respect restrictions on filming. |
+
+## 05. Buddhist, Jain and Sikh pilgrimage contexts
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Identify the text, edition, translator, commentary, community and ritual context as applicable. Attribute theological and spiritual claims, preserve regional variation and respect public versus restricted knowledge.
+
+**Source types to locate:** primary texts in traceable editions; authorized public teaching and ritual descriptions; consented community accounts and critical historical studies. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-075-031 | How can the four major Buddhist pilgrimage places be discussed across different Buddhist communities? | Identify canonical references, historical site development and contemporary attributed practices; avoid assuming a single ritual itinerary or interpretation across schools. |
+| BANK-075-032 | What does Jain pilgrimage to a tirtha reveal about ethics, community and public ritual? | Choose a named site, identify tradition-specific authorities and document public practices; preserve sectarian differences and avoid filming restricted observances. |
+| BANK-075-033 | How can a film explain Takhts and other important Sikh pilgrimage sites without equating all institutions? | Use authorized Sikh sources, institutional histories and consented accounts; distinguish religious roles, visitor conduct and current access rather than inventing a uniform temple model. |
+| BANK-075-034 | How do diaspora communities create meaningful pilgrimage or sacred visits far from historic centers? | Study one documented institution through founding records, attributed teachings and participant perspectives; avoid treating diaspora practice as automatically less authentic. |
+
+## 06. Comparative sacred journeys
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Identify the text, edition, translator, commentary, community and ritual context as applicable. Attribute theological and spiritual claims, preserve regional variation and respect public versus restricted knowledge.
+
+**Source types to locate:** primary texts in traceable editions; authorized public teaching and ritual descriptions; consented community accounts and critical historical studies. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-075-035 | How can Hajj and Umrah be distinguished respectfully in a comparative travel documentary? | Use authorized Islamic public sources on purpose, timing and participation; verify current official access rules and exclude restricted-site filming or practical claims beyond verified guidance. |
+| BANK-075-036 | What changed as the Camino de Santiago became a route for travelers with varied motivations? | Compare historical records, current pilgrim institutions and consented accounts; distinguish devotional continuity, tourism and personal meaning without deciding who is a true pilgrim. |
+| BANK-075-037 | How can a dargah's visitation practices be described without assuming every Muslim community shares the same view? | Use site authorities, attributed teachings and participant accounts; preserve theological differences and avoid converting one local practice into a universal rule. |
+
+## 07. Accessibility, care and changing conditions
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use dated official access rules, operator schedules and local information. Separate general planning from time-sensitive availability; include accessibility, local perspectives and changing environmental conditions.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official transport operators and destination authorities; current permit, visa and access rules; consented local accounts and independently verified travel costs. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
