+# 076 World Religions & Civilization: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**9 categories · 37 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for World Religions & Civilization. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Studying religion and civilization
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-076-013 | How can a history of religion distinguish practitioners' explanations from a historian's reconstruction? | Separate attributed theology, dated material evidence and scholarly interpretation in one bounded example; do not rank traditions by spiritual truth. |
+| BANK-076-014 | What changes when religion is studied through households rather than only founders and rulers? | Compare domestic objects, calendars, oral accounts and institutional records; acknowledge whose everyday practices were not preserved in archives. |
+| BANK-076-015 | Why can a modern religious label obscure differences across regions and periods? | Trace the changing use of one term in primary texts and community descriptions; avoid projecting present boundaries unchanged into antiquity. |
+| BANK-076-016 | How can a civilization documentary discuss nonreligious people alongside religious institutions? | Examine available self-descriptions, law, education and public debate for a named setting; distinguish secular administration, skepticism and absence of personal belief. |
+
+## 02. South Asian traditions and textual worlds
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Identify the text, edition, translator, commentary, community and ritual context as applicable. Attribute theological and spiritual claims, preserve regional variation and respect public versus restricted knowledge.
+
+**Source types to locate:** primary texts in traceable editions; authorized public teaching and ritual descriptions; consented community accounts and critical historical studies. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-076-017 | How do selected Upanishadic and Bhagavad Gita passages frame liberation differently? | Choose traceable editions and attributed commentaries on a bounded concept; preserve interpretive differences rather than constructing a single doctrine from isolated quotations. |
+| BANK-076-018 | What can Buddhist monastic rules reveal about institutions beyond meditation? | Compare identified Vinaya passages, translation context and historical evidence for communal life; do not assume every Buddhist school followed identical arrangements. |
+| BANK-076-019 | How do Jain accounts of nonviolence distinguish lay and monastic obligations? | Use authorized public teachings and traceable textual sources; preserve sectarian and historical differences and exclude instructions for restricted observances. |
+| BANK-076-020 | What does the public institution of langar reveal about Sikh religious and community life? | Use authorized Sikh sources, institutional records and consented accounts of service; avoid assuming each gurdwara operates identically or treating religious meaning as a nutritional claim. |
+| BANK-076-021 | How can bhakti literature be studied without assuming all poets shared one theology? | Compare selected attributed works, languages, devotional audiences and commentary traditions; verify authorship questions and distinguish poetic voice from biography. |
+
+## 03. West Asian religious histories
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Identify the text, edition, translator, commentary, community and ritual context as applicable. Attribute theological and spiritual claims, preserve regional variation and respect public versus restricted knowledge.
+
+**Source types to locate:** primary texts in traceable editions; authorized public teaching and ritual descriptions; consented community accounts and critical historical studies. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-076-022 | How can the history of the Hebrew Bible be distinguished from the histories narrated within it? | Compare manuscript evidence, canon formation and attributed Jewish interpretations with historical scholarship; do not present literary chronology as independently verified event chronology. |
+| BANK-076-023 | What does a comparison of Christian biblical canons reveal about institutions and authority? | Use authorized lists, council records where relevant and denomination-specific accounts; avoid treating one canon as an undisputed universal baseline. |
+| BANK-076-024 | How can the Quran's recitation tradition be described alongside its manuscript history? | Use authorized public Islamic sources, traceable manuscripts and attributed scholarship; distinguish devotional claims, performance practice and historical inference. |
+| BANK-076-025 | What can Zoroastrian textual transmission reveal about continuity across diaspora communities? | Identify editions, languages, community accounts and archival evidence; respect restricted ritual knowledge and avoid reconstructing lost practices as fact. |
+
+## 04. East Asian traditions and exchange
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Identify the text, edition, translator, commentary, community and ritual context as applicable. Attribute theological and spiritual claims, preserve regional variation and respect public versus restricted knowledge.
+
+**Source types to locate:** primary texts in traceable editions; authorized public teaching and ritual descriptions; consented community accounts and critical historical studies. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-076-026 | How did Buddhist translation choices affect the meanings available to Chinese readers? | Compare an identified Sanskrit or Pali term with traceable Chinese renderings and commentaries; do not assume a single translation resolved every conceptual difference. |
+| BANK-076-027 | How can Daoist texts and organized Daoist communities be studied without treating them as identical categories? | Compare selected public texts, institutional histories and attributed practices; avoid reducing living traditions to a few philosophical aphorisms. |
+| BANK-076-028 | What does the changing role of Confucian academies reveal about learning and state service? | Use records from a specified dynasty, curriculum evidence and attributed scholarship; distinguish ethical teaching, examination systems and individual belief. |
+| BANK-076-029 | How can Shinto shrine history be explained without assuming an unchanged national tradition? | Trace one site's records, changing institutions and public ritual descriptions; separate modern administrative categories from earlier local practices. |
+
+## 05. African, Indigenous and oral traditions
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use consented participant accounts, local records and the community's own descriptions. Include differences of position, access and power; testimony is not a representative survey or clinical proof.
+
+**Source types to locate:** consented interviews and oral histories; community publications and local records; field notes with method and consent documented. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-076-030 | What does an oral religious account require from a researcher beyond transcription? | Plan consent, speaker authority, language context, performance and permitted circulation; do not equate a public telling with unrestricted rights to sacred knowledge. |
+| BANK-076-031 | How can Yoruba religious traditions be discussed across African and Atlantic settings? | Use community-authorized accounts, identifiable histories and local terminology; preserve differences rather than portraying diaspora traditions as interchangeable copies. |
+| BANK-076-032 | Why can a museum label misrepresent an object removed from its ceremonial setting? | Trace provenance, acquisition, community interpretation and display history for a named object; exclude restricted images and avoid assigning one fixed meaning to all similar forms. |
+| BANK-076-033 | How can a documentary compare ancestor-related practices without calling them all the same religion? | Identify distinct communities, sources, relationships and public observances; attribute categories and avoid imposing an outsider's taxonomy as the participants' own. |
+
+## 06. Institutions, economies and material culture
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-076-034 | How did monasteries participate in education, landholding and local economies in one region? | Use dated charters, accounts, archaeology and institutional records; distinguish specific holdings from claims that every monastery functioned alike. |
+| BANK-076-035 | What can a temple or mosque's donation records reveal about patronage beyond royal support? | Compare named donors, objects, dates and gaps in surviving evidence; do not infer an entire community's wealth or devotion from recorded gifts. |
+| BANK-076-036 | How did printing alter the circulation and interpretation of religious texts? | Trace a specific edition, language, readership and distribution network; separate technological possibility from documented changes in authority or practice. |
+| BANK-076-037 | Why do religious calendars require attention to local reckoning and historical reform? | Compare official or community-authorized calendar rules in two named settings; verify current dates separately and avoid treating similar festival timing as proof of shared origin. |
+
+## 07. Conversion, migration and encounter
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-076-038 | How can conversion be investigated without reducing every case to either coercion or free choice? | Use bounded historical accounts, law, social conditions and participant testimony where available; preserve mixed motives and avoid generalizing from one region. |
+| BANK-076-039 | What does a translation controversy reveal about religion moving across languages? | Choose a documented debate with primary statements from participants; distinguish linguistic, political and theological objections rather than treating translation as mechanical transfer. |
+| BANK-076-040 | How did religious communities organize support along a particular trade route? | Compare inscriptions, travel accounts, buildings and commercial records; distinguish evidence of contact from proof of widespread conversion. |
+| BANK-076-041 | What changes when migrants recreate religious institutions in a new legal environment? | Study one documented community's registration, space, language and public life; include multiple generations without assuming inevitable assimilation. |
+
+## 08. Law, power and pluralism
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-076-042 | How can a documentary distinguish a state's official religion from its citizens' religious lives? | Compare the legal text, institutions, census methods and consented accounts for a named period and jurisdiction; avoid treating constitutional wording as complete implementation. |
+| BANK-076-043 | What did a specific historical toleration policy permit and still restrict? | Read the policy alongside enforcement records, exceptions and affected accounts; do not project present-day rights language uncritically onto earlier institutions. |
+| BANK-076-044 | How should religious demographic figures be compared across different census systems? | Inspect definitions, nonresponse, classification, coverage and dates; distinguish affiliation, practice and belief rather than treating them as the same measure. |
+| BANK-076-045 | What makes a heritage dispute different from a theological disagreement? | Separate property law, conservation, historical claims and religious interpretation using official records; avoid adjudicating living disputes from visual resemblance or one narrative. |
+
+## 09. Comparison, media and contested claims
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Define the rival claims and their evidence standards. Compare sources on equal terms, separate empirical findings from values or interpretations and do not imply that all positions have equal evidential support.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the original sources behind the compared claims; explicit definitions and measurement methods; independent critiques with traceable evidence. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-076-046 | When does comparing flood narratives reveal literary differences rather than a shared historical event? | Read traceable versions, dates, transmission hypotheses and scholarly arguments; distinguish narrative motifs from independently established disaster evidence. |
+| BANK-076-047 | How can claims that all religions teach the same thing be evaluated without ranking them? | Define the compared ethical, theological or ritual question and use attributed sources; preserve genuine disagreement and avoid manufacturing equivalence from translated keywords. |
+| BANK-076-048 | What evidence is needed before calling a symbol a borrowing between religions? | Establish chronology, provenance, meaning and plausible contact; keep parallel invention and missing evidence visible instead of equating resemblance with transmission. |
+| BANK-076-049 | How can a documentary correct a widely shared quotation misattributed to a religious teacher? | Trace earliest accessible attestations, editions and quotation chains; distinguish an unlocated source from proof that a teacher never expressed a related idea. |
