+# 078 Books & Literature: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 34 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Books & Literature. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Reading form and narrative
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-078-013 | How can a close reading make a literary claim visible in the actual words? | Annotate a short permission-cleared passage for diction, syntax and pattern; distinguish textual observation from interpretation and keep quotation within rights limits. |
+| BANK-078-014 | What changes when a narrator knows less than the reader? | Use original miniature stories to compare limited knowledge, dramatic irony and reliability; avoid treating the narrator as automatically identical to the author. |
+| BANK-078-015 | How do plot chronology and the order of telling create different questions? | Map an original story's event sequence against its presentation; identify withheld information without claiming all nonlinear writing produces the same effect. |
+| BANK-078-016 | Why can a recurring image matter without becoming a secret code? | Trace its contexts, variations and competing readings within one work; avoid assigning a universal symbolic meaning from an isolated appearance. |
+
+## 02. Poetry and performance
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-078-017 | How does a sonnet's structure shape an argument rather than simply count lines? | Compare a public-domain Shakespeare sonnet with a clearly labeled original example; inspect turns, syntax and sound without treating every sonnet as identical. |
+| BANK-078-018 | What changes when a Hindi poem is heard aloud rather than read silently? | Choose a rights-cleared text and attributed performance to compare pause, rhythm and emphasis; distinguish performer choices from the author's verified intent. |
+| BANK-078-019 | How can free verse organize rhythm without a regular metrical scheme? | Annotate repetition, line breaks, stress and phrasing in an authorized poem; do not equate absence of fixed meter with absence of craft. |
+| BANK-078-020 | Why does a poem's speaker require separate analysis from the poet's biography? | Compare textual voice, publication context and documented biography in a named work; avoid treating a lyric confession as a verified life event. |
+
+## 03. Drama, dialogue and character
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-078-021 | How does a soliloquy in Hamlet change the audience's access to a character? | Examine an identified scene, edition and performance choices; distinguish textual possibilities from claims about Shakespeare's private psychology. |
+| BANK-078-022 | What can subtext reveal when dialogue says less than the situation implies? | Analyze an original two-character exchange through stakes, action and silence; avoid using fictional dialogue as evidence about real people's hidden motives. |
+| BANK-078-023 | Why can staging the same dramatic scene produce different interpretations? | Compare permission-cleared performances for blocking, pace, costume and omissions; keep the play text distinct from a director's adaptation. |
+| BANK-078-024 | How does a minor character's viewpoint alter a familiar story's moral focus? | Develop a clearly labeled original retelling and compare access to events; do not present invented scenes as missing material from the source work. |
+
+## 04. Indian prose, regional literatures and social worlds
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-078-025 | What can Godaan reveal about the social world represented in Premchand's fiction? | Use a traceable Hindi edition, translation context and historical scholarship; distinguish narrative representation from a statistical portrait of rural India. |
+| BANK-078-026 | How do editions of Gitanjali change the text a reader is actually encountering? | Identify language, selection, translation and publication history using bibliographic records; avoid assuming English and Bengali volumes are word-for-word equivalents. |
+| BANK-078-027 | How can a Dalit autobiography be read as both literary form and situated testimony? | Choose an authorized work with attributed critical perspectives and historical context; preserve the writer's voice without treating one experience as universal. |
+| BANK-078-028 | What does a regional-language short story lose when discussed only through a plot summary? | Compare narrative voice, idiom, social address and form in a named authorized text; disclose translation limits rather than inventing untranslatable meanings. |
+| BANK-078-029 | How can oral storytelling and a printed retelling preserve different relationships with an audience? | Use consented performance and a traceable publication; examine variation, ownership and context without ranking print as the more authentic version. |
+
+## 05. World literature and comparative reading
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Define the rival claims and their evidence standards. Compare sources on equal terms, separate empirical findings from values or interpretations and do not imply that all positions have equal evidential support.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the original sources behind the compared claims; explicit definitions and measurement methods; independent critiques with traceable evidence. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-078-030 | How does The Odyssey organize homecoming through recognition and delay? | Analyze selected episodes in a named translation with attributed scholarship; distinguish epic convention, historical context and later adaptations. |
+| BANK-078-031 | What changes when a reader approaches Don Quixote as a book about reading? | Trace selected scenes, narrative levels and genre references in a specified edition; avoid reducing the novel to a single moral about imagination. |
+| BANK-078-032 | How can a comparison of Things Fall Apart and a colonial account avoid treating either text as a complete archive? | Identify publication context, narrative authority and specific representations; distinguish fiction, participant perspective and historical evidence. |
+| BANK-078-033 | What does a comparative reading of two city novels require beyond naming shared themes? | Choose bounded passages, dates, languages and urban institutions; compare formal choices and contexts instead of assuming every metropolis produces the same experience. |
+
+## 06. Genres and changing readerships
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-078-034 | Why can a detective story's solution depend on rules established earlier in the narrative? | Use a rights-cleared story to track clues, withheld information and retrospective explanation; separate fair-play analysis from real investigative procedure. |
+| BANK-078-035 | How do speculative fiction and historical fiction make different contracts about invented worlds? | Compare explicit world rules, historical references and reader expectations using authorized works; label invention rather than fact-checking every fictional event as a false claim. |
+| BANK-078-036 | What changed as serialized fiction moved between newspapers, magazines and books? | Trace one work's publication records, installments and revisions; avoid assuming cliffhangers alone explain readership or authorial decisions. |
+| BANK-078-037 | How can children's literature address adult readers at the same time? | Examine narrative address, wordplay, imagery and publication context in a permission-cleared work; avoid inferring a child's actual response without appropriate research. |
+
+## 07. Translation, editing and textual transmission
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-078-038 | How can two translations of one passage lead to different interpretations? | Compare the source, translator notes, lexical choices and target-language form with qualified expertise; distinguish interpretation from a claim that one version is fraudulent. |
+| BANK-078-039 | What does a critical edition show that a popular reprint may omit? | Inspect manuscript variants, editorial decisions, annotation and provenance for a named text; do not treat an editor's reconstruction as the untouched original. |
+| BANK-078-040 | How should a literary episode handle disputed authorship? | Trace attributed manuscripts, publication records and the scholarly arguments; separate evidence levels and avoid resolving a debate through stylistic resemblance alone. |
+| BANK-078-041 | Why can abridgment change a work's politics as well as its length? | Compare authorized editions for omitted voices, episodes and framing; distinguish demonstrable cuts from unsupported claims about the editor's motives. |
+
+## 08. Literary institutions, interpretation and responsible media
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-078-042 | How can a book-ban controversy be examined beyond a headline list of forbidden titles? | Use dated official decisions, affected editions, jurisdiction and public responses; distinguish challenges, restrictions and complete bans without inventing legal conclusions. |
+| BANK-078-043 | What should a reviewer disclose when discussing an advance copy or paid promotion? | Identify commercial relationships, edition read and review scope under applicable disclosure rules; avoid implying that sponsorship alone proves an opinion insincere. |
+| BANK-078-044 | How can a reading guide use copyrighted literature without reproducing the book? | Prioritize original analysis, brief necessary excerpts and permission-cleared examples; verify local rights rules and do not provide substitute summaries of protected expression at excessive length. |
+| BANK-078-045 | What makes a canon a historical selection rather than a neutral list of the best books? | Examine curricula, publishers, language access and attributed critical debates; distinguish institutional selection from claims about every excluded work. |
+| BANK-078-046 | How can reader-response evidence be distinguished from a critic's interpretation? | Compare reviews, letters, surveys and close reading with their sampling limits; avoid claiming one reading represents all audiences. |
+
+## Coverage limits and next step
+
+This is an expanded topic-bank draft, not exhaustive scholarship or a researched episode package. Each question has an individually written boundary. Source access, claim verification, current angle-scope review and publication approval remain pending. Add new branches and cases when they fill a real gap; no numeric topic cap applies.
+
+Select an exact case, edition, population, jurisdiction or product version, inspect sources and refine the question before using the existing registry idea/depth planning workflow. These BANK IDs are catalog identities, not approved IDEA IDs. Existing Q1-Q42 defaults, production queues and approval gates are unchanged.
