+# 079 Creative Arts: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 32 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Creative Arts. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Seeing and making decisions
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-079-013 | How can a visual idea be developed before choosing an expensive medium? | Use original thumbnail sketches to explore focal point, scale and mood; distinguish a design exploration from a finished work or universal recipe. |
+| BANK-079-014 | What changes when negative space becomes part of the composition? | Compare original arrangements for balance, grouping and movement; explain visible decisions without treating preference as an objective quality score. |
+| BANK-079-015 | How can value contrast establish a focal point before color is added? | Build a rights-free grayscale study and compare local contrasts; distinguish luminance relationships from a claim that one palette fits every image. |
+| BANK-079-016 | Why can two colors appear different when their surroundings change? | Use a labeled original swatch comparison and documented color-perception research; separate screen reproduction limits from a physical-pigment demonstration. |
+
+## 02. Drawing and observation
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-079-017 | How does gesture drawing answer a different question from contour drawing? | Compare original timed studies for action, proportion and edge observation; avoid equating speed with artistic merit or anatomical accuracy. |
+| BANK-079-018 | What does perspective construction assume about the viewer and picture plane? | Demonstrate simple original boxes and a defined viewpoint; distinguish geometric projection from every expressive or culturally specific spatial convention. |
+| BANK-079-019 | How can drawing from observation reveal errors that tracing may conceal? | Compare measured relationships, attention and revision in a permission-cleared study; avoid treating tracing as inherently illegitimate in every workflow. |
+| BANK-079-020 | Why can believable anatomy include hidden limbs rather than showing every part? | Use an original seated figure to trace structure, overlap, weight and cloth; distinguish intentional occlusion from missing anatomy without demanding an unnatural pose. |
+
+## 03. Paint, pigment and surfaces
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-079-021 | How do watercolor transparency and paper behavior interact? | Compare labeled washes, edges, absorbency and drying conditions using manufacturer guidance; avoid treating digital watercolor effects as evidence of physical pigment behavior. |
+| BANK-079-022 | What changes when an oil-painting passage is built in layers rather than completed wet-in-wet? | Study authorized process records and original swatches for opacity, edges and drying; consult material-safety guidance and exclude unsupported chemical formulations. |
+| BANK-079-023 | How can an acrylic underpainting influence the colors placed above it? | Compare controlled original samples with stated materials and lighting; distinguish a demonstrated optical effect from an undocumented conservation claim. |
+| BANK-079-024 | Why should lightfastness and opacity be considered separately when choosing paint? | Read manufacturer test definitions and conservation references; avoid inferring permanence from a color name or promising that a home test predicts decades of aging. |
+
+## 04. Sculpture, ceramics and physical form
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-079-025 | How does a sculptor plan a form that must be understood from several viewpoints? | Develop an original maquette with documented silhouettes, balance and negative spaces; distinguish visual balance from engineered structural stability. |
+| BANK-079-026 | What changes between modeling, carving and assembling a sculpture? | Compare material removal, revision options, joins and documented process examples; exclude hazardous equipment procedures and avoid ranking techniques by authenticity. |
+| BANK-079-027 | Why does a ceramic piece's final size depend on more than the wet clay shape? | Consult the actual clay body's specifications and documented drying and firing behavior; exclude kiln-operation recipes and explain uncertainty in shrinkage estimates. |
+| BANK-079-028 | How can surface texture communicate a different idea from an object's silhouette? | Compare original material studies under controlled lighting; separate intentional marks, tool limitations and later damage rather than assuming every irregularity is expressive. |
+
+## 05. Printmaking, collage and mixed media
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-079-029 | What makes a print an edition rather than simply a copy of an image? | Examine matrix, process, numbering, variation and artist documentation for a named method; distinguish printmaking terminology from ordinary digital reproduction. |
+| BANK-079-030 | How can collage create relationships that no individual source image contains? | Build an original composition from cleared materials and analyze scale, juxtaposition and edges; track licenses rather than treating transformation as automatic permission. |
+| BANK-079-031 | Why does relief printing require thinking differently about negative and positive marks? | Use an original safe paper simulation or documented studio example; keep explanation separate from unsupervised cutting-tool instruction. |
+| BANK-079-032 | How can mixed-media artists document materials so future care remains possible? | Record supports, adhesives, layers and intended changes; consult conservation sources and avoid promising long-term compatibility from a visually successful result. |
+
+## 06. Digital tools and hybrid workflows
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Inspect the actual product version, official documentation, terms and privacy policies. Test claims under stated conditions and distinguish account permissions, data retention, measured functionality and marketing.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official versioned documentation and release notes; dated privacy policies and service terms; reproducible product tests and original validation reports. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-079-033 | How can layers support revision without replacing composition decisions? | Compare nondestructive edits, masks and source organization in an original illustration; specify software version and distinguish tool availability from artistic judgment. |
+| BANK-079-034 | What changes when artwork moves from a screen to a physical print? | Trace dimensions, resolution, color management, paper and a proofing process; avoid promising identical color across uncalibrated devices. |
+| BANK-079-035 | How can a stylized character remain consistent across several images? | Create an original reference sheet for linework, proportions, palette and rendering; distinguish permitted variation from anatomy or style drift and disclose generated components. |
+| BANK-079-036 | What should an artist check before using a generated image as part of a commercial work? | Inspect current service terms, source provenance, likeness concerns and client requirements; avoid asserting universal ownership or copyright eligibility. |
+
+## 07. Practice, critique and artistic identity
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use consented participant accounts, local records and the community's own descriptions. Include differences of position, access and power; testimony is not a representative survey or clinical proof.
+
+**Source types to locate:** consented interviews and oral histories; community publications and local records; field notes with method and consent documented. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-079-037 | How can a critique describe what the work does before prescribing how to change it? | Use consented original work to compare observation, interpretation and suggestions; keep the maker's aims distinct from the critic's preferences. |
+| BANK-079-038 | What does a useful art-study series hold constant and deliberately vary? | Plan original studies of light direction, material or composition with recorded choices; avoid equating repetition or volume with guaranteed improvement. |
+| BANK-079-039 | How can an artist learn from a masterwork without presenting a study as an original invention? | Verify reference rights, label the study and distinguish copied structure from new decisions; avoid suggesting attribution alone permits every commercial use. |
+| BANK-079-040 | Why can a portfolio benefit from process evidence rather than only polished final images? | Compare selected drafts, revisions and personal contributions for a defined audience; avoid exposing client material without authorization. |
+
+## 08. Exhibition, livelihood and cultural responsibility
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-079-041 | What should be clarified before accepting an art commission? | Map scope, revisions, deliverables, payment, usage rights and cancellation in an illustrative brief; verify local contract requirements rather than providing a universal legal template. |
+| BANK-079-042 | How can an exhibition label add context without dictating a single interpretation? | Compare material facts, attributed intention and accessible language in original labels; avoid inventing the maker's beliefs or replacing the audience's encounter with jargon. |
+| BANK-079-043 | What distinguishes respectful cultural research from copying a restricted motif? | Use community-authorized guidance, provenance and consent for a named context; do not assume public visibility grants unrestricted rights or sacred access. |
+| BANK-079-044 | How can an artist discuss rejection without turning it into evidence that the work has no value? | Use consented experiences and submission criteria to separate fit, constraints and judgment; exclude mental-health diagnosis or promises that persistence guarantees recognition. |
+
+## Coverage limits and next step
+
+This is an expanded topic-bank draft, not exhaustive scholarship or a researched episode package. Each question has an individually written boundary. Source access, claim verification, current angle-scope review and publication approval remain pending. Add new branches and cases when they fill a real gap; no numeric topic cap applies.
+
+Select an exact case, edition, population, jurisdiction or product version, inspect sources and refine the question before using the existing registry idea/depth planning workflow. These BANK IDs are catalog identities, not approved IDEA IDs. Existing Q1-Q42 defaults, production queues and approval gates are unchanged.
