+# 080 Film & Entertainment Analysis: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 32 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Film & Entertainment Analysis. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Evidence for screen analysis
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-080-013 | How can a film-analysis claim be supported by a sequence rather than a plot summary? | Annotate shot, sound, editing and performance in a short rights-cleared scene; distinguish observation, interpretation and verified production context. |
+| BANK-080-014 | What does a film's opening teach viewers about how to watch the rest? | Compare perspective, information, rhythm and genre cues in an authorized example; avoid assuming later meanings were consciously intended by every collaborator. |
+| BANK-080-015 | Why can a viewer's emotional response differ from a claim about the film's technique? | Separate a personal reaction from observable formal choices and reception evidence; do not infer a universal audience response from one critic's experience. |
+
+## 02. Cinematography, space and production design
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-080-016 | How can blocking make a power shift visible without a line of dialogue? | Trace an original staged scene for distance, height, movement and eyelines; distinguish camera interpretation from a diagnosis of a character's psychology. |
+| BANK-080-017 | What does offscreen space add to suspense? | Analyze sound cues, framing and withheld views in a rights-cleared sequence; avoid reducing suspense to a single camera angle or revealing unmarked major spoilers. |
+| BANK-080-018 | How can production design communicate a character's circumstances without explanatory dialogue? | Compare props, wear, layout and costume in an authorized scene; separate documented design choices from speculative biographical claims about the maker. |
+| BANK-080-019 | Why does a long take still contain editorial decisions? | Inspect rehearsal, camera movement, focus and action timing through the scene and authorized production sources; distinguish apparent continuity from verified capture methods. |
+
+## 03. Editing and time
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-080-020 | How does the Odessa Steps sequence in Battleship Potemkin organize action through montage? | Study a traceable edition's shot relationships and rhythm with attributed scholarship; distinguish dramatic construction from documentary evidence of the depicted event. |
+| BANK-080-021 | What changes when a reaction shot is placed before rather than after an action? | Create an original two-version edit and compare available information; label the demonstration and avoid claiming all viewers interpret it identically. |
+| BANK-080-022 | How can crosscutting create a relationship between events that never share a frame? | Map an authorized sequence's locations, timing cues and unresolved assumptions; distinguish implied simultaneity from verified story chronology. |
+| BANK-080-023 | Why can a cut feel smooth even when it skips part of an action? | Examine movement matching, expectation, screen direction and sound bridges in original footage; keep continuity conventions separate from rules that every style must obey. |
+
+## 04. Sound, music and silence
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-080-024 | How does the boundary between story-world sound and score change a scene's meaning? | Trace an authorized sequence where the source becomes clear or ambiguous; distinguish audible evidence from unverified composer or director intention. |
+| BANK-080-025 | What can silence do that continuous background music might prevent? | Compare original edits for attention, room tone and anticipation; avoid equating reduced loudness with absence of sound design. |
+| BANK-080-026 | How can a recurring musical motif change across a film? | Follow identified appearances, orchestration and narrative context using rights-cleared excerpts and score commentary; do not assume one fixed emotional meaning. |
+| BANK-080-027 | Why can dialogue intelligibility be a creative and accessibility question at once? | Compare mix, performance, subtitles and listening conditions; distinguish a documented production decision from a viewer's particular playback problem. |
+
+## 05. Performance, genre and narrative perspective
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-080-028 | How does Rashomon organize conflicting accounts without offering every speaker equal credibility automatically? | Analyze a specified version's framing, repeated events and withheld information; separate the film's formal uncertainty from claims about real eyewitness reliability. |
+| BANK-080-029 | What can Pather Panchali's everyday scenes contribute beyond advancing the plot? | Inspect duration, gesture, setting and narrative context with attributed criticism; avoid reducing the film to a factual portrait of every rural household. |
+| BANK-080-030 | How can a comedy establish timing through performance as well as editing? | Compare pause, movement, framing and audience information in cleared examples; avoid defining humor solely by joke counts or a critic's reaction. |
+| BANK-080-031 | What changes when a familiar genre convention is withheld or reversed? | Choose one bounded convention and compare an authorized example with audience expectations; distinguish deliberate subversion from an unverified production accident. |
+| BANK-080-032 | How can star persona influence a role without determining every audience's reading? | Compare prior roles, publicity and documented reception for a named performer; avoid private-life speculation or treating fan accounts as representative research. |
+
+## 06. Adaptation, television and serialized entertainment
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Define the rival claims and their evidence standards. Compare sources on equal terms, separate empirical findings from values or interpretations and do not imply that all positions have equal evidential support.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the original sources behind the compared claims; explicit definitions and measurement methods; independent critiques with traceable evidence. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-080-033 | What does a novel-to-film adaptation need to translate beyond the source plot? | Compare viewpoint, interior thought, pacing and medium-specific devices in authorized works; distinguish creative interpretation from a simple fidelity score. |
+| BANK-080-034 | How does a television pilot distribute questions differently from a feature-film opening? | Map character promises, episode closure and season-level uncertainty in a cleared example; avoid assuming every series follows the same production model. |
+| BANK-080-035 | Why can an episode recap change what viewers remember as important? | Analyze selection, omission and ordering against the previous episode; distinguish editorial emphasis from evidence of actual memory effects. |
+| BANK-080-036 | How should a remake comparison account for time, language and industry context? | Compare specific scenes, audience assumptions and authorized production histories; avoid treating similarity as proof of theft or one version as automatically superior. |
+
+## 07. Industry, audiences and public claims
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use current official terms, filings or macroeconomic data; state jurisdiction, date, fees, taxes, risk, units and assumptions. Use transparent illustrative calculations rather than personal recommendations or return promises.
+
+**Source types to locate:** regulator or official statistical publications; actual contracts, fee schedules and audited filings; original economic studies and disclosed calculation inputs. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-080-037 | What does a box-office figure omit about a film's commercial outcome? | Separate gross receipts, distribution shares, budgets, marketing, territories and reporting dates using primary disclosures; avoid inventing profit from a headline total. |
+| BANK-080-038 | How can streaming popularity claims be compared when platforms publish different metrics? | Inspect time windows, account definitions, viewing hours and geography from official methodology; do not turn incompatible rankings into a universal audience chart. |
+| BANK-080-039 | What can festival selection reveal and leave unknown about a film's reception? | Review selection criteria, jury context, distribution and varied critical responses; distinguish an award from a measure of all viewers' preferences. |
+| BANK-080-040 | How can publicity shape an entertainment controversy before the work is widely available? | Build a dated record of official statements, trailers and reporting; distinguish verified content, promotional framing and unsubstantiated allegations. |
+
+## 08. Criticism, rights and responsible presentation
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-080-041 | How should a video essay decide which clips are necessary for its argument? | Prioritize analysis, minimal relevant excerpts, licensing and applicable local exceptions; do not assume a fixed clip length guarantees lawful use. |
+| BANK-080-042 | What does spoiler-aware analysis need beyond placing a warning at the start? | Separate premise discussion, scene analysis and ending interpretation with clear signposts; respect the audience's choice without pretending spoiler tolerance is universal. |
+| BANK-080-043 | How can a critic discuss representation without claiming to speak for an entire community? | Use attributed perspectives, textual evidence and production context; include disagreement and avoid inferring creators' private intentions from stereotypes alone. |
+| BANK-080-044 | What should change in an analysis when a production anecdote proves unsupported? | Correct the factual record, identify which interpretations depended on it and update the visible claim; preserve analysis that remains supported by the actual work. |
+
+## Coverage limits and next step
+
+This is an expanded topic-bank draft, not exhaustive scholarship or a researched episode package. Each question has an individually written boundary. Source access, claim verification, current angle-scope review and publication approval remain pending. Add new branches and cases when they fill a real gap; no numeric topic cap applies.
+
+Select an exact case, edition, population, jurisdiction or product version, inspect sources and refine the question before using the existing registry idea/depth planning workflow. These BANK IDs are catalog identities, not approved IDEA IDs. Existing Q1-Q42 defaults, production queues and approval gates are unchanged.
