+## 01. Theatre as a live relationship
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-081-013 | What changes when a story is performed for a physically present audience? | Compare timing, shared space, contingency and feedback through an original scene; avoid claiming that recorded performance lacks artistic value or that audiences respond uniformly. |
+| BANK-081-014 | How do proscenium, thrust and in-the-round stages create different visibility problems? | Use original blocking diagrams with sightlines and entrances; distinguish spatial affordances from a rule that one configuration suits every play. |
+| BANK-081-015 | What does a theatre programme reveal about work beyond the lead performers? | Map credited design, stage management, technical, access and production roles using authorized materials; avoid attributing collaborative decisions to a single celebrity. |
+
+## 02. Text, dramaturgy and adaptation
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-081-016 | How can a dramaturg turn research into decisions a production can actually use? | Develop a bounded source packet for an original scene with context, pronunciation and unresolved questions; distinguish research support from dictating one interpretation. |
+| BANK-081-017 | What does cutting a scene change besides the running time? | Compare information, character relationships and later payoffs in a rights-cleared script; track necessary approvals and avoid presenting altered copyrighted work without permission. |
+| BANK-081-018 | How can translating a play affect performance choices beyond literal meaning? | Compare a short authorized passage for rhythm, address, register and cultural references; attribute translators and distinguish readable text from speakable choices. |
+| BANK-081-019 | Why can documentary theatre require stricter provenance than an invented monologue? | Trace interviews, public records, editing and consent in an authorized example; label composites and do not present invented words as actual testimony. |
+
+## 03. Acting, rehearsal and ensemble
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use consented participant accounts, local records and the community's own descriptions. Include differences of position, access and power; testimony is not a representative survey or clinical proof.
+
+**Source types to locate:** consented interviews and oral histories; community publications and local records; field notes with method and consent documented. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-081-020 | How can an actor pursue an objective without explaining every thought to the audience? | Use an original scene to compare playable actions, obstacles and observable behavior; distinguish rehearsal vocabulary from claims about a universal psychology. |
+| BANK-081-021 | What makes ensemble timing more than performers remembering their own lines? | Examine cues, listening, rhythm and shared attention in consented rehearsal footage; avoid inferring a performer's private emotions from the result. |
+| BANK-081-022 | How can rehearsal feedback remain specific without becoming a judgment of the performer as a person? | Compare task-focused notes, agreed language and opportunities to clarify in an original example; preserve consent and exclude humiliation as a training method. |
+| BANK-081-023 | What should be negotiated before rehearsing physically intimate or violent-looking scenes? | Use recognized professional standards on consent, qualified coordination and boundaries; exclude combat choreography, coercive exercises and unsupervised imitation. |
+
+## 04. Voice, movement and music
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-081-024 | How does stage projection differ from simply making a voice louder? | Consult qualified voice pedagogy on intelligibility, resonance and venue conditions; exclude strain-inducing exercises or diagnosing vocal health. |
+| BANK-081-025 | What can a gesture communicate differently at the back of a large auditorium? | Compare scale, tempo and sightlines in a documented performance context; distinguish legibility from a universal preference for exaggerated acting. |
+| BANK-081-026 | How does a musical number change dramatic action rather than pause it? | Analyze a rights-cleared example for objective, information, rhythm and transition; separate the book, score, choreography and performer contributions. |
+| BANK-081-027 | Why does performing with live musicians require a different cue relationship from a fixed recording? | Inspect tempo flexibility, conductor communication and technical dependencies through authorized practice records; avoid presenting one company's arrangement as industry-wide. |
+
+## 05. Design, stage management and technical coordination
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-081-028 | How can lighting guide attention while preserving what performers need to see? | Compare an original design concept, documented cue intent and visibility constraints; exclude electrical installation or hazardous rigging procedures. |
+| BANK-081-029 | What makes a costume functional for a performer as well as meaningful to an audience? | Examine movement, quick changes, material, maintenance and character information using authorized designs; avoid treating historical silhouette as proof of exact period accuracy. |
+| BANK-081-030 | How does a stage manager's prompt book organize a repeatable performance? | Use a fictional cue extract to connect script location, responsibilities and communication; do not expose a real production's restricted documents. |
+| BANK-081-031 | Why can a scene change become part of the production's storytelling? | Compare visible labor, pace, music and transition imagery in cleared examples; distinguish aesthetic choice from a claim that hidden changes are obsolete. |
+
+## 06. Performance traditions and attributed lineages
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Identify the text, edition, translator, commentary, community and ritual context as applicable. Attribute theological and spiritual claims, preserve regional variation and respect public versus restricted knowledge.
+
+**Source types to locate:** primary texts in traceable editions; authorized public teaching and ritual descriptions; consented community accounts and critical historical studies. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-081-032 | How can a Kathakali episode distinguish public performance analysis from specialist training? | Use authorized performers' accounts, public repertoire and attributed scholarship on gesture, costume and narrative; avoid teaching restricted or technically demanding practices from clips. |
+| BANK-081-033 | What can a Noh performance reveal about convention and audience knowledge? | Choose an identified play, translation and authorized performance context; explain selected public conventions without treating all Japanese theatre as one form. |
+| BANK-081-034 | How do nautanki or tamasha performances relate to their local audiences and changing venues? | Study one named tradition through consented performers, scripts and historical records; preserve regional variation rather than collapsing folk theatre into a single style. |
+| BANK-081-035 | How can a contemporary production draw on classical Indian dramaturgy without claiming direct continuity for every choice? | Compare an identified public textual concept with the production's own attributed explanation; distinguish inspiration, interpretation and historically demonstrated transmission. |
+
+## 07. Audiences, access and institutions
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use consented participant accounts, local records and the community's own descriptions. Include differences of position, access and power; testimony is not a representative survey or clinical proof.
+
+**Source types to locate:** consented interviews and oral histories; community publications and local records; field notes with method and consent documented. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-081-036 | How can captioning, audio description and relaxed performances alter access differently? | Compare services, preparation and consented audience feedback for a named venue; avoid claiming that one access offer meets every person's needs. |
+| BANK-081-037 | What changes when theatre is made with a community rather than only about it? | Trace participation, decision authority, payment, consent and representation; distinguish collaboration from inviting testimony into a predetermined script. |
+| BANK-081-038 | Why can ticket price alone fail to explain who attends theatre? | Investigate transport, timing, language, information and belonging using ethical audience research; avoid treating one venue's attendees as a population sample. |
+
+## 08. Economics, recording and critique
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-081-039 | What must a touring production change when moving into another venue? | Map dimensions, crew roles, access, permissions and technical compatibility using authorized riders; exclude unsafe improvisation or assuming the original design fits unchanged. |
+| BANK-081-040 | How can a theatre budget distinguish creative ambition from operational commitments? | Use transparent illustrative costs for rehearsal, rights, staffing, venue and contingency; avoid promising profitability from a full auditorium alone. |
+| BANK-081-041 | What rights need checking before recording and distributing a stage performance? | Review play, music, design, performer and venue permissions under current local rules; distinguish permission to attend from permission to publish footage. |
+| BANK-081-042 | How can a reviewer assess an experimental performance without judging it only by conventional plot? | Identify the work's apparent aims, formal choices and audience relationship using observable evidence; separate personal preference from a claim of objective failure. |
