+# 083 Jewelry & Precious Materials: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 30 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Jewelry & Precious Materials. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Jewelry as material and object
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-083-013 | What should be identified before describing a piece of jewelry as gold, silver or platinum? | Separate base material, alloy, coating, marks and documented testing; avoid declaring purity from color or an unverified stamp. |
+| BANK-083-014 | How do a gemstone's identity, treatment and origin answer different questions? | Use recognized laboratory terminology and report formats; distinguish natural formation, synthetic growth, enhancement and geographic-origin opinions. |
+| BANK-083-015 | Why can a jewelry photograph misrepresent scale, color and condition? | Compare lighting, magnification, reference dimensions and retouching in original images; do not infer authenticity or value from photographic brilliance. |
+
+## 02. Metals and manufacture
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-083-016 | What does a precious-metal fineness mark establish when it is genuine? | Read the applicable hallmarking authority's rules, date and mark system; distinguish a claimed mark from verified assay and do not universalize one country's scheme. |
+| BANK-083-017 | How do cast and fabricated jewelry pieces differ in construction evidence? | Compare documented process images, joints, surface finish and repair implications; exclude metal-melting procedures and avoid treating one method as inherently superior. |
+| BANK-083-018 | Why can plating thickness and the underlying metal affect long-term appearance? | Inspect manufacturer specifications, wear conditions and care guidance; avoid promising service life or diagnosing allergic reactions from composition alone. |
+| BANK-083-019 | What makes a jewelry clasp or stone setting a functional design problem? | Analyze load, access, wear and maintenance in an owned example using qualified jeweler guidance; exclude improvised structural repair instructions. |
+
+## 03. Diamonds and laboratory reports
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-083-020 | How do diamond cut, color, clarity and carat describe different attributes? | Use recognized grading definitions and traceable example reports; distinguish the grading system from a guarantee of appearance, ethical sourcing or resale value. |
+| BANK-083-021 | What can a laboratory-grown diamond report establish about the tested stone? | Inspect identity, growth terminology, grading scope and report verification; avoid assuming that a report documents the entire retail supply chain. |
+| BANK-083-022 | Why is diamond carat weight not the same as visible face-up size? | Compare documented dimensions, proportions and shape with labeled diagrams; avoid inferring quality or price solely from spread. |
+| BANK-083-023 | How can a consumer check whether a grading report corresponds to the offered item? | Use the issuing laboratory's official verification process and qualified inspection; distinguish matching identifiers from proof against every possible substitution. |
+
+## 04. Colored stones, pearls and organic materials
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-083-024 | What does a ruby or sapphire treatment disclosure change about interpreting a report? | Review laboratory terminology for documented treatments and limits of detection; avoid home-testing recipes or claims that an untreated stone is automatically more beautiful. |
+| BANK-083-025 | Why can emerald clarity and care need different expectations from diamond jewelry? | Consult qualified gemological and manufacturer guidance on inclusions, treatment and construction; exclude heat, chemical or ultrasonic cleaning prescriptions for unknown stones. |
+| BANK-083-026 | How are cultured pearls distinguished from imitation materials in professional assessment? | Use recognized terminology and laboratory methods at an explanatory level; do not recommend destructive home tests or infer identity from one surface feature. |
+| BANK-083-027 | What makes opal classification depend on structure and assembly as well as color? | Compare laboratory definitions for solid material, composites and treatments; distinguish descriptive labels from quality grades or verified geographic origin. |
+
+## 05. Design, wear and conservation
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use consented participant accounts, local records and the community's own descriptions. Include differences of position, access and power; testimony is not a representative survey or clinical proof.
+
+**Source types to locate:** consented interviews and oral histories; community publications and local records; field notes with method and consent documented. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-083-028 | How can a ring's design be evaluated for the wearer's daily tasks? | Compare profile, snagging, fastening, size changes and maintenance with consented user needs; avoid assuming one style suits every occupation or access requirement. |
+| BANK-083-029 | What should be assessed before resizing or modifying an inherited piece? | Consider construction, stones, prior repairs, significance and qualified jeweler advice; distinguish sentimental preservation from the feasibility of physical alteration. |
+| BANK-083-030 | How can jewelry storage reduce avoidable damage without requiring elaborate equipment? | Use material-specific professional guidance on contact, abrasion, moisture and security; avoid universal cleaning or storage instructions for mixed historic materials. |
+| BANK-083-031 | Why should historic jewelry sometimes be conserved rather than restored to a new appearance? | Inspect provenance, surviving surfaces, reversibility and conservation goals; do not treat polishing as automatically harmless or patina as proof of authenticity. |
+
+## 06. Cultural histories and provenance
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-083-032 | What can a documented Indian temple-jewelry tradition reveal about makers and ritual context? | Choose a named tradition with authorized accounts, dated objects and craft records; distinguish contemporary style labels from verified ritual provenance. |
+| BANK-083-033 | How do archaeological jewelry finds reveal trade without identifying every wearer? | Compare material analysis, find context and distribution in a published case; distinguish possible sourcing from a complete ownership history. |
+| BANK-083-034 | What questions should be asked about a piece advertised as royal or celebrity-owned? | Trace dated inventories, photographs, sale records and chain of custody; avoid treating a seller's narrative as established provenance. |
+| BANK-083-035 | How can a wedding-jewelry episode preserve differences within a community? | Use consented wearer and maker accounts alongside attributed histories; avoid presenting one region's forms, spending or symbolism as universal obligations. |
+
+## 07. Valuation, markets and consumer terms
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use current official terms, filings or macroeconomic data; state jurisdiction, date, fees, taxes, risk, units and assumptions. Use transparent illustrative calculations rather than personal recommendations or return promises.
+
+**Source types to locate:** regulator or official statistical publications; actual contracts, fee schedules and audited filings; original economic studies and disclosed calculation inputs. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-083-036 | How do replacement appraisal, auction estimate and realized resale price differ? | Define date, market, fees, condition and appraisal purpose using transparent examples; exclude personalized investment advice or guaranteed returns. |
+| BANK-083-037 | What costs can be hidden when jewelry is marketed mainly by metal weight? | Compare alloy content, making charges, stone valuation, taxes and buyback terms from current official disclosures; label illustrative figures and verify jurisdiction. |
+| BANK-083-038 | How can a buyback promise be assessed before purchase? | Inspect deductions, condition requirements, documents, settlement method and counterparty obligations; distinguish an advertisement from the actual dated contract. |
+| BANK-083-039 | Why can a gemstone's rarity fail to predict an easy resale market? | Examine demand, identification costs, liquidity, provenance and transaction channels through documented cases; avoid speculative price forecasts. |
+
+## 08. Rights, sourcing and claim integrity
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
