+## 02. Fluids, pressure and heat
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-087-017 | How does a siphon move liquid and what conditions stop it working? | Compare pressure, height, continuity and documented physical models using safe water-only illustrations; exclude fuels, hazardous liquids and unsupported claims of perpetual flow. |
+| BANK-087-018 | What does a refrigerator move rather than simply create? | Trace heat transfer, refrigerant states and electrical work through manufacturer diagrams; exclude servicing sealed systems or handling refrigerants. |
+| BANK-087-019 | Why can a pressure cooker raise cooking temperature under controlled conditions? | Explain pressure-temperature relationships and engineered controls at a conceptual level; rely on official product guidance and exclude modification or blocked-vent demonstrations. |
+| BANK-087-020 | How does a thermostatic control differ from an appliance merely having a heater? | Compare measurement, switching, hysteresis and setpoint in a safe simulation; distinguish control logic from uniform temperature at every location. |
+| BANK-087-021 | What changes between evaporation cooling and refrigeration? | Compare energy transfer, humidity, airflow and system boundaries; use labeled examples and avoid claiming equal performance in every climate. |
+
+## 03. Electricity, light and sensing
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-087-022 | How does an LED turn electrical input into light differently from an incandescent lamp? | Use authoritative device explanations and labeled energy flows; distinguish mechanism, efficiency measurements and product lifespan claims without wiring demonstrations. |
+| BANK-087-023 | What does a transformer change in an alternating-current circuit? | Explain electromagnetic induction, turns ratio and load assumptions through diagrams; exclude mains experiments and avoid implying energy amplification. |
+| BANK-087-024 | How can a touch screen detect contact without seeing a finger? | Compare documented capacitive and resistive principles with device-specific limitations; avoid claiming all gloves, styluses or screens work the same way. |
+| BANK-087-025 | Why can a smoke alarm respond differently to different fire signatures? | Use official fire-safety and manufacturer explanations of sensing methods; exclude disabling alarms or presenting a comparison as personalized placement guidance. |
