+# 088 How It's Made: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 31 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for How It's Made. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Following an object's production chain
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-088-013 | How can a manufacturing episode trace one object without implying every factory uses the same method? | Define product variant, facility, date and observed stages using authorized process records; distinguish a representative explanation from a universal recipe. |
+| BANK-088-014 | What changes between raw material, intermediate stock and a finished product? | Follow a documented aluminum-can chain with explicit material states and ownership boundaries; avoid equating one factory tour with the full supply network. |
+| BANK-088-015 | Why should a how-it-is-made film show inspection and rejected work as well as smooth production? | Map quality checks, rework, sampling and disposition in a permitted facility; distinguish observed process control from a claim that every product is flawless. |
+
+## 02. Metal objects
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-088-016 | How does a drawn beverage can differ from an assembled metal container? | Use manufacturer diagrams to trace sheet formation, shaping and closure; exclude machine settings or pressure-vessel fabrication advice. |
+| BANK-088-017 | What makes a steel fastener's production more than cutting a thread? | Compare documented forming, heat treatment, coating and inspection for a named specification; do not infer rated strength from appearance. |
+| BANK-088-018 | How can a kitchen knife move through shaping, heat treatment and finishing? | Use authorized industrial process descriptions with material identification and quality checks; exclude home-forging instructions or weapon-performance demonstrations. |
+| BANK-088-019 | Why can a metal casting need machining even when the mold already defines its shape? | Trace shrinkage allowances, surface finish and functional tolerances in a bounded product; keep conceptual explanation separate from foundry operating procedures. |
+
+## 03. Ceramics, glass and mineral-based products
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-088-020 | How does a glass bottle acquire a repeatable shape without every bottle being individually blown by hand? | Trace a documented industrial forming process, annealing and inspection; distinguish machine methods from studio practice and exclude furnace-operation instructions. |
+| BANK-088-021 | What separates porcelain production from simply firing any clay white? | Inspect documented raw materials, forming, glazing and controlled firing for a named product; avoid universal recipes or claims based on surface color alone. |
+| BANK-088-022 | How can a ceramic tile's pattern be produced at different stages of manufacture? | Compare documented body coloring, glazing and printing with quality checks; distinguish decorative finish from verified slip or wear performance. |
+| BANK-088-023 | Why does cement production need to be distinguished from making concrete products? | Trace feedstock, clinker, blending and later concrete manufacture through official process diagrams; exclude construction mixes or performance prescriptions. |
+
+## 04. Paper, textiles and packaging
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-088-024 | How is corrugated cardboard made into a box that can be shipped flat? | Follow paper supply, fluting, lamination, cutting and scoring with authorized diagrams; distinguish packaging design from certified load capacity. |
+| BANK-088-025 | What happens between raw cotton and a finished woven shirt? | Trace spinning, weaving, finishing, cutting and sewing with stated facility boundaries; avoid presenting one brand's sequence as the whole textile industry. |
+| BANK-088-026 | How does an industrial bookbinding line turn printed sheets into a usable volume? | Compare folding, gathering, binding, trimming and inspection in a named method; distinguish the original print edition from later rebinding. |
+| BANK-088-027 | Why can food packaging require layers that are difficult to see or separate? | Use actual manufacturer and regulatory documentation on barrier functions and intended contact; avoid guessing composition or recyclability from appearance. |
+
+## 05. Electronics and precision products
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-088-028 | What stages separate a bare printed circuit board from an assembled electronic device? | Trace board manufacture, component placement, soldering, testing and enclosure through official references; exclude hazardous chemical or high-temperature fabrication instructions. |
+| BANK-088-029 | How does a semiconductor wafer become packaged chips that can be installed in a product? | Explain documented fabrication, probing, dicing, packaging and testing at a bounded level; distinguish a simplified flow from a reproducible fabrication recipe. |
+| BANK-088-030 | Why can an optical lens require several shaping and coating stages? | Use manufacturer process records for a specified lens material and application; include inspection without claiming the same route serves all optics. |
+| BANK-088-031 | How are rechargeable cells inspected before being assembled into a battery pack? | Compare documented electrical, mechanical and traceability checks; exclude cell joining, pack construction or bypassing protection systems. |
