+# 089 Inventions & Inventors: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 32 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Inventions & Inventors. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Defining invention and historical credit
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-089-013 | What distinguishes inventing a principle from making a practical device that uses it? | Compare dated records of concept, prototype, reliable operation and adoption in a named case; avoid giving one person credit for every development stage. |
+| BANK-089-014 | How can an invention have several plausible inventors without the historical question becoming meaningless? | Define the claimed contribution, chronology, evidence and contact among contributors; preserve independent work and distinguish priority from later fame. |
+| BANK-089-015 | What can a patent establish about an inventor's claim and what can it not prove? | Read the actual dated document, jurisdiction and claims alongside technical evidence; distinguish a legal filing from first conception, working performance or commercial success. |
+| BANK-089-016 | Why can the name attached to a device obscure technicians, laborers and earlier makers? | Trace workshop records, correspondence and institutional archives; identify missing documentation without inventing unrecorded biographies. |
+
+## 02. Communication and recording technologies
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-089-017 | How should a telephone-invention story separate patents, demonstrations and practical networks? | Compare primary filings, documented tests, legal records and infrastructure histories; avoid a single-hero narrative or resolving disputed priority from a textbook label. |
+| BANK-089-018 | What changed between early photography experiments and reproducible photographic practice? | Trace identified processes, dates, exposure evidence and material requirements through museum or archival records; exclude hazardous historical chemical recipes. |
+| BANK-089-019 | How can movable-type histories be compared across East Asian and European contexts? | Identify materials, scripts, printing institutions and dated evidence; distinguish separate developments and avoid assuming one standard narrative covers every printing technology. |
+| BANK-089-020 | Why did recorded sound require changes in production and playback as well as capturing vibration? | Follow a bounded sequence of documented devices and formats; distinguish laboratory possibility from widespread household adoption. |
+
+## 03. Power, transport and material change
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-089-021 | What did James Watt's documented work change within the longer development of steam engines? | Compare patents, engine designs, operating context and predecessor systems using primary engineering histories; avoid crediting one person with creating steam power from nothing. |
+| BANK-089-022 | How can a light-bulb history distinguish a glowing filament from a viable lighting system? | Examine documented lamps, material life, power supply and distribution; separate invention claims from manufacturing and infrastructure achievements. |
+| BANK-089-023 | What made a bicycle design suitable for broader use beyond proving that two wheels could move? | Trace documented geometry, drivetrain, tires, manufacturing and user conditions; avoid presenting one surviving example as the first of every bicycle type. |
+| BANK-089-024 | Why can the history of reinforced concrete not be reduced to one formula or patent? | Compare material, reinforcement, structural design and documented construction systems; exclude practical structural-design instructions. |
+
+## 04. Medicine, agriculture and everyday life
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-089-025 | How can an antibiotic-discovery narrative distinguish observation, isolation and scaled production? | Use dated laboratory records, publications and industrial histories for a named drug; separate historical explanation from present treatment advice. |
+| BANK-089-026 | What changes when a crop innovation is studied from the farmer's field rather than only the inventor's laboratory? | Compare seed development, local trials, inputs, adoption and farmer accounts; avoid assuming a yield claim applies across climates and farming systems. |
+| BANK-089-027 | How did refrigeration become an everyday food-system technology rather than a single machine? | Trace documented equipment, cold chains, energy and public regulation in one region; distinguish preserved-food claims from current food-safety advice. |
+| BANK-089-028 | Why can an everyday fastener such as a zipper take years to become commercially useful? | Inspect patents, manufacturing tolerances, product redesign and adoption records; avoid treating a filing date as the date every user obtained the finished form. |
+
+## 05. Computing and collective research
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-089-029 | How can the history of the transistor distinguish scientific principle, device demonstration and mass production? | Compare primary papers, laboratory records and manufacturing histories; preserve collaborative contributions and do not infer all modern chip design from the first device. |
+| BANK-089-030 | What does a software invention require as evidence when no single physical artifact defines it? | Trace dated source, publications, demonstrations and version history for a bounded method; distinguish implementation, mathematical prior work and later naming. |
+| BANK-089-031 | How did packet switching become a networking practice across several projects? | Use published designs, institutional archives and deployment records; distinguish similar concepts, documented influence and operational systems. |
+| BANK-089-032 | Why can open-source contribution records complicate a founder-centered product history? | Compare commits, issue discussions, releases and governance with attribution limits; avoid equating commit count with total creative or maintenance contribution. |
+
+## 06. Adoption, standards and unintended consequences
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-089-033 | Why can a technically successful invention fail to find users? | Choose a documented case and compare cost, reliability, infrastructure, competing practices and business constraints; avoid inventing consumer motives from sales alone. |
+| BANK-089-034 | What changes when a technical design becomes an industry standard? | Trace committee records, interoperability goals, licensing and adoption for a named standard; distinguish formal acceptance from universal implementation. |
+| BANK-089-035 | How can an invention solve one problem while creating a new dependency? | Map the original use, maintenance, supply chain and documented consequences in a bounded case; separate retrospective judgment from the inventor's verified knowledge. |
+| BANK-089-036 | Why should an innovation story include users who adapted the device after purchase? | Use repair records, correspondence, community accounts and later design changes; distinguish documented user invention from speculative influence. |
+
+## 07. Myth, failure and historical method
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Define the rival claims and their evidence standards. Compare sources on equal terms, separate empirical findings from values or interpretations and do not imply that all positions have equal evidential support.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the original sources behind the compared claims; explicit definitions and measurement methods; independent critiques with traceable evidence. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-089-037 | How can a famous eureka anecdote be checked without losing the larger scientific story? | Trace earliest attestation, contemporary records and later retellings; label uncertain anecdotes and distinguish a memorable account from the actual research process. |
+| BANK-089-038 | What evidence is needed before calling a failed prototype a suppressed invention? | Inspect documented performance, independent tests, financing and communications; avoid treating lack of commercial adoption as proof of conspiracy. |
+| BANK-089-039 | How can a replica test historical plausibility without proving an original device existed? | Define materials, assumptions, missing dimensions and test conditions; distinguish a modern reconstruction's success from archaeological or archival proof. |
+| BANK-089-040 | What do surviving failed designs reveal that a museum display of successful objects may omit? | Compare prototypes, test notes, rejected options and selection effects; avoid assuming every failed idea was foolish or every survivor was inevitable. |
+
+## 08. Credit, rights and responsible invention storytelling
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-089-041 | How should a documentary distinguish a patent dispute's legal outcome from the full history of invention? | Use judgments, claims and contemporary technical records with jurisdiction and date explicit; avoid converting a narrow legal finding into a universal priority verdict. |
+| BANK-089-042 | What can institutional ownership records reveal about the difference between inventor and patent owner? | Read contracts where public, assignment records and official policies; distinguish authorship, employment and legal title without offering individual legal advice. |
+| BANK-089-043 | How can histories of underrecognized inventors avoid replacing one exaggerated hero story with another? | Use traceable contributions, context and uncertainty while crediting collaborators; do not manufacture priority or technical impact to repair a representational gap. |
+| BANK-089-044 | What should a creator disclose when animating an invention whose details are incomplete? | Label documented parts, inferred geometry and illustrative motion; distinguish explanatory reconstruction from an exact working blueprint or surviving original. |
+
+## Coverage limits and next step
+
+This is an expanded topic-bank draft, not exhaustive scholarship or a researched episode package. Each question has an individually written boundary. Source access, claim verification, current angle-scope review and publication approval remain pending. Add new branches and cases when they fill a real gap; no numeric topic cap applies.
+
+Select an exact case, edition, population, jurisdiction or product version, inspect sources and refine the question before using the existing registry idea/depth planning workflow. These BANK IDs are catalog identities, not approved IDEA IDs. Existing Q1-Q42 defaults, production queues and approval gates are unchanged.
