+# 091 Cooking & Culinary: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 31 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Cooking & Culinary. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Ingredients, heat and cooking evidence
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-091-013 | How can a cooking episode explain an ingredient's function instead of only listing a quantity? | Use an original dish to compare structure, flavor, moisture and texture roles; distinguish observed changes from untested substitution claims. |
+| BANK-091-014 | Why do boiling, steaming and roasting produce different results from similar ingredients? | Compare heat transfer, surface moisture and documented food chemistry using safe examples; keep food-safety requirements tied to official guidance rather than appearance. |
+| BANK-091-015 | What should a recipe test record so another cook can understand its limits? | Specify ingredient form, quantities, equipment, timing, observed endpoints and tested yield; distinguish a successful trial from proof across every appliance and kitchen. |
+| BANK-091-016 | How can taste descriptions separate saltiness, acidity, bitterness, sweetness and aroma? | Use ordinary food examples, sensory vocabulary and individual variation; avoid medical claims or implying that one preference is an objective standard. |
+
+## 02. Indian cooking techniques and regional context
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-091-017 | What changes during a tadka beyond the spices becoming fragrant? | Review documented heat, fat, volatile compounds and ingredient variation in a named culinary context; exclude unsafe hot-oil demonstrations or universal timing rules. |
+| BANK-091-018 | How do dum, bhunao and ordinary simmering organize moisture and heat differently? | Compare attributed culinary descriptions and controlled original observations; preserve regional terminology rather than treating every household's usage as identical. |
+| BANK-091-019 | Why do different rice preparations require different expectations about separate grains or softness? | Compare identified rice types, preparation goals and tested methods; do not present one texture as the correct standard for every cuisine. |
+| BANK-091-020 | How can a dosa episode distinguish batter development from a single fixed fermentation schedule? | Document ingredients, temperature, acidity evidence and authoritative food-safety limits; avoid declaring a batter safe from smell or appearance alone. |
+
+## 03. Doughs, batters and structure
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-091-021 | How does kneading change a wheat dough compared with merely mixing it? | Compare documented protein networks, hydration and rest in original trials; avoid generalizing wheat behavior to every flour. |
+| BANK-091-022 | What makes a chemically leavened cake rise differently from a yeast bread? | Trace gas production, structure setting and ingredient roles through food-science sources; distinguish mechanism from a universal substitution ratio. |
+| BANK-091-023 | Why can a roti puff in one trial and remain flat in another? | Investigate thickness, moisture distribution, heat and handling through controlled observations; avoid attributing every failure to a single ingredient or skill judgment. |
+| BANK-091-024 | How can gluten-free baking be studied without assuming all gluten-free flours behave alike? | Compare specific starches, proteins, binders and tested recipes; keep allergy or coeliac-safety claims separate and grounded in current official guidance. |
+
+## 04. Flavor building and sensory comparison
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-091-025 | What distinguishes caramelization from browning involving proteins and sugars? | Use authoritative food-chemistry explanations and labeled examples; avoid diagnosing a reaction solely from brown color or giving hazardous high-heat procedures. |
+| BANK-091-026 | How does an acidic ingredient change a dish beyond making it taste sour? | Compare flavor, texture and documented ingredient interactions in a bounded recipe; do not infer preservation safety from sourness. |
+| BANK-091-027 | Why can whole and ground spices produce different results in the same dish? | Compare surface area, storage, extraction and timing in clearly labeled trials; avoid universal freshness or shelf-life claims. |
+| BANK-091-028 | How can a blind tasting reduce the influence of brand or presentation? | Specify coding, sample order, temperature and participant consent; distinguish a small preference test from representative consumer research. |
+
+## 05. Vegetables, legumes and proteins
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-091-029 | What should be compared when dried beans cook unevenly? | Inspect species, age, soaking conditions, water and tested cooking method using extension or food-science guidance; do not issue safety conclusions from tenderness alone. |
+| BANK-091-030 | How does vegetable cut size change cooking behavior as well as appearance? | Compare surface area, heat exposure and texture in original controlled samples; distinguish recipe goals from a universal ideal size. |
+| BANK-091-031 | What makes tofu, paneer and legumes different design choices in a meat-free dish? | Compare structure, moisture, flavor uptake and culinary context; avoid treating them as identical nutrition substitutes or prescribing a diet. |
+| BANK-091-032 | How can a cooking video discuss meat doneness without using color as its only evidence? | Use current official guidance on validated measurements, food type and equipment; exclude unverified temperature claims or judging safety from video footage. |
+
+## 06. Kitchen workflow and food safety
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Inspect current professional guidance and original studies for the exact population, intervention or measurement. Report comparators, absolute outcomes, uncertainty, harms and limits. Do not diagnose, prescribe or imply that education replaces professional care.
+
+**Source types to locate:** current official clinical or public-health guidance; original trials, cohort studies or diagnostic-validation reports; study registrations, protocols and adverse-event records. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-091-033 | How can mise en place improve a home meal without requiring restaurant-scale preparation? | Map recipe dependencies, limited counter space and cleanup in an original workflow; distinguish useful organization from a compulsory ritual. |
+| BANK-091-034 | What should a home-cooking episode verify before discussing cooling and reheating leftovers? | Consult current official time-temperature guidance for the actual food and setting; avoid homemade preservation rules or claims that reheating reverses every hazard. |
+| BANK-091-035 | How can an allergen-aware recipe distinguish ingredient choice from cross-contact control? | Use current labeling and food-safety authorities, actual product information and explicit limitations; never imply that omitting one ingredient guarantees an allergy-safe meal. |
+| BANK-091-036 | Why do pressure cookers and air fryers need model-specific instructions in a recipe test? | Compare capacity, controls, loading and manufacturer guidance; separate the observed recipe from approval for modified appliances or every model. |
+
+## 07. Culinary histories and adaptation
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-091-037 | How can a biryani comparison respect regional methods without declaring one universal original? | Use attributed regional cooks, traceable historical sources and clearly described versions; distinguish culinary identity from unsupported priority claims. |
+| BANK-091-038 | What happens when a restaurant dish is adapted to a small home kitchen? | Compare equipment, batch size, holding, timing and ingredient availability in a disclosed adaptation; do not imply identical results from materially different conditions. |
+| BANK-091-039 | How can a family recipe's oral history be documented without turning memory into verified chronology? | Obtain consent, record variations and distinguish remembered transmission from dated evidence; respect private knowledge and publication rights. |
+
+## 08. Recipe development, rights and honest media
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-091-040 | How can a recipe developer distinguish a tested substitution from a plausible idea? | Record the actual trial, changed variables, failures and sensory outcomes; label untested suggestions and separate culinary flexibility from food-safety claims. |
+| BANK-091-041 | What should an edited cooking video reveal about resting, chilling and preparation done off camera? | Disclose real elapsed time, prepared components, batch changes and retakes; avoid presenting an assembled sequence as one uninterrupted cook. |
+| BANK-091-042 | How can a creator credit a recipe's sources while adding original explanation? | Identify the attributed cook or publication, permission needs and substantive tested changes; do not assume attribution authorizes copying protected text, images or video. |
+| BANK-091-043 | How can a cooking episode include nutritional information without pretending to provide a personal meal plan? | State database, quantities, serving assumptions and calculation limits; avoid treatment claims, individualized dietary prescriptions or guaranteed nutrient values. |
+
+## Coverage limits and next step
+
+This is an expanded topic-bank draft, not exhaustive scholarship or a researched episode package. Each question has an individually written boundary. Source access, claim verification, current angle-scope review and publication approval remain pending. Add new branches and cases when they fill a real gap; no numeric topic cap applies.
+
+Select an exact case, edition, population, jurisdiction or product version, inspect sources and refine the question before using the existing registry idea/depth planning workflow. These BANK IDs are catalog identities, not approved IDEA IDs. Existing Q1-Q42 defaults, production queues and approval gates are unchanged.
