+# 093 Agriculture & Farming: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 32 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Agriculture & Farming. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Farming systems and local conditions
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-093-013 | How can a farm be understood as a system rather than a single crop? | Map land, labor, water, inputs, livestock and markets in one documented setting; avoid assuming one production model represents a whole region. |
+| BANK-093-014 | What does a crop plan need to know about soil and climate before discussing yield? | Use local soil testing, weather records and extension guidance; distinguish site-specific recommendations from a guaranteed harvest. |
+| BANK-093-015 | Why should a farming episode distinguish experimental plots from working farms? | Compare control, scale, resources, labor and weather exposure; avoid transferring a trial's result directly into an adoption recommendation. |
+| BANK-093-016 | How can farmer knowledge and formal research be compared without treating either as infallible? | Use consented accounts, clear questions and measured local observations; attribute experience and distinguish tested claims from proposals. |
+
+## 02. Soil, water and fertility
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-093-017 | What can a soil-health assessment measure beyond nutrient levels? | Compare structure, organic matter, biological indicators and sampling limitations using local authoritative methods; avoid a single score standing in for all functions. |
+| BANK-093-018 | How can irrigation scheduling account for crop stage and actual field conditions? | Inspect extension methods, weather, soil measurements and system performance; exclude universal watering quantities or unverified equipment claims. |
+| BANK-093-019 | Why can fertilizer response differ between fields receiving the same application? | Compare baseline fertility, water, soil properties, timing and crop conditions through original trials; avoid prescribing rates without site-specific assessment. |
+| BANK-093-020 | What does a salinity problem require before it can be distinguished from other crop stress? | Use laboratory or field measurements, water records and qualified agronomic interpretation; exclude diagnosis from leaf appearance alone. |
+
+## 03. Seed, establishment and crop management
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-093-021 | What should be compared when choosing between a crop variety and a named hybrid? | Inspect local trial performance, seed rules, disease traits and intended market; distinguish genetic category from guaranteed yield or seed-saving suitability. |
+| BANK-093-022 | How can planting density change competition and harvest quality? | Review crop-specific trials, spacing, water and machinery context; avoid converting one optimum into a universal field prescription. |
+| BANK-093-023 | Why do crop rotations need attention to plant families and local disease history? | Map actual sequences, residues, hosts and extension evidence; distinguish plausible breaks from proof that rotation removes every risk. |
+| BANK-093-024 | How can a farmer assess a new intercropping proposal without judging it only by total biomass? | Compare component yields, land use, labor, market demand and trial design; separate system performance from a promotional photograph. |
+
+## 04. Pests, disease and integrated management
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-093-025 | What observations are needed before naming the cause of crop damage? | Record distribution, timing, plant stage, weather and identified organisms with qualified diagnosis; avoid recommending a pesticide from an image alone. |
+| BANK-093-026 | How can a pest-monitoring threshold be distinguished from noticing the first insect? | Use crop- and region-specific extension definitions, sampling methods and economic context; exclude improvised treatment triggers. |
+| BANK-093-027 | Why can pesticide resistance become a management problem over time? | Explain documented selection, monitoring and official stewardship guidance; exclude unapproved mixtures, application recipes or evasion of label requirements. |
+| BANK-093-028 | What should a biological-control claim establish about the agent and the local ecosystem? | Inspect authorization, target range, field evidence and unintended effects; exclude releasing organisms or transferring species without official approval. |
+
+## 05. Livestock, mixed farms and welfare
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Inspect current professional guidance and original studies for the exact population, intervention or measurement. Report comparators, absolute outcomes, uncertainty, harms and limits. Do not diagnose, prescribe or imply that education replaces professional care.
+
+**Source types to locate:** current official clinical or public-health guidance; original trials, cohort studies or diagnostic-validation reports; study registrations, protocols and adverse-event records. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-093-029 | How can a mixed-farming episode distinguish nutrient cycling from uncontrolled waste handling? | Trace manure, storage, crop use and current environmental and animal-health guidance; do not infer sanitary safety from traditional use alone. |
+| BANK-093-030 | What does livestock welfare require beyond weight gain or output? | Use species-specific authority guidance, behavior, housing and health indicators; exclude diagnosing individual animals or prescribing treatments. |
+| BANK-093-031 | Why should animal-disease information be tied to the exact species, place and date? | Consult current veterinary authorities and official reporting procedures; distinguish general biosecurity education from an outbreak claim or medication recommendation. |
+
+## 06. Harvest, storage and value chains
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-093-032 | What changes between harvest maturity and readiness for storage or sale? | Compare crop-specific measures, handling damage, moisture and buyer requirements using official guidance; avoid one visual test for all produce. |
+| BANK-093-033 | How can postharvest losses be measured without counting every unsold item as physically spoiled? | Separate quantity, quality, market rejection and food use with explicit definitions; use documented sampling rather than invented loss percentages. |
+| BANK-093-034 | Why can cold storage help one product but damage another? | Use crop-specific official storage evidence, chilling sensitivity and handling conditions; exclude universal temperature or shelf-life prescriptions. |
+| BANK-093-035 | What does a cooperative change about a farmer's access to inputs and buyers? | Inspect governance, fees, services and consented member experiences in one named case; distinguish organizational promises from measured outcomes. |
+
+## 07. Economics, technology and climate adaptation
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use current official terms, filings or macroeconomic data; state jurisdiction, date, fees, taxes, risk, units and assumptions. Use transparent illustrative calculations rather than personal recommendations or return promises.
+
+**Source types to locate:** regulator or official statistical publications; actual contracts, fee schedules and audited filings; original economic studies and disclosed calculation inputs. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-093-036 | How can a farm budget distinguish gross revenue from household income? | Map variable costs, fixed costs, labor, debt and retained output using illustrative figures; verify actual prices and local taxes before scripting financial conclusions. |
+| BANK-093-037 | What should be measured before claiming a precision-farming tool pays for itself? | Compare baseline performance, equipment, training, subscriptions, failures and adoption scale; separate demonstration capability from verified returns. |
+| BANK-093-038 | How can climate-adaptation options be compared without assuming a forecast is certain? | Use official local scenarios, crop sensitivities, costs and decision horizons; distinguish conditional planning from predicting a particular season. |
+| BANK-093-039 | Why can a higher-yielding crop still leave a farm financially vulnerable? | Compare input dependence, price variation, debt, insurance and market access in transparent scenarios; avoid personal investment or borrowing advice. |
+| BANK-093-040 | How can agricultural-water comparisons avoid confusing withdrawal, consumption and local scarcity? | Define the metric, basin, season and production boundary using official data; do not turn a global average into a local impact verdict. |
+
+## 08. Land, labor and claims of sustainability
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-093-041 | What does organic certification require in one jurisdiction and leave unassessed? | Read the current official standard, audit scope and allowed claims; distinguish certification from automatic conclusions about yield, nutrition or every environmental impact. |
+| BANK-093-042 | How can a land-tenure episode distinguish legal title from practical access to farming? | Use official records, local rules and consented accounts for a named setting; avoid deciding an individual's property rights. |
+| BANK-093-043 | Why should a farm documentary include seasonal workers and unpaid family labor? | Map tasks, pay where documented, working conditions and consented perspectives; do not infer absence of labor from mechanized footage. |
+| BANK-093-044 | What evidence would justify calling a farming system regenerative? | Define the actual practices, baseline, measured outcomes and reporting period; distinguish marketing terminology from a certified or demonstrated improvement. |
