+# 094 Rural Life & Village Culture: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 28 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Rural Life & Village Culture. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Describing a village through its residents
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Use consented participant accounts, local records and the community's own descriptions. Include differences of position, access and power; testimony is not a representative survey or clinical proof.
+
+**Source types to locate:** consented interviews and oral histories; community publications and local records; field notes with method and consent documented. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-094-013 | How can a village documentary begin with residents' questions rather than an outsider's idea of simple living? | Use informed consent, varied households and local priorities to define the story; avoid presenting one family as the voice of an entire place. |
+| BANK-094-014 | What does a day-in-the-life film omit if it records only visually attractive work? | Map planning, waiting, care, travel and seasonal demands alongside visible tasks; distinguish one filmed day from the village's ordinary annual rhythm. |
+| BANK-094-015 | How can local place names and settlement histories be recorded responsibly? | Compare speakers, maps, official records and attributed memories; preserve disagreement and do not treat one oral account as the complete chronology. |
+
+## 02. Livelihoods beyond a single farm image
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use consented participant accounts, local records and the community's own descriptions. Include differences of position, access and power; testimony is not a representative survey or clinical proof.
+
+**Source types to locate:** consented interviews and oral histories; community publications and local records; field notes with method and consent documented. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-094-016 | What can a rural household's livelihood include beyond cultivating its own land? | Trace wage work, services, remittances, crafts and unpaid labor through consented accounts; avoid publishing private finances or assuming all residents own farmland. |
+| BANK-094-017 | How does seasonal work change a village's daily schedule? | Compare labor, school, transport and care across documented seasons; separate observed patterns from claims about every household. |
+| BANK-094-018 | What changes when a local market connects producers with outside buyers? | Examine transport, intermediaries, bargaining and actual terms in one market; avoid assuming every intermediary is exploitative or every direct sale is better. |
+| BANK-094-019 | How can a craft household describe its income without being reduced to an endangered tradition? | Center maker choices, contemporary customers, costs and alternatives; distinguish continuity, innovation and economic pressure using consented accounts. |
+
+## 03. Homes, infrastructure and daily access
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-094-020 | What does reliable water access require beyond the presence of a tap? | Compare source, availability, quality records, maintenance and household access in one location; do not infer drinking-water safety from clarity or appearance. |
+| BANK-094-021 | How can electricity access differ from reliable electricity service? | Use official connection records, outages, costs and consented experiences; distinguish a connection count from the quality available to all households. |
+| BANK-094-022 | Why can a new road change a village unevenly? | Trace travel, services, land use, trade and residents' differing experiences with dated evidence; avoid treating infrastructure as either automatic progress or inevitable loss. |
+| BANK-094-023 | What should a rural connectivity story investigate beyond mobile-signal bars? | Compare devices, cost, language, digital skills and actual service tasks; avoid equating network coverage with universal useful access. |
+
+## 04. Education, care and public services
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use consented participant accounts, local records and the community's own descriptions. Include differences of position, access and power; testimony is not a representative survey or clinical proof.
+
+**Source types to locate:** consented interviews and oral histories; community publications and local records; field notes with method and consent documented. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-094-024 | How can a rural-school story include teaching conditions without judging children from one lesson? | Use official records, consented educators and protected student perspectives; separate resources, attendance and learning evidence and avoid filming minors without appropriate permission. |
+| BANK-094-025 | What does a journey to healthcare reveal about access beyond the nearest clinic's distance? | Map transport, opening hours, referral pathways, costs and current official service information; exclude diagnosis or claims about an individual facility from rumor. |
+| BANK-094-026 | How do households organize elder care when younger adults work elsewhere? | Use consented accounts of visits, money, daily assistance and local support; avoid assuming migration means abandonment or that family care is always available. |
+
+## 05. Migration, identity and changing generations
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use consented participant accounts, local records and the community's own descriptions. Include differences of position, access and power; testimony is not a representative survey or clinical proof.
+
+**Source types to locate:** consented interviews and oral histories; community publications and local records; field notes with method and consent documented. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-094-027 | How can migration create ongoing village connections instead of a simple story of departure? | Trace remittances, visits, land, care and return plans through willing participants; distinguish intentions from guaranteed future moves. |
+| BANK-094-028 | What changes when a returning resident starts a new enterprise? | Compare local knowledge, capital, market access and community responses in a documented case; avoid presenting one success as an easy route for every migrant. |
+| BANK-094-029 | How can youth perspectives challenge a nostalgic account of village life? | Include consented views on opportunity, autonomy, language and belonging; avoid editing disagreement into a generational stereotype. |
+| BANK-094-030 | Why can a rural settlement contain several different ideas of home? | Compare long-term residents, newcomers and absent members with permission; preserve differences rather than treating community identity as unanimous. |
+
+## 06. Festivals, food and public culture
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Identify the text, edition, translator, commentary, community and ritual context as applicable. Attribute theological and spiritual claims, preserve regional variation and respect public versus restricted knowledge.
+
+**Source types to locate:** primary texts in traceable editions; authorized public teaching and ritual descriptions; consented community accounts and critical historical studies. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-094-031 | How can a village festival be documented without exposing restricted rituals? | Agree filming boundaries with authorized participants, attribute meanings and include preparation labor; distinguish public celebration from unrestricted cultural access. |
+| BANK-094-032 | What can a local food tradition reveal about season, labor and trade? | Use consented cooks, ingredient histories and market evidence; keep food-safety claims separate from the value of inherited practice. |
+| BANK-094-033 | How do songs or oral narratives change between private use and a staged cultural programme? | Compare permitted performances, audience, purpose and transmission with attributed accounts; obtain rights and avoid calling one version the authentic original without evidence. |
+
+## 07. Governance, inequality and environmental change
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-094-034 | How can village governance be explained through an actual public-service decision? | Use current official powers, meeting records and multiple resident perspectives; distinguish written authority, implementation and contested outcomes. |
+| BANK-094-035 | Why should a documentary investigate who cannot access a shared village resource? | Compare documented rules, location, costs and consented experiences of exclusion; avoid identifying vulnerable participants or making unsupported allegations. |
+| BANK-094-036 | How can a changing water body be studied without blaming only the nearest residents? | Trace catchment, land use, official monitoring and competing demands; distinguish measured changes from an assumed single local cause. |
+| BANK-094-037 | What does a rural disaster-recovery story need after the dramatic event footage ends? | Follow official assistance, housing, livelihoods and consented experiences over time; avoid declaring recovery complete from a reopened road or a donated asset. |
+
+## 08. Ethical production and representation
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-094-038 | How can compensation and consent be handled when filming residents' daily work? | Agree participation, payment, rights, withdrawal limits and intended distribution; distinguish voluntary cooperation from consent inferred through a local intermediary. |
+| BANK-094-039 | What should a rural-life creator disclose about staging and repeated tasks? | Label recreated sequences, time compression and directing choices; avoid presenting an arranged activity as spontaneous evidence of everyday behavior. |
+| BANK-094-040 | How can a village episode give viewers enough location context without exposing private homes or sensitive sites? | Use agreed geographic detail, anonymization and community review where appropriate; do not publish protected locations or assume filming access grants publication consent. |
+
+## Coverage limits and next step
+
+This is an expanded topic-bank draft, not exhaustive scholarship or a researched episode package. Each question has an individually written boundary. Source access, claim verification, current angle-scope review and publication approval remain pending. Add new branches and cases when they fill a real gap; no numeric topic cap applies.
+
+Select an exact case, edition, population, jurisdiction or product version, inspect sources and refine the question before using the existing registry idea/depth planning workflow. These BANK IDs are catalog identities, not approved IDEA IDs. Existing Q1-Q42 defaults, production queues and approval gates are unchanged.
