+# 101 Memory & Learning: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 31 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Memory & Learning. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Memory systems and everyday experience
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-101-010 | How can remembering an event differ from knowing a fact or performing a skill? | Compare episodic, semantic and procedural constructs through original research and harmless examples; avoid mapping each system to one isolated brain region. |
+| BANK-101-011 | What does working memory describe beyond briefly storing a few items? | Review task demands, manipulation, attention and competing models; distinguish measured performance from a fixed personal capacity score. |
+| BANK-101-012 | Why can recognizing an answer feel easier than recalling it without prompts? | Compare cue availability, familiarity and retrieval in original studies; avoid treating recognition as proof of full understanding. |
+| BANK-101-013 | How can a memory be vivid while some details remain inaccurate? | Examine confidence, reconstruction and corroboration in published experiments; do not infer truth or deception from how emotionally a person remembers. |
+
+## 02. Encoding, attention and organization
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-101-014 | What happens when attention is divided while new information is being learned? | Compare task type, interference, timing and later tests in original experiments; avoid exporting one laboratory effect to every everyday task. |
+| BANK-101-015 | How can organizing information by meaning change later recall? | Use a controlled original word example and traceable studies; distinguish semantic processing from promises of permanent retention. |
+| BANK-101-016 | Why can prior knowledge help memory while also introducing errors? | Compare schemas, expectation and reconstruction using published paradigms; avoid implying that all inference is false memory. |
+| BANK-101-017 | What does chunking change when the chunks are genuinely familiar? | Compare novices, experienced participants and structured material; distinguish learned organization from a trick that expands capacity without prerequisites. |
+
+## 03. Forgetting and interference
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-101-018 | What did Ebbinghaus's forgetting experiments measure and leave untested? | Read traceable original or authoritative historical sources on materials, participants and procedure; do not turn one curve into a universal daily retention schedule. |
+| BANK-101-019 | How do proactive and retroactive interference ask different questions about forgetting? | Use original studies and labeled learning sequences; distinguish competing material from assumptions that stored memories were physically erased. |
+| BANK-101-020 | Why can forgetting a name differ from failing to recognize the person? | Compare retrieval cues and task definitions in primary research; exclude diagnosing a viewer from an ordinary lapse. |
+| BANK-101-021 | When can a context change make learned information harder to retrieve? | Review controlled studies, cue conditions and generalization limits; avoid suggesting that studying only in one location is always best. |
+
+## 04. Retrieval, spacing and durable learning
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-101-022 | What does a retrieval-practice study need to show beyond an immediate quiz improvement? | Inspect delayed tests, feedback, comparison groups and transfer; keep this bank's cognitive mechanism focus distinct from school-policy coverage in bank 069. |
+| BANK-101-023 | Why can spacing intervals depend on the time at which knowledge will be needed? | Compare original schedule studies and target delays; avoid one optimal calendar for every person, subject and deadline. |
+| BANK-101-024 | How can self-testing reveal both knowledge and misleading confidence? | Compare answers, confidence ratings and later performance in a voluntary exercise; distinguish calibration from a diagnosis of ability. |
+| BANK-101-025 | What makes a retrieval cue helpful in one context but misleading in another? | Examine cue distinctiveness, overlap and study conditions; avoid treating a mnemonic's memorability as proof it supports understanding. |
+
+## 05. Strategies and skill acquisition
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-101-026 | How does the method of loci organize retrieval without replacing subject knowledge? | Use an original nonsensitive list, published experiments and task limits; separate memory competition from everyday learning and avoid universal performance promises. |
+| BANK-101-027 | What does elaborative explanation add beyond rereading the same passage? | Compare generated connections, accuracy checks and original learning studies; distinguish useful elaboration from rehearsing an incorrect explanation. |
+| BANK-101-028 | Why can blocked practice feel smoother while a different schedule produces better later performance? | Review task-specific interleaving evidence, prior knowledge and test conditions; avoid claiming that harder-feeling practice is always superior. |
+| BANK-101-029 | How do perceptual and motor learning differ from memorizing verbal facts? | Compare training tasks, feedback and retention measures in original studies; do not transfer results between domains without evidence. |
+
+## 06. Sleep, emotion and clinical boundaries
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Inspect current professional guidance and original studies for the exact population, intervention or measurement. Report comparators, absolute outcomes, uncertainty, harms and limits. Do not diagnose, prescribe or imply that education replaces professional care.
+
+**Source types to locate:** current official clinical or public-health guidance; original trials, cohort studies or diagnostic-validation reports; study registrations, protocols and adverse-event records. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-101-030 | What does research on sleep and memory actually compare? | Inspect learning time, sleep measures, control conditions and later outcomes in primary studies; exclude personalized sleep prescriptions or claims that sleep guarantees recall. |
+| BANK-101-031 | How can stress affect encoding and retrieval differently? | Compare timing, intensity, population and task in original research; avoid explaining an individual's symptoms from a general mechanism. |
+| BANK-101-032 | Why should normal memory variation be distinguished from concerns that need professional assessment? | Use current official clinical guidance and clearly bounded educational language; do not diagnose from a checklist or offer reassurance about specific symptoms. |
+| BANK-101-033 | What evidence would a memory-training product need to show broad everyday benefits? | Compare trained tasks, active controls, transfer, follow-up and conflicts of interest; distinguish improved game scores from demonstrated clinical prevention. |
+
+## 07. Research methods, replication and myths
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-101-034 | What can the Stroop task reveal about interference without being a general intelligence test? | Inspect original methods, task variants and measured outcomes; avoid scoring viewers or converting performance into a diagnosis. |
+| BANK-101-035 | How can a false-memory experiment distinguish suggestion from ordinary forgetting? | Review ethical protocols, ground truth and later recall tests; avoid demonstrations intended to implant consequential beliefs in real people. |
+| BANK-101-036 | Why do laboratory word lists provide limited evidence about autobiographical memory? | Compare material, stakes, timescale and verification; distinguish controlled mechanism research from a complete model of lived recollection. |
+| BANK-101-037 | How should a learning episode handle an influential study with mixed replication results? | Compare original and replication methods, sample uncertainty and competing explanations; avoid treating replication as either automatic disproof or irrelevant criticism. |
+
+## 08. Records, technology and responsible interpretation
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Inspect the actual product version, official documentation, terms and privacy policies. Test claims under stated conditions and distinguish account permissions, data retention, measured functionality and marketing.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official versioned documentation and release notes; dated privacy policies and service terms; reproducible product tests and original validation reports. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-101-038 | How can external notes change what a person needs to remember? | Compare access, retrieval, verification and original studies on cognitive offloading; avoid assuming either digital tools or handwriting universally improves memory. |
+| BANK-101-039 | What does a spaced-repetition app's algorithm know and not know about understanding? | Inspect official scheduling documentation, self-ratings and tested outcomes; distinguish prediction of card recall from mastery of a subject. |
+| BANK-101-040 | How can a learner audit a study method without turning a small experiment into universal advice? | Specify baseline, comparable material, delayed tests and limitations; keep results illustrative and avoid fabricated improvement percentages. |
+
+## Coverage limits and next step
+
+This is an expanded topic-bank draft, not exhaustive scholarship or a researched episode package. Each question has an individually written boundary. Source access, claim verification, current angle-scope review and publication approval remain pending. Add new branches and cases when they fill a real gap; no numeric topic cap applies.
+
+Select an exact case, edition, population, jurisdiction or product version, inspect sources and refine the question before using the existing registry idea/depth planning workflow. These BANK IDs are catalog identities, not approved IDEA IDs. Existing Q1-Q42 defaults, production queues and approval gates are unchanged.
