+# 102 Communication: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 30 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Communication. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Meaning, audience and context
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-102-010 | Why can a technically accurate message still fail to answer its audience's question? | Compare assumed knowledge, purpose, vocabulary and missing context in original examples; distinguish accuracy from usefulness without prescribing one universal style. |
+| BANK-102-011 | How can communication goals be separated into informing, requesting and persuading? | Rewrite one harmless message for each goal and inspect the changed evidence and action; do not equate clarity with agreement. |
+| BANK-102-012 | What changes when a message moves from conversation to a permanent written record? | Compare context, ambiguity, audience expansion and correction options; avoid publishing real private exchanges as examples. |
+
+## 02. Listening and asking useful questions
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use consented participant accounts, local records and the community's own descriptions. Include differences of position, access and power; testimony is not a representative survey or clinical proof.
+
+**Source types to locate:** consented interviews and oral histories; community publications and local records; field notes with method and consent documented. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-102-013 | How can a clarifying question reduce uncertainty without making the speaker repeat everything? | Use original service or workplace exchanges to distinguish missing facts, assumptions and desired outcomes; avoid interrogation scripts or diagnosis of hidden motives. |
+| BANK-102-014 | What does active listening require beyond repeating the other person's words? | Compare checking meaning, acknowledging uncertainty and allowing correction in fictional dialogue; do not promise a technique will resolve every disagreement. |
+| BANK-102-015 | How can an interview question invite detail without placing an answer in the participant's mouth? | Compare open, leading and compound questions using a consented research context; preserve participants' ability to decline. |
+| BANK-102-016 | Why can silence mean different things across people and situations? | Use attributed cultural accounts and conversation studies with their limits; avoid treating pause length as proof of dishonesty or emotion. |
+
+## 03. Explaining complex material
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-102-017 | How can an explanation introduce a technical term exactly when the audience needs it? | Build an original process explanation with staged vocabulary and examples; distinguish reduced jargon from removing necessary precision. |
+| BANK-102-018 | When does an analogy clarify a mechanism and when does it mislead? | Map the useful correspondences and failed correspondences in a safe original example; label the analogy instead of treating it as evidence. |
+| BANK-102-019 | How can a concrete example expose a misunderstanding that a definition hides? | Compare examples, counterexamples and audience questions in an original lesson; avoid claiming an explanation was understood without appropriate feedback. |
+| BANK-102-020 | What makes a visual and its narration work together rather than compete? | Analyze pacing, labels and information order in an original storyboard; distinguish a design hypothesis from measured cognitive effects. |
+
+## 04. Writing, editing and document structure
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-102-021 | How can an email make the requested action visible without sounding artificially urgent? | Revise an invented message around purpose, necessary context, owner and timing; avoid manipulative pressure or promises that wording guarantees a response. |
+| BANK-102-022 | What should an executive summary retain from a longer report? | Compare decision, evidence, uncertainty and implications using original material; do not hide qualifications that would change the conclusion. |
+| BANK-102-023 | How can a paragraph develop one claim without becoming a list of disconnected facts? | Revise original prose for topic, support, relationships and transitions; distinguish stylistic preference from a single compulsory sentence pattern. |
+| BANK-102-024 | Why can plain language require more precision rather than fewer details? | Compare official-style instructions with an original clear revision; preserve eligibility, exceptions and consequences instead of simplifying away the rule. |
+
+## 05. Speaking, presentations and facilitation
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-102-025 | How can a presentation be organized around what the audience must decide? | Use an original briefing to connect the question, evidence, options and tradeoffs; avoid treating a slide template as the argument. |
+| BANK-102-026 | What changes when a speaker rehearses for timing instead of memorizing every word? | Compare transitions, examples, pauses and recovery in voluntary practice; exclude universal claims about performance anxiety or personality. |
+| BANK-102-027 | How can a facilitator keep a discussion understandable without deciding every outcome? | Map turn-taking, scope, records and unresolved questions in a fictional meeting; distinguish process authority from control over participants' views. |
+| BANK-102-028 | What makes a question-and-answer response useful when the speaker lacks the requested fact? | Compare acknowledging uncertainty, stating known limits and a concrete verification path; avoid inventing an answer to preserve confidence. |
+
+## 06. Cross-language communication and access
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use consented participant accounts, local records and the community's own descriptions. Include differences of position, access and power; testimony is not a representative survey or clinical proof.
+
+**Source types to locate:** consented interviews and oral histories; community publications and local records; field notes with method and consent documented. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-102-029 | Why can literal translation preserve words while changing the social meaning of a request? | Compare register, idiom and context with qualified speakers using original examples; avoid stereotyping an entire language community's politeness. |
+| BANK-102-030 | How can an interpreter-mediated conversation preserve the participant's own voice? | Use recognized professional guidance on roles, turn-taking, confidentiality and clarification; distinguish interpreting from speaking on behalf of the person. |
+| BANK-102-031 | What should accessible communication offer beyond captions? | Compare readable text, audio description, sign-language access, navigation and user-defined needs; do not assume one format serves every disability. |
+| BANK-102-032 | How can a public message be tested with readers who differ from its authors? | Plan consented task-based checks, misunderstandings and revisions; distinguish usability evidence from a representative opinion poll. |
+
+## 07. Disagreement, feedback and misinformation
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Define the rival claims and their evidence standards. Compare sources on equal terms, separate empirical findings from values or interpretations and do not imply that all positions have equal evidential support.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the original sources behind the compared claims; explicit definitions and measurement methods; independent critiques with traceable evidence. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-102-033 | How can disagreement be made specific enough for evidence to matter? | Separate facts, definitions, values and proposed actions in original dialogue; avoid suggesting every conflict can or should be negotiated away. |
+| BANK-102-034 | What makes feedback actionable without turning it into a verdict on a person's character? | Compare observable work, consequences, expectations and support in fictional examples; avoid disclosing private performance information. |
+| BANK-102-035 | How should a correction connect the false claim with the evidence that replaces it? | Use original harmless misinformation and traceable primary sources; avoid repeating a harmful allegation unnecessarily or inventing audience-impact data. |
+| BANK-102-036 | Why can certainty in delivery be mistaken for certainty in evidence? | Compare equivalent scripts with different confidence language and evidence quality; distinguish an illustrative contrast from proof about every audience. |
+
+## 08. Digital channels and accountability
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Inspect the actual product version, official documentation, terms and privacy policies. Test claims under stated conditions and distinguish account permissions, data retention, measured functionality and marketing.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official versioned documentation and release notes; dated privacy policies and service terms; reproducible product tests and original validation reports. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-102-037 | What should a team decide before moving every conversation into a group chat? | Compare urgency, searchability, access, retention and decision records using current tool documentation; avoid assuming a new platform resolves organizational ambiguity. |
+| BANK-102-038 | How can an AI-assisted message preserve a human author's intended meaning? | Check factual claims, commitments, tone, privacy and actual recipient context; disclose assistance where required and do not send examples to real people. |
+| BANK-102-039 | What does a communication audit need beyond counting messages sent? | Map purpose, audience, comprehension, action and unwanted burden with ethical measurements; avoid presenting output volume as communication success. |
