+# 105 Consumer Psychology: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 31 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Consumer Psychology. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Consumer decisions and evidence standards
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-105-010 | How can a purchase decision be studied without assuming shoppers are either rational or foolish? | Compare goals, information, constraints and actual behavior in original research; distinguish a simplified economic model from a judgment of individuals. |
+| BANK-105-011 | What is the difference between a stated purchase intention and an observed transaction? | Inspect survey design, context, follow-up and behavioral measures; avoid treating willingness to buy as verified demand. |
+| BANK-105-012 | Why can the same product be evaluated differently when the comparison set changes? | Use original harmless examples and primary choice research; identify the alternatives and avoid universal predictions about a viewer. |
+
+## 02. Price, reference points and perceived value
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-105-013 | How does a displayed reference price affect interpretation of a discount? | Review original experiments, actual price history and current consumer rules; distinguish a psychological hypothesis from proof that a particular discount is deceptive. |
+| BANK-105-014 | What can anchoring studies establish about judgments of product value? | Compare anchor relevance, task design, populations and replication; avoid promising a technique will control another person's spending. |
+| BANK-105-015 | Why can a bundle feel different from buying its components separately? | Inspect pricing transparency, usage needs and original choice studies; distinguish perceived savings from the actual total cost. |
+| BANK-105-016 | How can loss aversion be examined without using it as an explanation for every purchase? | Read the exact experimental tasks, competing models and boundary conditions; avoid translating group tendencies into guaranteed individual behavior. |
+
+## 03. Attention, framing and presentation
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-105-017 | How can a product's position on a shelf affect what is noticed? | Review field and laboratory methods, visibility, movement and competing factors; distinguish attention from purchase causation. |
+| BANK-105-018 | What changes when a product feature is framed as a gain rather than an avoided loss? | Compare matched wording, audience, stakes and measured outcomes in original research; exclude manipulative targeting of vulnerable users. |
+| BANK-105-019 | Why can a package color suggest a flavor or category without determining every response? | Use controlled perception studies and cultural context; separate associations, actual sensory differences and marketing intention. |
+| BANK-105-020 | How can cognitive load affect the usefulness of a complicated price comparison? | Inspect task demands, information format and original decision studies; avoid claiming consumers cannot understand options because of personal deficits. |
+
+## 04. Social information, identity and trust
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-105-021 | What does social-proof research distinguish between popularity and credible expertise? | Compare source information, context and actual behavior measures; avoid treating follower count as evidence of product quality. |
+| BANK-105-022 | How can a product become part of an identity without reducing buyers to stereotypes? | Use consented accounts and original consumer research on meaning and affiliation; distinguish interpretation from a fixed personality diagnosis. |
+| BANK-105-023 | Why can a testimonial be compelling while remaining weak evidence of typical results? | Inspect selection, verification, incentives and missing outcomes; separate one person's experience from causal or representative evidence. |
+| BANK-105-024 | How should parasocial relationships be discussed in influencer marketing? | Review original definitions, measurement and populations; avoid inferring a particular viewer's attachment or recommending exploitative persuasion. |
+
+## 05. Choice architecture and digital commerce
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Inspect the actual product version, official documentation, terms and privacy policies. Test claims under stated conditions and distinguish account permissions, data retention, measured functionality and marketing.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official versioned documentation and release notes; dated privacy policies and service terms; reproducible product tests and original validation reports. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-105-025 | When does a default option change behavior and what else must be measured? | Compare notice, ease of switching, preference and actual outcomes in original studies; avoid assuming every default is beneficial or coercive. |
+| BANK-105-026 | How can a checkout design be evaluated for informed choice rather than conversion alone? | Inspect total price, consent, reversibility and current regulator guidance; exclude instructions for concealing fees or making cancellation difficult. |
+| BANK-105-027 | What distinguishes a genuine limited offer from an urgency claim that needs verification? | Check current terms, stock or timing evidence and official consumer rules; do not infer deception from a countdown alone. |
+| BANK-105-028 | Why can personalization make it hard to compare two users' shopping experiences? | Inspect account state, region, documented testing conditions and privacy terms; avoid collecting sensitive profiles or assuming every price difference is discriminatory. |
+
+## 06. Satisfaction, ownership and postpurchase behavior
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
