+| BANK-105-030 | Why can product expectations change reported satisfaction after use? | Review original studies, actual performance and measurement timing; avoid claiming disappointment proves the product failed or the buyer was irrational. |
+| BANK-105-031 | What does a return reveal and leave unknown about a customer's experience? | Compare fit, defect, timing, policy and stated reasons using anonymized aggregate data; distinguish recorded reasons from complete motives. |
+| BANK-105-032 | How can sunk-cost reasoning be distinguished from a legitimate cost of switching? | Use transparent hypothetical examples with future costs and benefits explicit; do not prescribe personal financial decisions. |
+
+## 07. Methods, replication and cultural limits
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-105-033 | What must an A/B test establish before a design change is credited with more purchases? | Inspect assignment, exposure, sample size, duration and outcome definitions; distinguish significance from practical effect and include consent or privacy requirements. |
+| BANK-105-034 | Why can online experiments produce different results from shopping in a physical store? | Compare participants, stakes, attention and context in original studies; avoid treating either setting as automatically more truthful. |
+| BANK-105-035 | How can consumer research avoid turning demographic averages into individual stereotypes? | Inspect sampling, distributions, uncertainty and within-group variation; exclude sensitive targeting or inferred vulnerability. |
+| BANK-105-036 | What should change when a famous consumer-psychology effect fails to replicate consistently? | Compare original tasks, replication conditions and competing explanations; avoid declaring the entire field proven or disproven from one result. |
+
+## 08. Consumer protection and responsible explanation
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
