+# 106 Marketing & Advertising: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 32 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Marketing & Advertising. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Audience, offer and the marketing question
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-106-010 | How can a marketing brief define the problem before choosing a channel? | Specify audience task, offer, evidence, constraints and desired outcome in an original brief; distinguish communication goals from unsupported sales promises. |
+| BANK-106-011 | What makes a value proposition assessable rather than merely memorable? | Connect a defined customer need, specific capability and verifiable evidence; avoid implying a slogan proves superiority. |
+| BANK-106-012 | How does market research differ from asking existing followers which product they like? | Compare sampling, question wording, noncustomers and observed behavior; distinguish audience feedback from representative market demand. |
+| BANK-106-013 | Why can a customer segment be useful without becoming a stereotype? | Define actual needs, context and measurement limits; exclude sensitive profiling or assumptions about individuals from demographic averages. |
+
+## 02. Positioning, brand and creative development
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-106-014 | How can positioning be tested against the alternatives customers actually consider? | Use consented research, explicit comparison criteria and current product facts; avoid constructing weak rivals to manufacture an advantage. |
+| BANK-106-015 | What changes between a brand's intended identity and how audiences describe it? | Compare official messaging with ethical research and varied responses; distinguish recognition, reputation and purchase preference. |
+| BANK-106-016 | How can a creative concept translate one clear promise into several formats? | Develop original print, audio and video examples with consistent evidence; preserve necessary qualifications rather than shrinking them out of short formats. |
+| BANK-106-017 | Why can distinctive design help recognition without proving a brand is trusted? | Inspect original recognition and trust studies separately; avoid treating visual consistency as evidence of product quality. |
+
+## 03. Channels, content and distribution
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Inspect the actual product version, official documentation, terms and privacy policies. Test claims under stated conditions and distinguish account permissions, data retention, measured functionality and marketing.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official versioned documentation and release notes; dated privacy policies and service terms; reproducible product tests and original validation reports. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-106-018 | How should a search campaign distinguish an audience's question from a chosen keyword? | Use current platform documentation and an original intent map; separate query relevance, landing-page usefulness and actual conversions. |
+| BANK-106-019 | What does an email programme need beyond a growing contact list? | Inspect consent, useful content, deliverability, accessibility and current local requirements; exclude purchased-list spam or evasion of opt-outs. |
+| BANK-106-020 | How can a social-video campaign be planned around audience understanding rather than views alone? | Map hook, explanation, claim support and next action in original content; distinguish attention from verified learning or purchase. |
+| BANK-106-021 | What changes when a campaign relies on creators rather than the brand's own channels? | Compare rights, disclosure, creative control, audience fit and measurement using dated contracts or illustrative terms; avoid assuming followers equal customers. |
+
+## 04. Landing pages and customer journeys
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-106-022 | How can a landing page answer the uncertainties created by an advertisement? | Connect the actual offer, price, limitations, evidence and action in an original page outline; exclude hidden conditions or misleading urgency. |
+| BANK-106-023 | Why can a high click-through rate coexist with poor customer outcomes? | Inspect expectation mismatch, page performance, offer eligibility and downstream measures; distinguish enticing language from useful acquisition. |
+| BANK-106-024 | What makes an accessible call to action understandable beyond its visual prominence? | Compare wording, keyboard access, labels and consented task testing; avoid treating button color as a universal conversion solution. |
+| BANK-106-025 | How can a campaign include people who need more time or information before deciding? | Compare clear follow-up, saved information and voluntary contact options; exclude coercive retargeting or assumptions that delay means a weak prospect. |
+
+## 05. Measurement, experiments and attribution
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-106-026 | What does an advertising attribution model assume about credit for a sale? | Read the actual platform methodology, lookback windows and channel visibility; distinguish assigned credit from causal incrementality. |
+| BANK-106-027 | How can a holdout test examine whether advertising produced additional demand? | Specify assignment, spillovers, baseline, uncertainty and ethical data handling; avoid invented lift figures or overgeneralizing a small test. |
+| BANK-106-028 | Why can acquisition cost look better when the wrong customer population is counted? | Define new versus returning customers, fees, cancellations and time horizon; use transparent illustrative calculations rather than reporting fabricated campaign results. |
+| BANK-106-029 | What should an A/B creative test hold constant so the comparison is meaningful? | Compare audience, budget, exposure, timing and outcomes using published methods; distinguish a learning experiment from testing unlimited variants until one appears significant. |
+
+## 06. Economics, retention and operations
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use current official terms, filings or macroeconomic data; state jurisdiction, date, fees, taxes, risk, units and assumptions. Use transparent illustrative calculations rather than personal recommendations or return promises.
+
+**Source types to locate:** regulator or official statistical publications; actual contracts, fee schedules and audited filings; original economic studies and disclosed calculation inputs. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-106-030 | How should customer lifetime value be discussed when future retention is uncertain? | Show assumptions about margin, repeat behavior, discounting and costs in illustrative scenarios; avoid guaranteed payback or personalized investment claims. |
+| BANK-106-031 | What changes when a campaign succeeds beyond the company's delivery capacity? | Map inventory, staffing, service promises, returns and complaint handling; distinguish demand generation from fulfilled customer value. |
+| BANK-106-032 | Why can retaining customers require product or service change rather than more messages? | Use documented feedback, actual usage and operational evidence; avoid treating every departure as a communication problem. |
+| BANK-106-033 | How can marketing budgets compare short-term response with longer-term brand effects? | Inspect original measurement methods, horizon, uncertainty and confounding; avoid claiming one universal budget split. |
+
+## 07. Advertising rules and claim support
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-106-034 | What evidence should exist before an advertisement makes a comparative performance claim? | Use current regulator guidance, actual test conditions and the exact wording; distinguish a documented result from a broader superiority claim. |
+| BANK-106-035 | How should health or financial advertising be handled differently from an ordinary product demonstration? | Identify jurisdiction, current official rules and substantiation requirements; exclude treatment, return or personal-benefit promises without appropriate evidence and authorization. |
+| BANK-106-036 | What must a testimonial disclose when the featured outcome is unusual or incentivized? | Read current official endorsement guidance and verify the underlying account; avoid implying a selected story is typical. |
+| BANK-106-037 | How can environmental marketing avoid a vague claim that hides its measurement boundary? | Specify material, lifecycle stage, baseline and verification; distinguish a narrow improvement from a complete sustainability verdict. |
+
+## 08. Ethics, privacy and case-study literacy
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Define the rival claims and their evidence standards. Compare sources on equal terms, separate empirical findings from values or interpretations and do not imply that all positions have equal evidential support.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the original sources behind the compared claims; explicit definitions and measurement methods; independent critiques with traceable evidence. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-106-038 | How can first-party marketing data still create privacy responsibilities? | Map purpose, consent where required, minimization, retention and current official rules; use fictional records rather than profiling real people. |
+| BANK-106-039 | What should be checked before presenting a viral campaign as a business success? | Compare actual costs, distribution, sales evidence, backlash and disclosed objectives; distinguish publicity from verified profit. |
+| BANK-106-040 | How can a campaign case study separate the agency's contribution from product, price and seasonality? | Use dated primary records and explicit alternatives; avoid assigning every outcome to the most visually memorable advertisement. |
+| BANK-106-041 | What does responsible correction look like when an advertisement's central claim changes? | Update prominent messaging, inform affected audiences as required and preserve the dated record; do not rely on a hidden footnote to reverse a misleading impression. |
+
+## Coverage limits and next step
+
+This is an expanded topic-bank draft, not exhaustive scholarship or a researched episode package. Each question has an individually written boundary. Source access, claim verification, current angle-scope review and publication approval remain pending. Add new branches and cases when they fill a real gap; no numeric topic cap applies.
+
+Select an exact case, edition, population, jurisdiction or product version, inspect sources and refine the question before using the existing registry idea/depth planning workflow. These BANK IDs are catalog identities, not approved IDEA IDs. Existing Q1-Q42 defaults, production queues and approval gates are unchanged.
