+# 107 Retail & E-commerce: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 30 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Retail & E-commerce. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. A retail transaction as a system
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-107-010 | What happens between a customer seeing an item and receiving a completed order? | Map catalog, price, stock, payment, fulfillment and support in an original store example; distinguish displayed availability from a guaranteed shipment. |
+| BANK-107-011 | How do a marketplace and a direct retailer allocate different responsibilities? | Inspect current platform terms, seller identity, payment and returns; avoid assuming the platform itself sells every listed item. |
+| BANK-107-012 | Why can online and store inventory disagree even for the same retailer? | Trace updates, reservations, returns and location boundaries using documented systems; do not infer deception from one discrepancy. |
+
+## 02. Catalogs, merchandising and discovery
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Inspect the actual product version, official documentation, terms and privacy policies. Test claims under stated conditions and distinguish account permissions, data retention, measured functionality and marketing.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official versioned documentation and release notes; dated privacy policies and service terms; reproducible product tests and original validation reports. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-107-013 | How can a product page help a customer assess suitability without relying on promotional adjectives? | Compare specifications, dimensions, compatibility, images and limitations using original content; verify claims and avoid hiding essential conditions. |
+| BANK-107-014 | What makes a category filter useful rather than a long list of unchecked attributes? | Define customer tasks, consistent data and accessibility through consented tests; distinguish catalog organization from a universal preference hierarchy. |
+| BANK-107-015 | Why can search results fail when shoppers use different words from the catalog? | Compare synonyms, spelling, language and attribute data with current system documentation; avoid inventing proprietary ranking behavior. |
+| BANK-107-016 | How should a retailer distinguish a sponsored product position from ordinary results? | Review current official disclosure guidance, platform rules and actual labeling; avoid designing covert advertising. |
+
+## 03. Prices, promotions and payments
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use current official terms, filings or macroeconomic data; state jurisdiction, date, fees, taxes, risk, units and assumptions. Use transparent illustrative calculations rather than personal recommendations or return promises.
+
+**Source types to locate:** regulator or official statistical publications; actual contracts, fee schedules and audited filings; original economic studies and disclosed calculation inputs. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-107-017 | What should a retail price comparison include beyond the item price? | Map delivery, tax, service fees, required bundles and return costs using illustrative figures; verify current local rules before describing real offers. |
+| BANK-107-018 | How can a discount claim be checked against its reference price and period? | Use actual dated price records and official consumer guidance; distinguish a claimed saving from verified historical pricing. |
+| BANK-107-019 | What changes when a buyer uses installment credit at checkout? | Read current official terms, eligibility, fees, repayment and risk disclosures; exclude personal borrowing recommendations or assurances of affordability. |
+| BANK-107-020 | Why can a payment authorization appear before a merchant has captured the final charge? | Use official processor documentation and a fictional transaction timeline; distinguish holds, settlement, refunds and individual account advice. |
+
+## 04. Stock, fulfillment and returns
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-107-021 | How can order picking errors arise even when the stock count is correct? | Trace item identity, location, variants, scanning and packing in a documented workflow; avoid exposing private customer orders. |
+| BANK-107-022 | What does a delivery promise depend on after the parcel leaves the warehouse? | Map carrier acceptance, cutoff, route, customs where relevant and exception handling; distinguish a forecast from guaranteed arrival. |
+| BANK-107-023 | Why can returns create more work than reversing the original sale? | Compare inspection, condition, refund, restocking and disposal evidence; avoid assuming every returned item is resold or destroyed. |
+| BANK-107-024 | How can a retailer communicate an out-of-stock substitution without removing the customer's choice? | Use an original workflow with price, compatibility, consent and cancellation options; exclude automatic undisclosed changes. |
+
+## 05. Customer service, access and trust
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use consented participant accounts, local records and the community's own descriptions. Include differences of position, access and power; testimony is not a representative survey or clinical proof.
+
+**Source types to locate:** consented interviews and oral histories; community publications and local records; field notes with method and consent documented. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-107-025 | What makes a customer-service handoff preserve the problem rather than restart it? | Compare issue records, privacy, authority and follow-through in fictional cases; avoid exposing real tickets or promising every complaint can be resolved. |
+| BANK-107-026 | How can a retailer test accessibility across the entire purchase journey? | Include product discovery, checkout, payment, support and returns with consented users; distinguish a homepage check from end-to-end access. |
+| BANK-107-027 | What can product reviews establish when authenticity and selection remain uncertain? | Inspect platform policy, verified-purchase meaning, incentives and moderation; avoid inferring a seller's wrongdoing from a few extreme ratings. |
+| BANK-107-028 | Why can trust badges create an impression broader than their actual scope? | Verify the issuing body, current status and exact assurance; distinguish a badge from a complete guarantee of security or service. |
+
+## 06. Store operations and human work
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use consented participant accounts, local records and the community's own descriptions. Include differences of position, access and power; testimony is not a representative survey or clinical proof.
+
+**Source types to locate:** consented interviews and oral histories; community publications and local records; field notes with method and consent documented. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-107-029 | How can a physical store's layout balance discovery, access and staff work? | Map routes, replenishment, checkout and user needs through an authorized example; avoid treating sales per square meter as the only objective. |
+| BANK-107-030 | What does self-checkout change for employees and customers beyond fewer staffed tills? | Compare assistance, error handling, accessibility and actual labor arrangements; avoid assuming identical consequences across store formats. |
+| BANK-107-031 | How can retail staffing be studied without reducing workers to a cost line? | Use consented accounts, demand patterns, training and service tasks; distinguish efficiency claims from measured workload and welfare. |
+
+## 07. Unit economics and business claims
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use current official terms, filings or macroeconomic data; state jurisdiction, date, fees, taxes, risk, units and assumptions. Use transparent illustrative calculations rather than personal recommendations or return promises.
+
+**Source types to locate:** regulator or official statistical publications; actual contracts, fee schedules and audited filings; original economic studies and disclosed calculation inputs. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-107-032 | Why can a growing online store lose money on each additional order? | Use transparent illustrative margins, fulfillment, acquisition, fees and returns; distinguish revenue growth from contribution and cash flow. |
+| BANK-107-033 | What should an e-commerce success story disclose about advertising and returned orders? | Inspect actual reporting boundaries, period, discounts and cancellations; avoid presenting gross sales screenshots as verified income. |
+| BANK-107-034 | How can omnichannel services shift costs between online and store operations? | Trace pickup, returns, inventory and staff responsibilities in a documented case; avoid double-counting revenue or assuming channel integration is free. |
+| BANK-107-035 | What changes when a retailer sells through several marketplaces at once? | Compare fees, stock synchronization, customer data, terms and operational burden; verify current contracts rather than promising easy expansion. |
+
+## 08. Regulation, privacy and responsible commerce
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-107-036 | What should a seller verify before listing regulated or age-restricted goods? | Consult current official product, licensing, labeling and platform requirements; exclude evasion or procurement of prohibited products. |
+| BANK-107-037 | How can a store manage customer data without treating purchase history as unlimited marketing permission? | Use current privacy rules, purpose, access and retention limits; demonstrate only with fictional records. |
+| BANK-107-038 | What should an online retailer explain about cancellation and statutory remedies? | Use current official local rules and actual terms; avoid deciding an individual's entitlement or implying store policy eliminates legal rights. |
+| BANK-107-039 | How can a creator review a retail platform without hiding affiliate incentives? | Disclose commercial relationships, tested scope and dates; distinguish a promotional demonstration from independent evidence of typical seller results. |
+
+## Coverage limits and next step
