+# 108 Logistics & Supply Chain: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 30 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Logistics & Supply Chain. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Mapping a supply chain
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-108-010 | How can a product's supply chain be drawn without confusing physical movement and ownership? | Map suppliers, carriers, buyers, records and payment boundaries in an original example; distinguish material flow from contractual responsibility. |
+| BANK-108-011 | What changes between procurement, logistics and supply-chain planning? | Compare tasks, decisions, time horizons and handoffs in a documented product chain; avoid treating the terms as interchangeable departments. |
+| BANK-108-012 | Why can a small component become a constraint on a much larger system? | Trace dependencies, lead time, qualification and substitution evidence in a bounded case; distinguish a possibility from a verified disruption cause. |
+
+## 02. Demand, inventory and uncertainty
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-108-013 | What does safety stock protect against and what can it not resolve? | Use a transparent illustrative demand-and-lead-time model; state assumptions and exclude universal inventory quantities or financial guarantees. |
+| BANK-108-014 | How can a forecast be useful while being wrong in many individual periods? | Compare purpose, horizon, aggregation, error and decision consequences; distinguish a planning input from a promise of actual demand. |
+| BANK-108-015 | Why can order variability grow as it moves upstream from customer purchases? | Review original bullwhip-effect studies and a labeled simulation; distinguish possible mechanisms from a diagnosis of a real company's records. |
+| BANK-108-016 | What changes when inventory is pooled in one warehouse rather than spread across regions? | Compare response time, transport, risk, service and demand correlation using explicit assumptions; avoid a universal centralization verdict. |
+
+## 03. Warehouses, identity and records
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-108-017 | How does a warehouse know which physical item corresponds to an order record? | Trace identifiers, variants, lot tracking, location and verification with public standards or authorized documentation; exclude real customer information. |
+| BANK-108-018 | What makes cross-docking different from ordinary storage? | Compare arrival coordination, sorting, outbound timing and failure conditions in a bounded case; avoid assuming it eliminates inventory everywhere. |
+| BANK-108-019 | How can warehouse automation create new dependencies rather than remove human coordination? | Inspect exception handling, maintenance, software and replenishment; distinguish advertised throughput from a measured operating system. |
+| BANK-108-020 | Why can inventory accuracy remain poor despite frequent counting? | Compare transaction timing, units, returns, damage and item identity; avoid attributing discrepancies to theft without evidence. |
+
+## 04. Transport modes and container networks
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-108-021 | How does containerization simplify transfer while creating requirements at every terminal? | Use official port, carrier and standards documentation on dimensions, handling and records; exclude loading or dangerous-goods procedures. |
+| BANK-108-022 | What tradeoffs distinguish air, sea, rail and road freight for one product? | Compare time, cost, reliability, handling and emissions within stated boundaries; verify dated services and avoid one mode being declared universally best. |
+| BANK-108-023 | How can a port disruption spread to a factory far from the coast? | Trace scheduled connections, stock, lead time and documented alternatives in a public case; distinguish observed effects from hypothetical consequences. |
+| BANK-108-024 | What do official records of the Ever Given blockage reveal about network vulnerability? | Use dated canal-authority, court and carrier records; separate established delay effects from speculative global loss estimates. |
+
+## 05. Cold chains and specialized flows
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-108-025 | What does a cold-chain record need besides a single arrival temperature? | Inspect product-specific official requirements, time history, handling and monitoring limits; avoid declaring safety from one sensor reading. |
+| BANK-108-026 | How can transporting medical products differ from moving ordinary consumer parcels? | Review current official quality, traceability and handling requirements at a conceptual level; exclude personalized storage or treatment advice. |
+| BANK-108-027 | Why do hazardous goods require specialized classification before shipment? | Use current official transport rules, responsibilities and documentation; exclude packaging recipes or routes for bypassing restrictions. |
+| BANK-108-028 | How can reverse logistics differ for repair, reuse and waste recovery? | Compare ownership, inspection, routing and authorized destinations; distinguish collected material from verified recycling or successful reuse. |
+
+## 06. Suppliers, contracts and resilience
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use current official terms, filings or macroeconomic data; state jurisdiction, date, fees, taxes, risk, units and assumptions. Use transparent illustrative calculations rather than personal recommendations or return promises.
+
+**Source types to locate:** regulator or official statistical publications; actual contracts, fee schedules and audited filings; original economic studies and disclosed calculation inputs. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-108-029 | What changes when a company adds a second supplier for a critical part? | Compare qualification, capacity, cost, geographic dependencies and actual readiness; avoid equating a signed contract with available backup production. |
+| BANK-108-030 | How do Incoterms define certain responsibilities without replacing the entire sales contract? | Use the actual current official rule descriptions and an illustrative transaction; distinguish delivery, risk, payment and ownership rather than providing legal advice. |
+| BANK-108-031 | Why can nearshoring leave important upstream dependencies unchanged? | Map actual materials, components, skills and tooling in a documented relocation; distinguish assembly location from the full supply base. |
+| BANK-108-032 | What should a resilience investment be evaluated against besides normal-period cost? | Compare disruption scenarios, recovery time, service and opportunity cost with explicit assumptions; avoid inventing probabilities or guaranteed payback. |
+
+## 07. Labor, environment and public claims
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-108-033 | How can a supply-chain labor audit be evaluated for scope and blind spots? | Inspect tiers, worker access, subcontracting, sampling and remediation evidence; distinguish a completed audit from proof that all harms are absent. |
+| BANK-108-034 | What must an emissions comparison define before calling a delivery greener? | Specify route, load, mode, return trips and lifecycle boundary using primary methods; avoid relying on one per-parcel average. |
+| BANK-108-035 | How can a traceability system record an uncertain link honestly? | Compare evidence, declarations, verification and missing tiers; do not turn a digital ledger entry into proof of physical origin. |
+| BANK-108-036 | What distinguishes a sanctions or customs-compliance requirement from a carrier's own policy? | Use current official rules, dates and actual contract terms for named jurisdictions; exclude evasion guidance or transaction-specific legal conclusions. |
