+# 109 Railways & Trains: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 31 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Railways & Trains. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Railway systems and passenger questions
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-109-010 | How can a railway be understood as more than trains running on tracks? | Map vehicles, infrastructure, control, stations, staff and maintenance using official diagrams; distinguish operating roles from a universal national model. |
+| BANK-109-011 | What separates a train's published maximum speed from a passenger's journey time? | Compare stops, acceleration, route, timetable and constraints; verify service details by date rather than treating speed labels as travel-time promises. |
+| BANK-109-012 | Why can different rail gauges complicate movement between networks? | Use official infrastructure records to explain compatibility, transfer and documented solutions; exclude access to working track or mechanical modification instructions. |
+| BANK-109-013 | How do metro, suburban and intercity services serve different trip patterns? | Compare stop spacing, capacity, frequency and fare integration in named systems; avoid treating the categories as mutually exclusive everywhere. |
+
+## 02. Track, vehicles and traction
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-109-014 | What does a railway wheelset contribute to guidance and stability? | Use authoritative engineering diagrams and documented design limits; distinguish a conceptual model from vehicle maintenance or adjustment guidance. |
+| BANK-109-015 | How do electric and diesel-electric trains obtain and transmit traction power differently? | Trace energy paths, components and losses through manufacturer or operator documentation; exclude electrical access or repair procedures. |
+| BANK-109-016 | Why can track geometry require continuous monitoring despite appearing straight to a passenger? | Explain measurement, wear, settlement and maintenance through official standards; avoid diagnosing line safety from a video. |
+| BANK-109-017 | What changes when a route uses continuous welded rail rather than many short joints? | Compare documented construction, thermal management and maintenance concepts; exclude track work or claims that a jointless appearance eliminates all risk. |
+
+## 03. Signaling, control and capacity
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-109-018 | How does railway signaling coordinate separation without making every train movement identical? | Use public official explanations of one system type; keep the overview nonoperational and exclude bypasses or signal-manipulation instructions. |
+| BANK-109-019 | Why can a faster train reduce timetable flexibility on a mixed-traffic route? | Compare stopping patterns, overtaking, headways and junction constraints in a labeled simulation; distinguish illustrative capacity from an actual operating plan. |
+| BANK-109-020 | What makes a junction a potential bottleneck even when nearby track has spare space? | Trace conflicting routes and movement timing through public diagrams; avoid operational details that could enable interference with live services. |
+| BANK-109-021 | How do dispatch decisions balance recovering a delay against spreading it? | Use a documented public disruption account and stated priorities; distinguish retrospective possibilities from knowledge available to controllers. |
+
+## 04. Stations, boarding and accessibility
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use consented participant accounts, local records and the community's own descriptions. Include differences of position, access and power; testimony is not a representative survey or clinical proof.
+
+**Source types to locate:** consented interviews and oral histories; community publications and local records; field notes with method and consent documented. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-109-022 | What does an accessible rail journey require beyond a lift at the origin station? | Check current official information on paths, platforms, boarding, assistance and destination access; start from the traveler's stated needs rather than a generic symbol. |
+| BANK-109-023 | Why can station design affect how long trains spend at platforms? | Compare passenger flows, doors, level changes and crowd management through official studies; avoid filming sensitive security areas or staging congestion. |
+| BANK-109-024 | How can ticketing integration change a journey involving several operators? | Inspect dated official fares, transfer rules, payment and exclusions; distinguish a shared ticket medium from a through-fare guarantee. |
+| BANK-109-025 | What can passenger-information systems do when the exact recovery time is unknown? | Compare verified status, uncertainty, alternatives and update frequency using public operator communications; avoid inventing live service advice. |
+
+## 05. Freight, geography and railway history
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Compare dated primary records, material evidence and historical scholarship. Distinguish event dates, the dates of surviving witnesses, later retellings and disagreement. Do not invent quotations, intentions or an unbroken lineage.
+
+**Source types to locate:** dated archival records and original publications; inscriptions, artifacts or photographs with provenance; critical editions and historical scholarship. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-109-026 | How did a named railway corridor change access to markets in its region? | Use dated traffic, maps, economic records and local accounts; distinguish association from proof that rail alone caused growth. |
+| BANK-109-027 | What makes rail freight competitive for some loads and difficult for others? | Compare terminals, distance, shipment size, handling and service reliability with official data; avoid universal cost or emissions claims. |
+| BANK-109-028 | How can a colonial railway history include labor and land acquisition alongside engineering? | Use primary records, attributed scholarship and affected community accounts; distinguish documented impacts from a single progress-or-exploitation narrative. |
+| BANK-109-029 | Why can a preserved locomotive tell a different story from the network it once served? | Compare object history, routes, labor and infrastructure; avoid treating a restored machine as representative of every operating era. |
+
+## 06. Maintenance, safety and investigations
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-109-030 | What can an official rail-accident report establish that early footage cannot? | Trace evidence, findings, contributing factors and recommendations; preserve preliminary status and avoid identifying blame before the official record supports it. |
+| BANK-109-031 | Why does preventive maintenance require records rather than only visible cleanliness? | Use operator and regulator guidance on inspection, condition and work history; exclude maintenance instructions for safety-critical systems. |
+| BANK-109-032 | How can near-miss reporting support learning without becoming a complete measure of risk? | Inspect definitions, reporting culture, exposure and data limits; distinguish higher reporting from automatic deterioration in safety. |
+| BANK-109-033 | What should rail enthusiasts verify before filming at a station or heritage site? | Use current operator, landowner and local rules; exclude trespass, track access and filming that obstructs passengers or workers. |
+
+## 07. Projects, economics and environmental comparison
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use current official terms, filings or macroeconomic data; state jurisdiction, date, fees, taxes, risk, units and assumptions. Use transparent illustrative calculations rather than personal recommendations or return promises.
+
+**Source types to locate:** regulator or official statistical publications; actual contracts, fee schedules and audited filings; original economic studies and disclosed calculation inputs. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-109-034 | How can a new rail project's forecast be compared with its later use? | Align demand, cost, opening scope, fares and measurement period using official appraisals; distinguish a missed forecast from a complete public-value verdict. |
+| BANK-109-035 | What does electrification change beyond replacing a train's onboard engine? | Compare infrastructure, power supply, maintenance, operating flexibility and documented costs; avoid assuming every route has the same economics. |
+| BANK-109-036 | How should high-speed rail be compared with air travel for a specific corridor? | Define door-to-door time, occupancy, fares, infrastructure and emissions boundaries; verify current services rather than ranking modes universally. |
+| BANK-109-037 | Why can rail fares cover only part of a system's public financing? | Read official accounts on operating costs, capital, subsidy and policy goals; avoid treating farebox recovery as the sole performance measure. |
+
+## 08. Railway media and data literacy
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Analyze an identifiable work or create a clearly labeled original example. Connect creative decisions to observable audience information; respect rights, attribution, consent and the difference between intent and reception.
+
+**Source types to locate:** the actual work and authorized production materials; creator interviews or published craft explanations; permission-cleared examples and audience feedback. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-109-038 | How can a cab-view video explain a route without implying unrestricted access to operational knowledge? | Use authorized footage, public context and clear dates; exclude confidential controls, access details or instructions for interfering with operations. |
+| BANK-109-039 | What should a railway comparison disclose about punctuality definitions? | Check thresholds, cancellations, station versus endpoint measures and reporting periods; align official metrics before making rankings. |
+| BANK-109-040 | How can a train-history animation distinguish original infrastructure from later rebuilding? | Use dated maps, official records and labeled reconstructions; avoid presenting a composite route as one unchanged historical network. |
+
+## Coverage limits and next step
+
+This is an expanded topic-bank draft, not exhaustive scholarship or a researched episode package. Each question has an individually written boundary. Source access, claim verification, current angle-scope review and publication approval remain pending. Add new branches and cases when they fill a real gap; no numeric topic cap applies.
+
+Select an exact case, edition, population, jurisdiction or product version, inspect sources and refine the question before using the existing registry idea/depth planning workflow. These BANK IDs are catalog identities, not approved IDEA IDs. Existing Q1-Q42 defaults, production queues and approval gates are unchanged.
