+# 110 Transportation: Detailed Topic-Bank Draft
+
+**8 categories · 30 individually authored episode proposals.**
+
+Individually authored episode questions and coverage boundaries for Transportation. The subject progression is organized by concrete content rather than a topic-by-angle multiplication.
+
+[All-niche progress](../DEPTH_COMPLETION_STATUS.md) · [All-niche index](../ALL_NICHE_TOPIC_BANKS.md) · [Editable bank](./IN_DEPTH_TOPIC_BANK.json)
+
+Every question below has its own coverage boundary. All remain proposed; sources and current niche angle scopes have not been verified. Learning levels organize documentary research rather than physical difficulty. The older initial proposals remain in the JSON's prior_initial_map archive and are not counted again.
+
+## 01. Transport as access to activities
+
+**Level:** foundation. **Evidence direction:** Use consented participant accounts, local records and the community's own descriptions. Include differences of position, access and power; testimony is not a representative survey or clinical proof.
+
+**Source types to locate:** consented interviews and oral histories; community publications and local records; field notes with method and consent documented. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-110-010 | How can a transport system be evaluated by access rather than vehicle movement alone? | Compare journeys to work, care, education and services using official data and consented accounts; distinguish speed from usable opportunity. |
+| BANK-110-011 | What changes between measuring a trip and measuring a whole day's travel needs? | Map connected activities, transfers, waiting and care responsibilities; avoid treating one commute as every person's mobility pattern. |
+| BANK-110-012 | Why can a short trip still be inaccessible? | Examine crossings, cost, surfaces, assistance, safety and information using user-defined needs; distinguish physical distance from practical access. |
+
+## 02. Walking, cycling and streets
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-110-013 | How does crossing design affect walking time and access? | Use official design guidance, observed conditions and consented user perspectives; avoid engineering conclusions from photographs alone. |
+| BANK-110-014 | What makes a cycling network more than disconnected protected segments? | Map continuity, junctions, destinations and maintenance through public plans; distinguish installed length from a demonstrated useful network. |
+| BANK-110-015 | How can street space be compared across movement, deliveries and public use? | Use measured dimensions, official policy and varied local accounts; avoid assuming one allocation is best for every place. |
+| BANK-110-016 | Why can children and adults experience the same walking route differently? | Compare sightlines, crossings, time and accompaniment with appropriate consent; do not expose children's routines or treat one family as representative. |
+
+## 03. Buses, public transport and transfers
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-110-017 | What does frequent bus service offer that a timetable with a few fast trips cannot? | Compare waiting, transfers, span and reliability with current official data; distinguish average speed from useful access throughout the day. |
+| BANK-110-018 | How can bus priority change passenger journeys without widening a road? | Inspect signal, lane and stop designs through official evaluations; separate demonstrated corridor outcomes from universal claims. |
+| BANK-110-019 | Why can a poorly designed transfer outweigh gains on either side of a journey? | Trace walking, wait, information, fare and accessibility in a dated example; avoid assuming a mapped connection is feasible for everyone. |
+| BANK-110-020 | How can transit routes balance coverage and frequency under a fixed budget? | Use an explicit illustrative network, demand and access goals; distinguish a planning tradeoff from a personalized service recommendation. |
+
+## 04. Roads, traffic and shared mobility
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-110-021 | What does induced-demand research measure after road capacity changes? | Inspect original study design, network scope, horizon and alternatives; avoid asserting that every road project has an identical effect. |
+| BANK-110-022 | How can congestion be distinguished from unreliability and lack of travel options? | Compare travel-time distributions, access and mode alternatives using official metrics; avoid treating a single rush-hour image as a complete diagnosis. |
+| BANK-110-023 | What changes when a ride service is shared, privately booked or integrated with public transport? | Compare occupancy, waiting, empty travel, fares and accessibility; verify current terms and avoid universal sustainability rankings. |
+| BANK-110-024 | Why can parking policy affect a street beyond the vehicles already parked there? | Use official data and original studies on circulation, land use, access and revenue; distinguish measured effects from political preference. |
+
+## 05. Freight, ports and last-mile systems
+
+**Level:** intermediate. **Evidence direction:** Use the actual system documentation, a worked implementation and measurements. Identify inputs, outputs, constraints, tolerances, failure conditions and the limits of the example. Do not provide hazardous construction or unauthorized access procedures.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original engineering papers; official versioned product or standards documentation; test reports and drawings with clear provenance. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-110-025 | How do passenger and goods movements compete for the same urban space? | Map deliveries, stopping, loading windows and user access through official plans; avoid treating all freight as a removable inconvenience. |
+| BANK-110-026 | What determines whether cargo can shift between road, rail and water? | Compare terminals, load form, schedule, handling and infrastructure with documented cases; distinguish theoretical connectivity from an available service. |
+| BANK-110-027 | How can last-mile delivery be evaluated beyond the speed of the final courier? | Trace sorting, failed delivery, access, labor and return trips; use primary data where available and do not invent efficiency rates. |
+
+## 06. Accessibility, affordability and unequal burdens
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use consented participant accounts, local records and the community's own descriptions. Include differences of position, access and power; testimony is not a representative survey or clinical proof.
+
+**Source types to locate:** consented interviews and oral histories; community publications and local records; field notes with method and consent documented. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-110-028 | What does transport affordability mean when household time and income differ? | Compare fares, mandatory transfers, total trips and time costs with official methods; avoid one fixed threshold being presented as universally sufficient. |
+| BANK-110-029 | How can a mobility study include people who stay home because travel is difficult? | Use ethical surveys, access needs and missing-trip questions; distinguish observed ridership from all potential demand. |
+| BANK-110-030 | Why can gendered travel-pattern research require care about household roles and context? | Inspect sampling, trip chains, safety perceptions and within-group variation; avoid predicting an individual's needs from gender. |
+| BANK-110-031 | How can rural transport be studied without using urban frequency as the only benchmark? | Compare dispersed destinations, service days, assistance and local accounts; distinguish low density from absence of public responsibility. |
+
+## 07. Energy, environment and safety evidence
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use original observations or experiments, an appropriate scientific reference and the actual measurement method. Distinguish mechanisms, correlations, model assumptions, uncertainty and limits of generalization.
+
+**Source types to locate:** original research reports and data; research-institution observation records; methods papers and current scientific reference works. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-110-032 | What must be defined before comparing the emissions of two transport modes? | Specify occupancy, energy source, route, infrastructure and lifecycle boundary; distinguish global averages from local operating conditions. |
+| BANK-110-033 | Why can electric vehicles change tailpipe emissions without resolving every transport impact? | Compare energy, materials, tires, land use and access using primary evidence; avoid either zero-impact marketing or dismissing documented benefits. |
+| BANK-110-034 | How can road-safety performance be compared without confusing exposure with raw crash counts? | Use official definitions, travel measures, severity and reporting completeness; avoid declaring a road safe from a low count alone. |
+| BANK-110-035 | What does a safe-system approach change about assigning responsibility after a crash? | Read official framework documents and evaluated interventions; distinguish systemic prevention from removing individual legal accountability. |
+
+## 08. Planning, technology and public choices
+
+**Level:** advanced. **Evidence direction:** Use the current official legal text, policy, procedure or public dataset for a specific jurisdiction and date. Distinguish written rules, implementation and lived access; give no personal legal or financial conclusion.
+
+**Source types to locate:** official laws, rules and administrative guidance; audits, judgments and public service records; consented accounts from affected people and qualified specialists. These are requirements, not sources already inspected.
+
+| ID | Specific episode question | Individually written scope and exclusions |
+|---|---|---|
+| BANK-110-036 | How can a transport appraisal include benefits and costs borne by different groups? | Use official methods, explicit distribution and uncertainty; avoid presenting one monetary total as the complete public decision. |
+| BANK-110-037 | What should be checked before accepting an autonomous-mobility demonstration as evidence of broad capability? | Inspect operating domain, intervention, authorization and actual trial data; distinguish a staged route from general operation. |
+| BANK-110-038 | How can a city compare a pilot mobility service with a long-term commitment? | Track subsidy, access, reliability, data rights and evaluation design; distinguish uptake during free trials from durable demand. |
+| BANK-110-039 | How should a transport explainer report a project that exists only as an announcement? | Separate proposal, approval, funding, construction and operation with dated official records; avoid drawing a proposed line as an available route. |
+
+## Coverage limits and next step
+
+This is an expanded topic-bank draft, not exhaustive scholarship or a researched episode package. Each question has an individually written boundary. Source access, claim verification, current angle-scope review and publication approval remain pending. Add new branches and cases when they fill a real gap; no numeric topic cap applies.
+
+Select an exact case, edition, population, jurisdiction or product version, inspect sources and refine the question before using the existing registry idea/depth planning workflow. These BANK IDs are catalog identities, not approved IDEA IDs. Existing Q1-Q42 defaults, production queues and approval gates are unchanged.
